window.__DIYDB__=window.__DIYDB__||{data:{}};window.__DIYDB__.data["tj"]=[{"id":"tj-front-bumper","title":"Steel Front Bumper for the TJ \u2014 Budget Through Premium","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":1099},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","wire strippers (if relocating sensors)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wiring basics (fog light relocation)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","bumper","front-bumper","winch-mount","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Front Bumper with Winch Mount TJ 76721","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartan Front Bumper with Tow Hooks TJ 11563.03","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Full-Width Front Bumper with Stinger","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":699},{"name":"LOD Destroyer Series Full-Width Front Bumper TJ","vendor":"LOD Offroad","price_estimate_usd":899}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Front Bumper Buyer's Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/front-bumper-buyers-guide-tj/"},{"title":"Smittybilt XRC TJ Bumper Install Guide","url":"https://www.smittybilt.com/instructions"},{"title":"LOD Offroad Destroyer Series Overview","url":"https://www.lodoffroad.com/tj-destroyer"}],"alternatives":["tj-winch","tj-rear-bumper","tj-skid-plates"],"summary":"A steel bumper is the most visible protection upgrade on the TJ \u2014 and a winch-capable version doubles as the anchor point for the rest of your recovery setup.","description":"The TJ's stock plastic bumpers serve their purpose at highway speeds but offer zero protection from trail obstacles, have no tow hook capacity worth trusting, and can't mount a winch. A steel bumper replacement is the first armor upgrade most builds address because it simultaneously solves approach angle, recovery rigging, and frontal protection in one bolt-on swap.\n\nThe design choice is tube bumper vs. full-width plate. Tube bumpers (Smittybilt XRC, Rugged Ridge Spartan) use a receiver plate for the winch and outboard loops or ends made from steel tube \u2014 lighter, cheaper, and friendlier to approach angle because they don't extend as far forward as a solid plate. Full-width plate bumpers (Iron Rock Offroad, LOD) are heavier but provide broader collision protection and often have a lower integrated bash bar that protects the grille and radiator from frontal rock contact. On rock crawling rigs and overland builds that push into brush, full-width plates are worth the extra weight. On mild-trail and day-use builds, a quality tube bumper is entirely sufficient.\n\nWinch mount integration is the critical dimension: not all bumpers that claim winch compatibility are actually built to handle the load. A quality winch mount needs to tie into the frame rails \u2014 not just the bumper's mounting plate \u2014 so that winch force pulls against the frame, not against sheet metal. Smittybilt and Rugged Ridge both pass this test on their TJ bumpers. LOD and Iron Rock Offroad exceed it, using full-width mounting plates that tie into both frame rails simultaneously. Avoid any bumper where the winch mounts to the bumper face plate alone without frame tie-in.\n\nThe TJ's OEM tow hooks are retained on most designs through through-hooks or integrated hook mounts. Verify tow hook retention before purchasing \u2014 some bumpers remove the hooks entirely. If the TJ has a factory fog light, most steel bumpers require relocating it to auxiliary pods mounted on the bumper ends or hood.","steps":["Disconnect the battery negative terminal.","Remove plastic factory bumper by unbolting 4\u20136 bolts from the frame mounting brackets (most are 15mm or 18mm).","Disconnect factory fog light wiring harness and set aside.","Remove factory tow hook if not retained in new bumper design.","Transfer any factory bump stops or isolators to new bumper if specified by kit instructions.","Position steel bumper against frame rails. Start all mounting bolts hand-tight before torquing any.","Torque frame mounting bolts to kit specification \u2014 typically 55\u201375 ft-lbs on grade-8 hardware.","Reconnect fog light wiring to new pods or aftermarket light bar harness.","Reconnect battery and verify all electrical connections.","Verify no contact between bumper and radiator or engine accessories at full droop/compression."],"pros":["Enables winch mounting \u2014 transforms the TJ into a self-recovery capable rig","Steel construction survives frontal rock contact that destroys plastic bumpers","Improves approach angle on full-width designs by up to 5\u00b0 over stock plastic","Integration of tow hooks, D-ring mounts, and shackle tabs consolidates recovery rigging points","Bolt-on installation is reversible; no cutting or welding required on most designs"],"cons":["Adds 35\u201375 lbs of weight over the front axle \u2014 noticeable on front end feel over rough terrain","Fog light relocation required on most designs \u2014 plan an extra hour if retaining factory lights","Winch wiring still requires separate installation; bumper does not include wiring harness","Full-width designs can slightly reduce approach angle on some builds due to forward extension of lower bash bar","Less expensive bumpers (under $400) typically use thinner steel and may not survive serious frontal impacts"]},{"id":"tj-rear-bumper","title":"Rear Bumper with Hitch and Spare Tire Carrier for the TJ","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":349,"high":1299},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","floor jack","drill","level (for carrier alignment)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","minor drilling into frame"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","bumper","rear-bumper","tire-carrier","hitch"],"parts":[{"name":"Smittybilt XRC Rear Bumper with Hitch and D-Ring Mounts TJ 76733","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Spartacus Rear Bumper with Receiver Hitch 11542.10","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Rear Bumper with Tire Carrier (bolt-on)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"GenRight TJ Rear Swing-Out Spare Tire Carrier Bumper","vendor":"GenRight Offroad","price_estimate_usd":1099}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Rear Bumper and Tire Carrier Options","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/rear-bumper-tire-carrier-tj/"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Rear Bumper Installation Guide","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/tj-rear-bumper-instructions"},{"title":"GenRight TJ Swing-Out Carrier Overview","url":"https://www.genright.com/tj-swing-out"}],"alternatives":["tj-front-bumper","tj-winch","tj-recovery-gear-kit"],"summary":"The TJ's factory tailgate-mounted spare tire is already straining the tailgate hinges \u2014 a dedicated rear bumper with a proper tire carrier solves this while adding a hitch receiver and D-ring mounts for recovery.","description":"The TJ carries its spare tire on the tailgate, which is a reasonable design until you move to 33\" or 35\" tires. A 35\" LT tire weighs approximately 65\u201370 lbs, plus rim weight of 20\u201330 lbs depending on wheel \u2014 that's 85\u2013100 lbs hanging off the tailgate hinges. The factory tailgate hinges were engineered for a 28\u201330 lb OEM spare. Running an oversized spare on the tailgate long-term fatigues the hinges and warps the tailgate itself. A dedicated rear bumper with a bolt-on or swing-out tire carrier shifts that load to the frame rails where it belongs.\n\nThe basic choice is fixed carrier vs. swing-out. Fixed carriers (Smittybilt XRC, Iron Rock Offroad bolt-on) mount the spare over the rear bumper in a fixed position. They're lighter, simpler, and less expensive. The spare is accessible, but reaching the gate requires lifting and clearing the tire \u2014 manageable on most builds but awkward for daily-use rigs. Swing-out carriers (GenRight) hinge the entire tire mount on a pivoting arm so the spare swings clear of the tailgate entirely. Significantly more convenient, significantly more expensive, and requires precise installation to swing and latch correctly.\n\nHitch receiver integration matters for any TJ used as a tow vehicle or recovery anchor. The TJ's GVWR (3,500\u20134,000 lbs depending on year) limits towing to roughly 1,000 lbs trailer \u2014 a small trailer, a kayak, or a motorcycle carrier. A Class I or II hitch receiver welded or bolted into the rear bumper serves both towing and recovery purposes (Hi-Lift bumper jack points, tow strap receiver). Most steel rear bumpers in this category include a 2\" receiver as standard.","steps":["Disconnect battery if the license plate light is wired to the stock bumper.","Remove the OEM spare tire from the tailgate carrier and store the tire.","Unbolt and remove factory plastic rear bumper (typically 4 frame bolts and tailgate hinge screws for the spare carrier).","Remove factory spare tire mount from the tailgate \u2014 store hardware in case of future reversion.","Position new steel bumper against the frame rails; start all bolts hand-tight.","Torque all frame-rail mounting bolts per kit spec \u2014 typically 55\u201375 ft-lbs on grade-8 hardware.","For tire carrier: mount spare wheel to carrier arm. Verify carrier arm clears the tailgate when swung open. Adjust mount height if carrier includes vertical adjustment.","Reconnect license plate light wiring to the new bumper's integrated license plate mount.","Verify spare tire does not contact the tailgate or body when mounted. Verify D-ring or shackle mounts are accessible.","Torque wheel lug nuts on spare to 95 ft-lbs."],"pros":["Transfers spare tire load from the factory tailgate hinges to the frame rails \u2014 prevents hinge fatigue at 35\" tire weights","Integrated 2\" hitch receiver enables towing and recovery rigging from the rear","D-ring / shackle mounts provide dedicated rear recovery rigging points","Steel construction handles low-speed backing contact that destroys stock plastic","Swing-out designs (GenRight) allow full tailgate access without moving the spare"],"cons":["Adds 50\u201390 lbs of weight to the rear of the vehicle \u2014 shifts weight balance aft, noticeable in handling","Swing-out designs require precise installation for correct alignment; misalignment causes latch binding","Tire carrier access can be awkward in tight camping spots where the carrier can't swing fully open","Some designs require drilling new holes in the frame \u2014 verify before purchasing","Fixed-carrier designs require removing the spare to fully open the tailgate; plan for this limitation"]},{"id":"tj-skid-plates","title":"Skid Plate Kit \u2014 Engine, Transfer Case, and Fuel Tank","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":699},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill (for some kits)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","underbody access"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","skid-plates","underbody","transfer-case","fuel-tank"],"parts":[{"name":"Rubicon Express TJ Full Skid Plate Kit (engine, skid, t-case) RE4600","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Full Skid Plate System (3-piece, 1/4\" steel)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"Rugged Ridge TJ Aluminum Skid Plate Kit 18005.24","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Smittybilt TJ 6-Piece Skid Plate Kit 776100","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":349}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Skid Plate Options and Install","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/tj-skid-plate-install-guide/"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Skid Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/tj-skids-instructions"},{"title":"Rugged Ridge TJ Aluminum Skid Kit Overview","url":"https://www.ruggedridge.com/tj-skids"}],"alternatives":["tj-sliders","tj-front-bumper","tj-long-arm-lift"],"summary":"The TJ's fuel tank, transfer case, and oil pan are the three components that end a trail day instantly if they take a direct rock hit \u2014 skid plates are low-cost insurance against exactly that.","description":"The TJ runs its NP231 transfer case and Chrysler or Dana rear axle low in the chassis, making them genuinely vulnerable to rock strikes on any trail with ledges or water crossings. The factory skid plate is a thin stamped-steel unit that covers the transfer case only and provides marginal protection at best \u2014 a sharp rock hit can deform it into the transfer case housing. An aftermarket kit adds engine oil pan coverage, extends transfer case protection, and adds a proper fuel tank skid, addressing all three critical strike zones in one weekend install.\n\nSteel vs. aluminum is the core material choice. Steel skids (Iron Rock Offroad, Rubicon Express) in 3/16\"\u20131/4\" plate are the appropriate choice for rock crawling \u2014 they take repeated impacts without deforming significantly, and a steel skid will slide across a rock rather than bite in the way an aluminum skid can. Aluminum skids (Rugged Ridge) are 30\u201340% lighter, which matters for overland rigs focused on highway efficiency and payload. Aluminum deforms more readily than steel under point-load rock impacts; on moderate trails this is acceptable, but on technical crawling terrain the weight savings isn't worth the protection tradeoff.\n\nTransfer case fitment is a key purchase variable. Most TJs run the NP231 (Part-Time 4WD), but Sahara trims from 1997\u20132006 used the NP242 Select-Trac full-time transfer case. The NP231 and NP242 have different housings and different skid plate mounting geometries. Verify your transfer case before purchasing \u2014 the skid plates are not interchangeable. A sticker on the transfer case or the factory build sheet (door jamb sticker) confirms which unit you have. The Rubicon runs the Command-Trac NP241OR, which also requires its own specific skid geometry.","steps":["Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support on jack stands at all four corners.","Clean the mounting surfaces on the frame rails and existing factory skid hardware.","Remove factory transfer case skid plate (typically 4\u20136 bolts to the crossmember or frame).","Position new full skid kit starting from the front (engine/oil pan skid); use the mounting points specified by the kit. Start all bolts hand-tight.","Position transfer case skid section, verifying clearance to the NP231/NP242 housing. No skid should contact the transfer case under normal suspension travel.","Position fuel tank skid; verify clearance to tank and fuel lines.","Torque all mounting hardware to kit specification \u2014 typically 30\u201345 ft-lbs on 3/8\"\u20131/2\" hardware.","Lower the vehicle and verify no skid contact to drivetrain components with suspension at full droop."],"pros":["Protects the three most vulnerable underbody components: oil pan, transfer case, and fuel tank","Steel kits (Iron Rock Offroad) provide genuine rock crawling protection that the factory unit can't match","Bolt-on installation requires no cutting or welding on most kits","Aluminum kits save meaningful weight for overland builds focused on pavement mileage","Rubicon Express and Iron Rock Offroad kits are TJ-specific fitments with documented installation instructions"],"cons":["NP231 and NP242 transfer case skids are not interchangeable \u2014 verify your t-case before purchasing","Steel kits add 25\u201350 lbs of unsprung weight \u2014 affects suspension travel feel on long-travel builds","Aluminum skids deform under hard rock impact more readily than steel \u2014 not the right choice for aggressive rock crawling","Some kits do not include a fuel tank skid \u2014 verify coverage before purchasing a multi-piece set","Skid plates reduce clearance to the ground by 1/4\"\u20131/2\" depending on design \u2014 minimal but real"]},{"id":"tj-sliders","title":"Rock Sliders and Rocker Guards for the TJ","vehicle":"tj","category":"armor","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":699},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","drill","angle grinder","welder (weld-on kits)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","drilling into frame (bolt-on)","welding (weld-on kits)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["armor","sliders","rockers","rock-crawling","protection"],"parts":[{"name":"Rock Hard 4x4 TJ Wrangler Patriot Series Tube Rock Sliders RH-90203","vendor":"Rock Hard 4x4","price_estimate_usd":379},{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Wrangler Rocker Guards (weld-on)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":449},{"name":"GenRight TJ Rocker Protection Sliders (bolt-on)","vendor":"GenRight Offroad","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Smittybilt SRC Classic Rock Sliders (universal, TJ fitment)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 TJ Slider Comparison and Install Thread","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/tj-rock-sliders-comparison/"},{"title":"Rock Hard 4x4 TJ Slider Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.rockhard4x4.com/instructions"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Rocker Guard Overview","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/tj-rockers"}],"alternatives":["tj-skid-plates","tj-front-bumper"],"summary":"The TJ's rockers are the lowest structural point of the body \u2014 sliders protect them from side-rock contact that would otherwise cave in sheet metal and damage the unibody sill.","description":"The TJ is a monocoque (unibody) construction, not body-on-frame. That distinction matters enormously for slider selection: when a slider takes a hit, the load transfers directly into the frame rails, not a separate body mount. A slider that's poorly designed or bolted to marginal mounting points can actually transmit rock impact into the unibody sill and cause more damage than it prevents. This is why cheap universal sliders on TJs are a gamble \u2014 the mounting geometry has to match the TJ's specific frame rail geometry to transfer loads correctly.\n\nWeld-on sliders (Iron Rock Offroad) provide the most positive connection to the frame and allow a fabricator to optimize gusset placement and tube angles. They're the choice for dedicated rock crawlers where protecting the rocker panel at all costs matters more than reversibility. The weld-on joint can be ground smooth and painted to be nearly invisible. Bolt-on sliders (Rock Hard 4x4, GenRight) use the existing frame rail holes and tie into structural mounting points without welding. They're fully removable, which is relevant for anyone who street-drives the TJ regularly and wants the option to swap them out.\n\nThe TJ's narrow 64.5\" track width means sliders need to extend far enough outboard to cover the entire rocker panel \u2014 a shallow slider that doesn't reach the door sill edge will miss the contact point on a side-rock scrape. Rock Hard 4x4's tube design extends well past the sill and uses 1.75\" DOM tubing that can support the full weight of the vehicle for high-centering recovery. GenRight's approach is a plate-style guard rather than tube, which provides broader contact area across the sill at the expense of weight. Both are appropriate for serious trail use; tube sliders are lighter and shed mud better.","steps":["Clean the frame rail mounting surfaces with a wire brush and degreaser.","Position slider against the frame rails to check fitment before drilling or welding \u2014 mark mounting hole locations with a center punch.","For bolt-on kits: drill frame holes to kit specification (typically 1/2\" or 7/16\"). Install mounting hardware through frame rails. Start all fasteners hand-tight before torquing any.","For weld-on kits: tack weld the slider in place and verify fitment (parallel to the rocker panel, correct height) before running full welds. Grind and treat weld surfaces against rust.","Torque bolt-on fasteners to kit specification \u2014 typically 45\u201355 ft-lbs on 1/2\" grade-8 hardware.","Verify the slider can support vehicle weight: place a jack under the slider near the midpoint and raise the vehicle slightly to confirm it holds without deforming.","Confirm clearance to fuel lines, brake lines, and any OEM plastic cladding that needs trimming."],"pros":["Protects the TJ's unibody rockers from side-rock damage that would otherwise be expensive to repair","Tube-style sliders double as a high-centering recovery point and step for entry/exit","Bolt-on designs are fully reversible for street use or future sale","Rock Hard 4x4 and Iron Rock Offroad are TJ-specific fitments \u2014 no modification required for correct geometry"],"cons":["Weld-on installation requires welding skill or a fabrication shop \u2014 not a first-time project","Adds 35\u201355 lbs of unsprung weight depending on design","Some designs reduce ground clearance under the rocker by 1\"\u20132\" compared to stock sheet metal","Cheap universal sliders may not transfer load correctly to the TJ's unibody frame rails \u2014 avoid","Installation can interfere with factory step pads and door weather sealing on some builds"]},{"id":"dana30-tj","title":"TJ Dana 30 \u2014 Stock Rating and Upgrade Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","subcategory":"front_axle","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":350,"high":1800},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","torque wrench","ball joint press","axle puller"],"skills_required":["axle shaft service","u-joint replacement","diff fluid change"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["dana-30","front-axle","shaft-upgrade","ball-joints"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon chromoly axle shafts (27-spline pair)","part_number":"YA W24164","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":550},{"name":"Spicer 5-297X U-joints (pair)","part_number":"5-297X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Synergy heavy-duty ball joints","part_number":"8009","vendor":"Synergy MFG","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Yukon Grizzly Locker (27-spline)","part_number":"YGLD30-3-27","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":620}],"citations":[{"title":"The Jeep Dana 30 \u2014 The Ranger Station","url":"https://www.therangerstation.com/tech/the-jeep-dana-30/"},{"title":"Dana 30, Dana 35, and Dana 44 factory specifications","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/dana-30-dana-35-and-dana-44-factory-specifications.224/"}],"alternatives":["dana-44-front-swap-tj","outer-axle-shaft-replacement"],"summary":"The TJ Dana 30 will live behind 33s with stock shafts and behind 35s if you upgrade to chromoly shafts and stronger U-joints \u2014 but the moment you add a front locker, the weak link shifts to the axle shafts and ball joints.","description":"Every TJ except the Rubicon left the factory with a front Dana 30. It's a low-pinion, 7.125\" ring gear axle rated for roughly 3,500 lb gross axle weight. The ring and pinion is honestly underrated \u2014 most failures happen at the axle shafts and U-joints first, not the gears. That said, the Dana 30 has well-understood limits and an equally well-understood upgrade path.\n\nStock 27-spline shafts and the small 260X U-joints are reliable behind 31s and 33s with no locker. Once you bolt on 35s, the leverage at the tire patch starts to outpace what the small U-joints can handle, especially with throttle stabs in low range. Add a locker, and a stock shaft on the locked side becomes the sacrificial part \u2014 the locker eliminates the differential's natural torque relief, so any tire grip spike loads the shaft directly. Plan the upgrades in this order: U-joints, shafts, then locker.\n\nThe most common shaft upgrade is Yukon or RCV chromoly 27-spline axle shafts paired with Spicer 5-297X U-joints. The 5-297X is about 30% stronger than the stock 260X and uses larger trunnions. RCV CV-style shafts cost more (~$1,400/pair) but eliminate U-joint binding at full steering lock, which matters if you're rock crawling. Some builders go to 30-spline shafts, but the Dana 30 outer stub is still 27-spline, so the inner upgrade alone doesn't fully resolve the weak point and 30-spline shafts are harder to source in a hurry on the trail.\n\nBall joints are the other Dana 30 conversation. The factory ball joints have a known short service life \u2014 many TJs need replacement by 80\u2013100k miles, sooner if the rig has been lifted. Aggressive lift angles, larger tires, and rough trails accelerate wear. Synergy, Spicer Performance, and Dynatrac all make heavy-duty replacements that last meaningfully longer.\n\nFor builds aiming above 35s with a locker, the conversation shifts to a Dana 44 front swap (junkyard XJ or Rubicon JK donor) rather than continuing to upgrade the Dana 30 \u2014 at that point, you've spent enough on the Dana 30 that the swap is the better dollar.","steps":["Inspect ball joints first \u2014 grab the top of the tire at 12 and 6 o'clock and rock it; any play means replacement.","Pull both front axle shafts. Inspect U-joints for play, rust, and pitting.","If shafts are stock 27-spline with small U-joints and you run 33s+, plan a chromoly shaft + 5-297X upgrade.","Press out old ball joints with a ball joint press. Install heavy-duty replacements per torque spec (upper 80 ft-lb, lower 100 ft-lb).","Reassemble shafts with new U-joints, torque axle nut to 175 ft-lb.","If installing a locker, this is the time \u2014 pull the third member, swap the carrier, set backlash and pinion preload.","Refill diff with 75W-90 gear oil (2.0 qt) and a friction modifier if running a clutch-style limited slip."],"pros":["Cheaper than a full Dana 44 swap","Bolt-in upgrade path \u2014 no fabrication","Chromoly shafts and HD ball joints solve 80% of failures","Strong enough for 35s with the right shafts"],"cons":["Locker plus stock shafts is a recipe for trail breakage","Ball joints are a known weak spot, especially lifted","30-spline upgrade is incomplete unless outers also upgrade","Above 35s, the math favors a Dana 44 swap instead"]},{"id":"tj-dana44-rear","title":"TJ Dana 44 Rear Axle \u2014 What the Rubicon Has and Why It Matters","vehicle":"tj","category":"axles","type":"reference","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":2500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","axle stands","breaker bar","1/2\" impact","bearing press or rental","dial indicator for backlash setup"],"skills_required":["intermediate to advanced wrenching","drivetrain experience helpful"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["2003-2006 Rubicon only (factory D44)","1997-2006 (aftermarket swap)"],"tags":["axles","dana44","rubicon","locker","regear"],"parts":[{"name":"Alloy USA axle shaft kit \u2014 Dana 44 TJ rear","vendor":"Alloy USA","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Yukon Gear ring and pinion 4.10 \u2014 Dana 44","vendor":"Yukon Gear","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"ARB Air Locker \u2014 Dana 44 (35-spline)","part_number":"RD100","vendor":"ARB","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Eaton Detroit Locker \u2014 Dana 44","vendor":"Eaton","price_estimate_usd":425}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Rubicon Dana 44 Axle Specs \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/tj-rubicon-dana-44-axle-specs.html"},{"title":"Dana 44 vs Dana 35 \u2014 Trail Manual Database","url":"/db/?v=tj"},{"title":"Yukon Gear Ring and Pinion Installation Guide","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/install-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-dana35-upgrade","tj-axle-shaft-replacement","tj-locker-install","tj-regear"],"summary":"The 2003\u20132006 TJ Rubicon came with a Dana 44 rear axle with a factory Selec-Trac locker. Every other TJ came with a Dana 35 \u2014 a lighter axle that's adequate for mild trail use but a liability under real stress.","description":"The Dana 35 vs. Dana 44 split is the most consequential axle difference in the TJ lineup. The Dana 35 (2003\u20132006 non-Rubicon Sport and Sahara) uses 27-spline axle shafts with a 7.125\" ring gear. The Dana 44 (2003\u20132006 Rubicon) uses 30-spline shafts with an 8.5\" ring gear. The Dana 44 is meaningfully stronger \u2014 more material in the shafts, larger gears, and factory-ready for a locker. Under real off-road load with larger tires, the Dana 35 C-clip axle shafts are the known weak point.\n\n**Why C-clips matter:** The Dana 35 uses C-clip axle retention. When a shaft breaks, the C-clip releases and the wheel can come off the Jeep. The Dana 44 Rubicon uses a full-float axle design with a stub shaft \u2014 a broken shaft doesn't drop the wheel. On a shelf or at an overlander meetup, this difference is academic. On a hillclimb 12 miles from the trailhead, it isn't.\n\n**Your options:**\n1. **Run the Dana 35 as-is:** Fine for stock tire sizes (30\"\u201331\") and moderate dirt trails. Add Alloy USA 30-spline shafts to fix the weakest failure point ($200\u2013$250 for the pair). Not a major upgrade, but it delays the failure mode.\n2. **Regear the Dana 35:** If you're upgrading tires and need new gears anyway, have the Dana 35 regeared while it's out. More cost-effective than buying a D44 if the build stays mild.\n3. **Swap in a used Rubicon Dana 44:** A used Rubicon axle runs $500\u2013$900 at a salvage yard depending on condition, lockers, and ratios. It's a bolt-in on the TJ \u2014 same spring perch width and 35\" brake compatibility. Check for worn bearings and inspect the locker actuator.\n4. **Build or buy an aftermarket Dana 44:** Barnes4WD, RCV, and Currie make purpose-built D44s for the TJ. These are stronger than the Rubicon axle and can be ordered with your ratio and locker preference already set. Cost: $2,000\u2013$3,500. Makes sense for a dedicated rock crawler build.\n\n- Ring gear: 8.5\" diameter\n- Axle shaft: 30-spline (outer stub)\n- Factory locker: Selec-Trac electric locker (driver controls from dash)\n- Factory ratio: 4.10:1 (matches the front Dana 44)\n- Width: same as Dana 35 \u2014 direct bolt-in for TJ spring perch\n\nA locker forces both axle shafts to turn at the same speed. In a standard open differential, the path of least resistance gets the power \u2014 the wheel in the air, the wheel on ice. A locker eliminates that: both wheels turn together. On the trail, this makes the difference between crawling over an obstacle and spinning one wheel while the other does nothing. The Rubicon's electric locker is controlled from the dash and can be engaged at low speed. ARB air lockers and Eaton Detroit lockers are the two most common aftermarket options.\n\n- The Rubicon D44 uses a different ABS sensor location than the Dana 35. If your TJ has ABS, confirm the sensor compatibility before finalizing.\n- Rubicon D44 drum brakes require the Rubicon parking brake cable \u2014 it's longer than the non-Rubicon cable.\n- Differential cover bolts on used axles are often over-torqued by the previous owner and strip easily. Use a impact with torque limiting if you can.\n- After any gear ratio change, recalibrate your speedometer. On a TJ, this is done through the JTEC PCM with a dealer scan tool or DiabloSport tuner.\n\n- Used Rubicon Dana 44 from salvage: $500\u2013$900\n- Alloy USA shaft upgrade for existing Dana 35: $200\u2013$250\n- ARB Air Locker for D44: $500\u2013$550 at 4WP or Amazon\n- Regear (ring & pinion set + install): $600\u2013$900 installed at a drivetrain shop"},{"id":"tj-fender-flares","title":"TJ Fender Flares \u2014 Replacing or Upgrading for Wider Tires","vehicle":"tj","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":400},"tools_required":["10mm socket","flat-blade screwdriver","trim clip removal tool","drill with bits (for tube flares)","rivet gun (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic trim work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["body","fenders","tires","clearance","legality"],"parts":[{"name":"Bushwacker Flat Style fender flares \u2014 TJ","vendor":"Bushwacker","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Smittybilt XRC replacement fender flares","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Rugged Ridge tube fender flare kit","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":190},{"name":"Poison Spyder tube fenders (sheetmetal)","vendor":"Poison Spyder","price_estimate_usd":380}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Fender Flare Options and Tire Clearance Guide","url":"https://www.jk-forum.com/forums/tj-wrangler-technical-forum-15/fender-flares-guide/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Running 33s on a stock TJ you need wider flares to stay legal \u2014 stock flares cover stock width. Budget flares from Smittybilt bolt straight to stock holes; tube fenders need drilling but look cleaner with big lifts.","description":"Arizona, like most states, requires tires to be covered to the centerline. Stock TJ flares cover up to roughly 31-inch tires at stock width \u2014 move to 33s with any lift and the tire sidewall starts poking past the fender line. The fix is either wider replacement flares or tube fenders, depending on how aggressive the build is going.\n\nFor 33s and mild lifts (2.5 to 3 inches), a flat-style or extended OEM-style replacement flare adds 1 to 2 inches of coverage per side and installs in the factory clip holes. Bushwacker's Flat Style and Smittybilt's replacement flares are the common choices \u2014 both are OE-style ABS plastic, bolt on without drilling, and run $100 to $180 for the pair. They look stock enough to not draw attention.\n\nFor 35s and serious lift (4+ inches), tube fenders are the better answer. They add more coverage, they don't crack when a rock hits them like OEM-profile ABS flares do, and they clear the tire at full articulation rather than just at ride height. The tradeoff is drilling into the body for mounting bolts. Rugged Ridge tube flares run $180 and are considered entry-level. Poison Spyder's tube fenders are sheetmetal, look right, and hold up to rock contact \u2014 closer to $400. If you're building a rock crawler, metal tube fenders are worth it.\n\n- Replacement flares matched to TJ (OEM-style) or tube fenders\n- 10mm socket and trim clip removal tool for stock flare removal\n- Drill and step bit if installing tube fenders that require new mounting points\n- Wire brush and touchup paint \u2014 the areas under the flares are usually surface-rusted on high-mileage TJs\n\n1. **Remove old flares.** The stock TJ flares clip to the fender lip and secure with 10mm bolts at the wheel arch. Pop the clips with a trim tool, remove bolts, and lift flare off. It takes about 15 minutes per flare.\n\n2. **Clean the mounting surface.** Scrub any rust with a wire brush and hit bare metal with rattle-can primer. You won't see this area again for years.\n\n3. **Install replacement OEM-style flares.** Align over stock clip holes, press in clips, torque bolts. Test fit before final torque \u2014 misaligned flares look worse than old ones.\n\n4. **For tube fenders:** Test fit and mark drill points before committing. Drill pilot holes, step up to final diameter, deburr and prime. Bolt through with stainless hardware and locking nuts.\n\n5. **Check clearance at full lock and full droop.** Turn the wheel lock-to-lock with the Jeep on the ground. Check for rub points at full flex with a friend compressing the suspension by hand.\n\n- Tube fenders require re-locating the turn signal. Budget an extra hour and a turn signal relocation harness ($20\u2013$30).\n- Inspect the inner fender area for rust before reinstalling any flare \u2014 this is a common rust accumulation zone on older TJs from wet climates.\n- Not all \"TJ flares\" fit all years \u2014 check your year before ordering. A few flare kits fit either TJ or JK but not both.\n\nOEM-style replacement flares: $90\u2013$180 installed in under an hour with no drilling. Tube fenders: $180\u2013$400 depending on material, plus 3\u20134 hours of work and a turn signal relocation. Sheetmetal tube fenders outlast plastic alternatives in rock use."},{"id":"tj-soft-top-replacement","title":"TJ Soft Top Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"body","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":600},"tools_required":["trim panel tool","T25 Torx","scissors or utility knife","rubber mallet","helper (recommended)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","patience with fabric and bows"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["soft-top","weatherproofing","body"],"parts":[{"name":"Bestop Supertop NX \u2014 black diamond","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":530},{"name":"Bestop Supertop Classic (budget tier)","vendor":"Bestop","price_estimate_usd":330},{"name":"Rugged Ridge All-Terrain replacement top","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":260},{"name":"Smittybilt OEM-style replacement top","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"Bestop Supertop NX Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.bestop.com/fitment/vehicle/1997-2006-jeep-wrangler-tj/"},{"title":"TJ Soft Top Replacement \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/how-to-install-a-soft-top.html"}],"alternatives":["tj-hardtop-removal","tj-door-removal"],"summary":"Replacing a TJ soft top is a 2\u20133 hour job the first time; once you've done it, an hour is realistic. The biggest variable is bow frame condition \u2014 cracked or bent bows will prevent a clean seal no matter how good the fabric is.","description":"The TJ soft top is a folding bow frame assembly covered by fabric. When the top leaks, sags, or tears, the fabric itself is almost always the problem \u2014 the bow frames typically outlast two or three fabrics if the Jeep lives in a garage. Before buying a full replacement top, inspect the bow frames: look for cracks at the pivot points, bends from low-clearance parking damage, and broken end caps. If the frames are solid, a fabric-only kit runs $150\u2013$200 less than a full top kit.\n\nBestop is the OEM supplier for TJ soft tops and the Supertop NX is the benchmark. Smittybilt and Rugged Ridge are workable budget options but the vinyl is typically thinner and the zip seal quality is noticeably lower \u2014 fine for an occasional-use rig, harder to live with as a daily driver in rain or cold. In sub-freezing temps, cheaper vinyl becomes stiff and cracks at fold points within a couple of seasons.\n\nThe windows on the TJ top are zip-in \u2014 they attach to the top fabric via a zipper and are retained by snaps. Window replacement is a separate job and the clear vinyl tends to haze before the fabric fails. If the top is leaking at a window seam rather than at the bow rail or header, you might only need new windows.\n\n- The header seal leaks if the clamp levers don't fully engage. If you're getting water intrusion at the header on a new top, check that the bow is fully seated in the windshield channel before latching.\n- Don't force windows into the zipper track. If the zip-in window isn't tracking smoothly, back it out and realign \u2014 forcing it splits the zipper tape.\n- In cold weather, warm the fabric in sunlight for 20\u201330 minutes before installation. Cold vinyl doesn't stretch and the snap alignment will be off.\n- Snap alignment on budget tops is sometimes off by 5\u201310mm. A heated snap setter can reposition snaps; gluing them down is a last resort.\n\n- Bestop Supertop NX: $500\u2013$550 at 4WP, Quadratec, or Amazon\n- Smittybilt OEM-style: $240\u2013$270 at Amazon\n- Fabric-only (Bestop Sailcloth): $180\u2013$220 for just the skin\n- Windows (Bestop replacement rear window kit): $80\u2013$120\n\nBudget for the top + a half-hour of frustration on the header if it's your first install.","steps":["Fold the existing top rearward to expose the header (front bow). Release the header clamps \u2014 two levers that fold down and lock the front bar against the windshield frame.","Unsnap the top from the windshield channel. The fabric snaps to a metal strip that runs across the top of the windshield frame.","Work rearward unsnapping the top from the body tub. TJ tops use snaps along both sides and across the tailgate.","Unzip and remove windows. Store them flat \u2014 folding them causes crease hazing.","Remove the fabric from the bow frames. The fabric slides over the front bow and typically attaches with Velcro or screws at each bow. Consult the replacement kit instructions \u2014 attachment varies by manufacturer.","If reusing bow frames: inspect each pivot, wipe down all contact points, and apply a thin coat of rubber lubricant to the seals before fitting the new fabric.","Slide the new top over the front bow. Pull rearward so the fabric is smooth and even across the bows.","Attach the header to the windshield channel. The header bar should sit flush and snap tight \u2014 if it's lifting at the corners, the fabric tension is off. Work out the wrinkles before latching.","Work rearward snapping the fabric to the body tub. Start at the front corners and pull toward the back \u2014 chasing wrinkles toward the tailgate.","Reinstall and zip in the windows. Confirm all snaps and the zipper seal are seated."]},{"id":"tj-front-pads","title":"Front Brake Pad and Rotor Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"brakes","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":230},"tools_required":["3/8\" hex bit (caliper slider)","15mm or 18mm socket","torque wrench","C-clamp","wire brush","brake cleaner","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["brakes","pads","rotors","safety-critical","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front brake pads","part_number":"5018572AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":50},{"name":"Centric Premium front rotors (pair)","part_number":"120.67032","vendor":"Centric","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"Powerstop Z36 Truck and Tow front kit","vendor":"Powerstop","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"EBC Yellowstuff pads (heavy use)","vendor":"EBC","price_estimate_usd":75}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Wrangler Front Caliper Bolt Torque Spec (11-13 ft-lb)","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/torque-values-for-brake-caliper-bolts.479649/"},{"title":"TJ Brake Caliper Mounting Torque Discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/brake-caliper-mounting-bolt-torque.4461775/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-drums"],"summary":"TJ front pads run 30,000\u201345,000 miles depending on terrain and gear. The 1997\u20132002 TJ uses a 3/8\" hex bit on the caliper slider; the 2003+ Rubicon and LJ use a slightly larger bolt head. Caliper slider torque is 11\u201313 ft-lb \u2014 easy to strip.","description":"The TJ front brake setup is single-piston floating caliper on vented rotors, same general layout as the XJ Cherokee it shares an axle with. Two slider pins hold the caliper; two large bracket bolts hold the bracket to the steering knuckle. What makes the TJ slightly different from the XJ is the slider bolts use a 3/8\" hex (Allen) head on 1997\u20132002 trucks \u2014 you'll need a hex bit socket, not a regular hex socket, to break them loose without rounding them.\n\nA note on tools: many TJ owners discover mid-job that their breaker bar set is missing the 3/8\" hex bit. Pick one up before starting. The slider bolts often blue-Loctite from the factory and don't always come loose easily \u2014 a quality bit and a breaker bar beat a stripped slider every time.\n\nPad recommendations for the TJ skew toward heavier compounds than the XJ because TJs are typically lifted on bigger tires more aggressively. Stock pads from Mopar (PN 5018572AB) are fine for a stock-tire Sport. Anything past 33s, add weight, or tow occasionally \u2014 go to Powerstop Z36 or EBC Yellowstuff. The TJ's brake system is the same hardware as a much lighter vehicle; the only way to make it deal with 35s and a winch bumper is harder pad compound and a heavier rotor.\n\nRotor choice: Centric Premium ($40 each) or Powerstop Evolution ($45 each) are solid stock-replacement options that often outlast OEM. Skip the cheap blank rotors from chain auto parts stores \u2014 they warp within 5,000 miles on a daily-driven TJ. Mopar OEM rotors are decent but expensive; Centric Premium gives 90% of the durability for half the price.\n\nThe TJ caliper slider bolts are the most-stripped bolts on the front end. Chrysler spec is 11\u201313 ft-lb. That feels finger-tight to anyone used to lug nuts. Use a torque wrench. Over-torque and you either snap the bolt or strip the bracket threads \u2014 both leave you with a non-driveable Jeep until you helicoil the bracket or find a new one.","steps":["Crack lug nuts (19mm), lift the front of the TJ, support on stands at the front axle.","Remove front wheels. Pull the master cylinder reservoir cap and drape a rag.","Remove the two caliper slider bolts using a 3/8\" hex bit (1997\u20132002) or 13mm socket (2003+ LJ/Rubicon).","Slide caliper off the bracket. Hang from a wire \u2014 don't let it dangle by the hose.","Compress the caliper piston back with a C-clamp using an old pad as a buffer.","Pull the old pads from the bracket. Note clip orientation.","To replace rotors: remove the two bracket bolts (15mm on early TJ, 18mm on Rubicon/LJ) from the steering knuckle. Lift bracket off. Pull old rotor.","Wire-brush the hub mating face. Rust ridges here cause runout.","Install new rotor (light anti-seize on the hub face only, not on the friction surface). Reinstall bracket and torque to 100 ft-lb.","Install new pads with new anti-rattle clips. Light brake grease on pad backs and slider pins.","Slide caliper over pads onto the bracket. Reinstall slider bolts and torque to 11 ft-lb \u2014 do not exceed.","Reinstall wheels, torque lugs to 100 ft-lb.","Pump brake pedal until firm. Top off reservoir.","Bed in pads: 8\u201310 moderate stops from 35 mph, then 2\u20133 harder stops from 50 mph."],"pros":["Shared parts with XJ Cherokee \u2014 wide aftermarket support","Common floating caliper, no special tools beyond hex bit","Centric Premium rotors outlast OEM at half the price","Stock pad life 30K\u201345K on daily-driven TJ"],"cons":["Caliper slider bolts (11 ft-lb) easily over-torqued and stripped","3/8\" hex bit required for 1997\u20132002 sliders \u2014 easy to miss when prepping","Stock brakes undersized for lifted 33\"+ builds; need harder pad compound","Bargain rotors warp in 5,000 miles \u2014 buy Centric or better"]},{"id":"tj-rear-drum-service","title":"TJ Rear Drum Brake Service \u2014 Shoes, Hardware, and Adjustment","vehicle":"tj","category":"brakes","type":"procedure","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":120},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","lug wrench","brake spring pliers","flat-blade screwdriver","brake cleaner","torque wrench","drum puller (sometimes)"],"skills_required":["brake service","spring tension work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["brakes","drums","shoes","maintenance","safety"],"parts":[{"name":"Raybestos Professional Grade rear brake shoe set","part_number":"PGD166M","vendor":"Raybestos","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Wagner ThermoQuiet rear shoe set","vendor":"Wagner","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Brake drum hardware kit (springs, adjuster, pins)","vendor":"Dorman / ACDelco","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Bendix rear drums (if scoring requires replacement)","vendor":"Bendix","price_estimate_usd":45}],"citations":[{"title":"How Drum Brakes Work \u2014 Auto Service World","url":"https://www.autoserviceworld.com/how-drum-brakes-work/"},{"title":"TJ rear drum brake DIY","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"TJ rear drums are underappreciated and often neglected \u2014 most owners replace front pads and ignore the rears. Glazed shoes and a misadjusted star wheel are responsible for more TJ brake fade than worn pads.","description":"The TJ runs rear drum brakes on all trims (base and Rubicon). They're a fully mechanical system: two curved brake shoes press against the inside of a cast drum when the wheel cylinder extends. The parking brake integrates here too \u2014 a cable pulls the rear shoes directly. Drum brakes self-adjust through a star wheel adjuster when the vehicle is driven in reverse and the brakes are applied, but that adjuster gets lazy over time and the shoes drift away from the drum, extending pedal travel.\n\nService life on rear shoes is typically 40,000 to 60,000 miles in normal use, but varies widely \u2014 high-mileage off-road use shortens it, mostly because grit gets past the drum and acts as an abrasive. Glazed shoes (shiny, hardened contact surface) can happen earlier if the Jeep sat for a long time without use, which locks the shoes against the drum and bakes them.\n\nThis job requires more patience than skill. Drum brakes have a lot of springs under significant tension and the geometry of how they go back together matters. Take a photo before disassembly.\n\n- Floor jack, jack stands \u2014 never work under a TJ supported only by a jack\n- Brake spring pliers (the correct tool, not needle-nose \u2014 this is the one place where \"use the right tool\" matters most)\n- Brake cleaner and rags\n- New shoes and hardware kit\n- Micrometer or drum gauge (to measure drum wear \u2014 maximum diameter is cast into the drum)\n\n1. **Break lug nuts loose before jacking.** Lift the rear axle on the pumpkin (center differential housing) to lift both sides at once. Place jack stands under the axle tubes.\n\n2. **Remove wheels and drums.** Most TJ drums pull straight off once the wheels are removed. If the drum won't come off, back off the star wheel adjuster \u2014 there's an access hole in the backing plate. Never hammer a drum off the hub; you'll damage the wheel studs.\n\n3. **Photo before disassembly.** Drum brake layout seems obvious until it isn't. A quick photo saves 20 minutes of head-scratching.\n\n4. **Remove return springs first.** Use brake spring pliers. There are two shoe return springs at the top, one at the bottom, and a self-adjuster spring. The return springs are under the most tension \u2014 respect them.\n\n5. **Remove hold-down pins, adjuster, and shoes.** The hold-down pins go through the backing plate and are retained by a clip you rotate 90 degrees. The adjuster star wheel assembly pulls out once the shoes are free.\n\n6. **Clean the backing plate.** Wire brush any rust off the shoe contact pads on the backing plate. Apply a thin film of high-temp brake grease to those pads only \u2014 not to the drum contact surface on the shoes.\n\n7. **Install new shoes in reverse order.** Hardware kit replaces all springs and pins \u2014 don't reuse stretched springs. The longer shoe (primary) faces forward; the shorter (secondary) faces rearward.\n\n8. **Adjust the star wheel.** Expand the adjuster until the drum drags slightly as you spin it, then back off until it spins freely with light drag. This sets the initial clearance.\n\n9. **Reinstall drums, wheels. Torque lugs to 95 ft-lb.**\n\n10. **Pump brakes until firm before moving the Jeep.** The wheel cylinders need to extend to seat the new shoes.\n\n11. **Bed the shoes.** Drive at 30 mph, apply moderate brake pressure to slow to 5 mph (don't stop). Repeat 6\u20138 times. This seats the shoe-to-drum contact surface.\n\n- If the wheel cylinder is leaking (wet or weeping brake fluid behind the drum), replace it now. It's a $15 part and takes 30 minutes \u2014 much less than pulling the drum again later.\n- Maximum drum diameter is stamped on the drum face. If the drum is beyond spec, replace it. Drums are cheap insurance.\n- Safety-critical: never drive the Jeep with the brakes disassembled. Keep a buddy nearby the first time you pull the drums off a vehicle new to you \u2014 stuck calipers and failed wheel cylinders aren't always obvious until you see them.\n\nShoe set: $28\u2013$45. Hardware kit: $18. New drums if needed: $40\u2013$55 each (most TJs need them by 80,000 miles). Full rear drum service parts total: $65\u2013$120. Don't skip the hardware kit \u2014 springs fatigue and the $18 replacement is cheap relative to pulling the drums again."},{"id":"tj-coolant-flush","title":"TJ 4.0L Cooling System Flush and Coolant Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":60},"tools_required":["drain pan (2+ gallon)","funnel","garden hose","pliers or hose clamp tool","rags"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["cooling","maintenance","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Peak 50/50 premixed coolant (1 gallon)","vendor":"Peak","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Zerex Original Green concentrate (1 gallon)","vendor":"Zerex","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"BlueDevil Radiator Flush (optional pre-flush)","vendor":"BlueDevil","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Thermostat \u2014 Stant 45352 (195\u00b0F)","part_number":"45352","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":12}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ 4.0L Cooling System Overview \u2014 Jeep Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/cooling-system-service-specs.html"},{"title":"Coolant Drain and Fill Procedure \u2014 Quadratec Tech","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-tech/cooling-system-maintenance"}],"alternatives":["tj-thermostat-replacement","tj-radiator-replacement","tj-water-pump"],"summary":"Drain the lower radiator hose, flush with water, refill with fresh 50/50 coolant \u2014 the TJ 4.0L holds about 2 gallons. Do it every 2 years or 30,000 miles regardless of color.","description":"The TJ's AMC 4.0L inline-six runs a conventional green-formula coolant system. Chrysler spec is green (silicate-based) coolant, which depletes inhibitor faster than OAT or HOAT formulas \u2014 2 years or 30,000 miles is the real service interval even if the coolant still looks green. Old coolant turns acidic and starts attacking aluminum components: the water pump impeller, the thermostat housing, and the heater core are the first casualties.\n\nThe TJ doesn't have a petcock drain on the radiator \u2014 you drain by disconnecting the lower radiator hose at the radiator inlet. It's a 30-second job but messy. Have a wide drain pan and rags ready.\n\nCapacity is approximately 7.5 quarts (1.875 gallons) for the 4.0L. The recovery bottle holds another 0.5\u20130.75 quarts. Two gallons of 50/50 premixed coolant covers it with a little left over.\n\nRunning a pre-flush product (BlueDevil, Prestone flush, or similar) is worth doing if the coolant hasn't been changed in more than 4 years or is visibly rusty. Add the flush product, run the engine for 10\u201315 minutes, drain, flush with clean water, and then refill.\n\n- Coolant smell at the heater after a flush usually means air is still in the system. Run the engine with the heater on MAX until the hot air holds steady.\n- Rusty or brown coolant is a sign the inhibitor is long gone. A flush product won't fully remove scale \u2014 a shop radiator flush machine does a better job on badly neglected systems.\n- If the engine is slow to warm up or runs cooler than normal after a flush, suspect the thermostat. A stuck-open thermostat often hides until a flush makes it obvious. Replace it while the system is open \u2014 a Stant 45352 (195\u00b0F) is about $12.\n- Coolant disposal: old coolant is toxic to animals. Don't pour it down a storm drain. Most auto parts stores accept used coolant.\n\n- 50/50 premixed coolant (2 gallons): $22\u2013$28 at any auto parts store\n- Concentrate + distilled water (mix your own): roughly the same cost, more control over ratio\n- Radiator flush product: $7\u2013$10, optional but worthwhile on older systems\n- New lower hose clamps if the old ones are rusty: $3\u2013$5 each","steps":["Let the engine cool completely. Don't open the cap on a hot system.","Place drain pan under the passenger-side lower radiator hose (connects to the lower radiator inlet).","Loosen the hose clamp with pliers or a clamp tool. Twist the hose to break the seal and pull it off the radiator neck. Let coolant drain.","Reconnect the hose once draining slows. Tighten the clamp.","(Optional flush): Add radiator flush product and top off with water. Start engine, let reach operating temperature, idle 10\u201315 minutes. Drain again.","Flush with plain water: fill with water, idle 5 minutes, drain. Repeat until the drain runs clear.","Reconnect the lower hose and tighten the clamp.","Remove the radiator cap. Fill slowly with 50/50 premixed coolant (or a 50/50 mix of concentrate and distilled water). Fill to the base of the filler neck.","Fill the overflow recovery bottle to the MIN mark.","Start the engine and let it come to operating temperature with the heater set to MAX HEAT. This bleeds air from the system \u2014 the heater core is the highest point in the circuit.","Watch the coolant level drop as air purges. Top off as needed.","Install the radiator cap. Check for leaks at the lower hose.","After the first drive, recheck the overflow bottle level and top to MIN/MAX as needed."]},{"id":"tj-radiator-replacement","title":"TJ Radiator Replacement \u2014 When to Replace and What to Buy","vehicle":"tj","category":"cooling","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":75,"high":320},"tools_required":["drain pan","Phillips screwdriver","10mm socket","pliers","coolant funnel","flush kit (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic cooling system work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["cooling","radiator","overheating","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"OSC Radiator \u2014 4.0L TJ (4-cylinder models use different part)","part_number":"2521","vendor":"OSC Automotive","price_estimate_usd":90},{"name":"Spectra Premium CU2521 aluminum radiator","vendor":"Spectra Premium","price_estimate_usd":105},{"name":"Mishimoto Performance aluminum radiator \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Mishimoto","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"Prestone 50/50 premixed coolant","vendor":"Prestone","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep TJ 4.0 cooling system overview","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/tech-support/jeep-tj-cooling-system"},{"title":"How to Replace a Radiator","url":"https://www.autozone.com/diy/cooling-system/how-to-replace-radiator"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A stock TJ 4.0 radiator lasts 10\u201315 years before internal corrosion or plastic-end cracking becomes the failure mode. The replacement is a straightforward 2-hour job \u2014 budget aluminum upgrades from Mishimoto are worth the price on any TJ seeing trail or tow use.","description":"The TJ 4.0L runs a two-row aluminum core with plastic end tanks from the factory. The end tanks crack at the crimp joint \u2014 usually the lower tank, often after 10+ years or 120,000+ miles. Signs of failure: coolant dripping from the lower radiator area, slow coolant loss with no leak visible until the engine is running, or visible green or orange staining on the lower hose fittings. A pressure test will confirm it.\n\nAftermarket aluminum radiators (both end tanks and core are aluminum) cost $90 to $120 for standard replacements \u2014 essentially the same price as OEM-spec units. The plastic-tank failure mode goes away entirely. For a TJ used for towing, off-road summer use in hot climates, or with an engine that runs warm, the Mishimoto Performance unit ($280) has a 3-row core that noticeably drops operating temps. Unless you're tracking temps and finding headroom, the standard replacement unit is fine.\n\nDon't chase overheating with just a new radiator if you haven't verified the thermostat, water pump, and fan clutch are working properly. A failing fan clutch causes overheating at idle and slow-speed crawling on hot days \u2014 the symptom pattern the TJ is famous for.\n\n- Replacement radiator (confirm 4-cylinder vs. 6-cylinder \u2014 different radiators)\n- 2 gallons of premixed 50/50 coolant (or 1 gallon distilled water + 1 gallon antifreeze concentrate)\n- Drain pan\n- Phillips and 10mm socket\n- New hose clamps (optional but recommended if original clamps are 15+ years old)\n\n1. **Let the engine cool completely.** Never open a hot cooling system \u2014 the pressurized coolant will cause burns.\n\n2. **Drain the coolant.** Open the petcock at the lower driver's side of the radiator and drain into a clean pan. Dispose of coolant properly \u2014 don't pour it on the ground. Coolant is toxic to animals.\n\n3. **Disconnect the upper and lower hoses.** Loosen clamps, twist and pull \u2014 a strap wrench helps on stubborn hoses. Have rags ready.\n\n4. **Disconnect the transmission cooler lines (if automatic).** Use a flare-nut wrench if possible to avoid rounding the fittings. Cap the lines.\n\n5. **Remove radiator shroud bolts and upper mount bolts.** The shroud (fan surround) typically lifts off with the radiator. The radiator mounts to a top bracket with two 10mm bolts and sits in rubber lower cushions.\n\n6. **Lift the radiator out.** Watch the fan blades \u2014 they're close. Tilting the top toward you clears the fan.\n\n7. **Install the new radiator.** Seat in the lower cushions first, then bolt the upper bracket. Reconnect transmission cooler lines if applicable.\n\n8. **Reinstall hoses and fill with coolant.** Fill slowly \u2014 the 4.0 system holds about 11 quarts. Fill, run to operating temp, check level after bleeding any trapped air pockets.\n\n9. **Pressure check.** Use a radiator pressure tester to confirm no leaks before driving.\n\n- Inspect the upper and lower hoses while you have the radiator out. If they're soft, cracked, or original-to-vehicle, replace them. A hose failure two weeks after a radiator job is aggravating.\n- The TJ 4.0 also runs a small coolant line to the throttle body \u2014 usually just a bypass hose. Inspect it at the same time.\n- If you're replacing the radiator due to overheating and the new radiator doesn't fix it, the fan clutch is the next thing to check. Spin the fan by hand with the engine cold \u2014 it should have moderate resistance. Free-spinning or extremely stiff (seized) both indicate failure.\n\nOEM-spec replacement radiator: $90\u2013$120. Performance aluminum (Mishimoto): $275\u2013$300. Coolant: $22\u2013$28 for premixed. Full cooling system service (radiator + hoses + thermostat + flush): $150\u2013$200 in parts \u2014 a reasonable 3-hour Saturday job on a high-mileage TJ."},{"id":"tj-ax15-tj","title":"AX-15 Manual Transmission Service","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":80},"tools_required":["15/16 inch socket or 24mm","ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-1999","2000-2002","2003-2004"],"tags":["maintenance","drivetrain"],"parts":[{"name":"Redline MT-90 75W-90 GL-4 (qt)","part_number":"50304","vendor":"Red Line Oil","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Pennzoil Synchromesh","vendor":"Pennzoil","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Magnetic Drain Plug Replacement","part_number":"Omix-Ada 18880.74","vendor":"Omix-Ada","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"AX-15 Manual Transmission Service \u2014 JeepSpecs","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/tj-generation/ax-15-transmission-fluid-change/"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Fluid Capacities","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/"},{"title":"TJ Transmission and Transfer Case Fluid Change","url":"https://www.billswebspace.com/TJTrans.htm"}],"alternatives":["tj-np231-tj","tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj"],"summary":"Drain and refill the AX-15 with 3.3 quarts of 75W-90 GL-4 (not GL-5) every 30,000 miles \u2014 60-minute job, magnetic drain plug, no special tools.","description":"The AX-15 is a 5-speed Aisin manual that came in TJs from 1997 to 2004 paired with the 4.0L. It's the strongest manual Jeep ever bolted behind the 4.0 and it shifts cleanly for decades when the fluid is fresh. What kills an AX-15 early is the wrong fluid \u2014 and there is a persistent forum debate about what \"the right fluid\" actually is. The factory service manual specifies GL-3, but GL-3 is essentially extinct in the consumer market. The Mopar PN for the original fluid (75W-90 GL-3) has been superseded.\n\nThe honest answer: run 75W-90 GL-4. GL-5 gear oils contain sulfur-phosphorus additives that attack the AX-15's yellow-metal synchronizer parts and will cause crunchy shifts within a few thousand miles. Redline MT-90 (75W-90 GL-4) is the most-recommended choice on TJ forums for a reason \u2014 it shifts smoothly cold and hot. Pennzoil Synchromesh is the other widely-used option, especially for owners who want a slightly thinner feel in cold weather. Stay away from anything labeled \"gear oil 80W-90\" off the auto parts shelf unless you confirm the GL-4 rating on the bottle.\n\nCapacity is 3.3 quarts (some sources round to 3.25). The drain plug is on the passenger-side bottom of the case and takes a 15/16\" or 24mm socket. It's magnetic \u2014 pull it after every change and wipe the metal paste off. Fine glitter is normal. Chunks, shavings, or anything the size of a grain of rice means a synchro or bearing is on its way out and you should pull the transmission for inspection.\n\nCrack the fill plug FIRST before opening the drain. The fill plug is on the driver's side of the case (square 3/8\" or 1/2\" drive). If the fill plug is seized and you've already drained, you have no way to refill without removing the transmission.\n\nWhile you're under there: feel the shift rail boot at the top of the transmission for cracks (a torn boot lets water into the case), inspect the rear yoke seal for weeping, and check the slave cylinder line for swelling if you have a hydraulic clutch.","steps":["Drive the Jeep 10\u201315 miles to warm the gear oil. Park on level ground.","Locate the fill plug on the driver's side of the case. Break it loose first \u2014 do not skip this step.","Position drain pan under the passenger-side drain plug. Remove the drain plug with a 15/16\" or 24mm socket.","Let it drain fully \u2014 15 to 20 minutes for warm fluid.","Wipe the magnetic drain plug clean. Inspect the magnet for chunks or large shavings. Reinstall and torque to 14\u201320 ft-lbs.","Use a fluid pump to refill through the fill hole until fluid drips back out \u2014 about 3.3 quarts.","Reinstall the fill plug and torque to 25 ft-lbs.","Drive a few miles, run through all five gears plus reverse, and recheck for leaks at the drain and fill plugs."],"pros":["One-hour job with a $40 fluid bill","Catches early bearing or synchro failure via drain plug debris","Restores notchy-cold shifts that the original fluid no longer handles"],"cons":["Wrong fluid (GL-5) will damage the synchros \u2014 easy mistake at the parts counter","Fill plug seizes on rust-belt TJs and can require heat to break free","Capacity is awkward to measure \u2014 fill until it overflows, not by quart count"]},{"id":"tj-front-driveshaft","title":"Front Driveshaft \u2014 CV Joint Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","subcategory":"driveshafts","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":600},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","12mm socket","13mm socket","torque wrench","ball peen hammer","u-joint press or vise"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","u-joint service"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["vibration","lift","rubicon"],"parts":[{"name":"Spicer 5-213X greaseable u-joint (Rubicon 1330 CV)","part_number":"5-213X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Spicer 211179X CV centering yoke + spring kit (TJ Rubicon)","part_number":"211179X","vendor":"Spicer","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Tom Wood's custom CV front driveshaft (TJ Rubicon, 1330)","vendor":"Tom Wood's","price_estimate_usd":525},{"name":"Denny's rebuild kit \u2014 TJ Rubicon 1330 CV (2 u-joints + centering yoke)","vendor":"Denny's Driveshaft","price_estimate_usd":165}],"citations":[{"title":"Denny's TJ Rubicon 1330 CV Rebuild Kit","url":"https://www.dennysdriveshaft.com/p2298_jeep_tj_rubicon_2003_to_2006_cv_rebuild_kit_1330_series_incl.html"},{"title":"WranglerTJ Forum \u2014 Driveshaft torque specs","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/driveshaft-torque-specs.24608/"},{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 Wrangler TJ Driveshaft Parts","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-replacement-parts/wrangler-tj/drive-shafts"}],"alternatives":["tj-np231-service","tj-track-bar"],"summary":"A vibrating front driveshaft on a TJ is almost always a worn u-joint or, on Rubicons, a collapsed CV centering yoke \u2014 both are rebuildable for under $200, but past 4\" of lift most TJs are better off with a new CV-style shaft.","description":"Non-Rubicon TJs (1997\u20132002 and 2003\u20132006 non-Rubi) came with a plain two-u-joint front driveshaft. It's a 1310-series shaft, cheap to service, and tolerant of small lifts. Rubicons got a 1330-series shaft with a CV (double-cardan) joint at the transfer case end \u2014 this lets the driveline run at steeper angles without vibration, which matters once you lift the truck or run a Slip Yoke Eliminator on the rear. The CV joint has two u-joints back-to-back plus a centering ball and spring inside the yoke. That centering ball is the wear part most people miss.\n\nSymptoms of a worn u-joint: a vibration that comes in at a specific speed range (usually 40\u201360 mph), a clunk on shift into 4WD, or visible rust dust at the cap edges. Symptoms of a collapsed centering yoke on a CV shaft: vibration at low speed and under load that doesn't track with engine RPM, and obvious play when you grab the shaft and rock it side-to-side at the t-case yoke. Don't replace just one u-joint on a CV shaft \u2014 do both u-joints and the centering yoke together. Once one is worn, the other isn't far behind, and the labor is identical.\n\nPlan the job around removing the shaft. The four straps on the axle pinion yoke and four bolts on the t-case flange come off with the shaft mostly in place. Mark the orientation of the shaft to both yokes before you pull it \u2014 re-installing 180\u00b0 out can introduce a vibration. Once on the bench, knock out the old u-joint caps with a socket and a vise (a u-joint press is faster), clean the yoke ears, and seat the new caps evenly. The CV centering yoke press-fits with a new spring; follow Denny's or Spicer's kit instructions on spring orientation.\n\nIf your TJ is sitting on 4\" or more of lift and a CV-style shaft isn't already there, the rebuild is a band-aid. A Tom Wood's custom CV front shaft drops in, ends the vibration permanently, and is built for the operating angle your suspension actually runs. $500-ish complete is cheaper than re-doing u-joints every two years on a non-CV shaft that's living past its design angle.","steps":["Lift the front of the truck, support on stands, place transmission in neutral.","Mark the orientation of the shaft to both the axle pinion yoke and t-case flange with a paint marker.","Remove the four 12mm strap bolts at the axle pinion. Catch the u-joint caps so they don't fall.","Remove the four 13mm flange bolts at the t-case end.","Pull the shaft off the truck, support the t-case yoke so it doesn't drop.","Bench-press old u-joints out of the shaft yokes.","On Rubicon CV shafts, pry the centering yoke out and replace with kit-included new yoke and spring.","Install new u-joints \u2014 caps go in evenly, snap rings seated, joint moves freely with no bind.","Grease the fittings until grease comes out all four cap seals.","Reinstall the shaft to match your alignment marks. Torque axle strap bolts to 15 ft-lbs (hand-tight on the straps), t-case flange bolts to 18\u201320 ft-lbs, large flange nuts to 160 ft-lbs if applicable."],"pros":["Rebuild is under $200 in parts","New u-joints solve most TJ vibration complaints","Greaseable Spicer u-joints last longer than sealed OE","CV shaft handles steeper lift angles"],"cons":["A non-CV shaft at 4\"+ lift will vibrate again \u2014 rebuilding is a delay, not a fix","CV centering yoke is fiddly to press without the right tools","Cheap u-joints fail fast \u2014 buy Spicer or Neapco, not no-name"]},{"id":"tj-locker-rear","title":"Rear Locker for the TJ \u2014 Chrysler 8.25\", Dana 35, and Dana 44","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":449,"high":999},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","bearing puller","pinion depth gauge","dial indicator","ring gear installation tool","RTV sealant"],"skills_required":["differential work","axle disassembly","bearing preload setting"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["locker","differential","traction","rear-axle","dana-35","chrysler-8-25"],"parts":[{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" (TJ Sport/Sahara rear) RD101","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":799},{"name":"Detroit Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" 80-1256-1","vendor":"Detroit Locker (Eaton)","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Yukon Grizzly Locker for Chrysler 8.25\" YG C8.25-27","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":549},{"name":"ARB Air Locker for Dana 44 (TJ Rubicon rear) RD100","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":849}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Rear Locker Options: Dana 35 vs 8.25 vs Dana 44","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/rear-locker-tj-options/"},{"title":"ARB Air Locker Chrysler 8.25 Installation Guide","url":"https://arb.com.au/arb-air-locker-instructions"},{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 Dana 35 Rear Axle Strength Discussion","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/dana-35-axle-strength/"}],"alternatives":["tj-regear","tj-skid-plates"],"summary":"If your TJ has a Dana 35 rear axle, address the axle's known weak points before adding a locker \u2014 a locked Dana 35 on aggressive terrain is asking the C-clips and side shafts to do something they weren't built for.","description":"The rear axle choice in the TJ is a meaningful build variable. Dana 35 units (base 1997\u20132002 models) use C-clip axle retention \u2014 when a side shaft breaks under load, the wheel can separate from the vehicle. The Dana 35's small ring gear and thin side shafts are the limiting factor. Adding a locker to a Dana 35 and running 35\" tires on technical terrain is a scenario where side shaft failure becomes a real probability, not a theoretical one. The correct answer is either upgrading to a Chrysler 8.25\" (a common axle swap) or installing a Chrome-Moly axle upgrade from Superior Axle before adding the locker.\n\nThe Chrysler 8.25\" rear axle (Sport and Sahara trim 1997\u20132006) is a substantially stronger unit \u2014 larger ring gear diameter, full-float flange (no C-clips), and a track record as a reliable trail rear end. Most serious TJ builds run the 8.25\" or swap to a Dana 44 (from a Rubicon or other source). For the 8.25\", the Detroit Locker ($499) is the value pick: a mechanical locker that engages automatically under torque differential and disengages on deceleration. On-trail behavior is predictable once you know what to expect \u2014 slight ratcheting on tight pavement turns is the trade-off for consistent engagement when one wheel lifts.\n\nThe ARB Air Locker ($799 for 8.25\") is the selectable option: locked when you want it, open diff when you don't. For a rig that mixes street driving and trail use, selectable is the correct choice \u2014 an always-on locker like the Detroit noticeably changes driving behavior on pavement, including oversteer tendency in corners. The ARB requires an air compressor and solenoid kit (sold separately, approximately $250); plan for the additional install time and wiring.\n\nRubicon owners with the factory Dana 44 rear already have a stronger axle foundation \u2014 the ARB Air Locker RD100 is the premium choice and pairs well with the Rubicon's factory ARB front air locker for a matched selectable system.","steps":["Drain the rear differential fluid completely.","Raise the rear axle on jack stands with both rear tires off the ground.","Remove both rear wheels and brake drums or calipers (set aside; do not disconnect brake lines).","Remove rear axle shafts \u2014 on the Chrysler 8.25\", remove the differential housing cover and retaining pin to release C-clips on Dana 35; on 8.25\" (full-float) remove the axle retaining plate bolts.","Remove the differential carrier assembly from the housing.","Press out existing spider gears and side gears; install locker unit per manufacturer instructions.","Reinstall carrier assembly with new bearing preload as specified. Set backlash to factory spec (0.005\"\u20130.008\" on Chrysler 8.25\").","For ARB Air Locker: route air line through axle housing per ARB instructions; connect to solenoid and compressor.","Install new diff cover gasket or apply RTV; torque cover bolts to 20\u201325 ft-lbs in a cross pattern.","Reinstall axle shafts and retaining hardware; torque to spec.","Refill with 80W-140 gear oil (Chrysler 8.25\": 2.5 pints); use friction modifier if TJ has a limited-slip OEM unit.","Reinstall brake hardware, wheels; torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs.","Test on dry pavement at low speed to verify engagement feel before trail use."],"pros":["Chrysler 8.25\" with a Detroit or ARB locker is a durable, capable rear end for moderate to aggressive trail use","ARB selectable design preserves on-road driving behavior \u2014 unlocked for streets, locked for trails","Detroit Locker provides automatic engagement with no compressor, wiring, or solenoid","Rubicon Dana 44 with ARB creates a matched selectable front/rear system","Dramatically improves traction on side-hills, loose terrain, and one-wheel-up obstacle lines"],"cons":["Dana 35 C-clip retention is a failure risk under locker loads \u2014 upgrade axle shafts or replace the axle before locking it","ARB Air Locker requires compressor, solenoid wiring, and air line routing \u2014 significant additional install complexity and cost","Detroit Locker causes noticeable handling changes on pavement \u2014 understeer in tight turns, potential for rear-end stepping in low-grip corners","Installation requires differential expertise \u2014 incorrect bearing preload or backlash will damage the ring and pinion","The locker alone does not compensate for regear needs \u2014 35\" tires still require 4.56 or 4.88 gears regardless of locking differential"]},{"id":"tj-np231-tj","title":"NP231 Transfer Case \u2014 Service and Weak Points","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":500},"tools_required":["13mm socket","drain pan","fluid pump","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid changes"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["transfer-case","sye","slip-yoke","fluid"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline ATF+4 (quart)","part_number":"VV324","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 (quart)","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":11},{"name":"JB Conversions Super Short SYE","vendor":"JB Conversions","price_estimate_usd":425},{"name":"Teraflex SYE kit","part_number":"2204000","vendor":"Teraflex","price_estimate_usd":295},{"name":"Rough Country SYE shaft","part_number":"50-7906","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":130}],"citations":[{"title":"NP231 fluid type \u2014 Wrangler Forum","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/what-fluid-and-quantity-for-np231-transfer-case.1284833/"},{"title":"JB Conversions NP231 SYE","url":"https://www.jbconversions.com/products/sye/np231j_std_sye.php"},{"title":"Teraflex SYE install \u2014 Quadratec","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/c/howto/how-install-teraflex-slip-yoke-eliminator-2wd-low-range-np231-transfer-case"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The NP231 in your TJ is one of the toughest chain-driven transfer cases ever built \u2014 the gears almost never fail, but the slip-yoke output is its one real weakness and lifted Jeeps need a slip-yoke eliminator.","description":"The NP231 (also called NP231J) is the transfer case bolted to nearly every TJ except the Rubicon. The planetary low range, drive sprockets, and chain are overbuilt \u2014 owners routinely run them past 200,000 miles with nothing but fluid changes. The original 27-spline rear output shaft is the one weak point, and the slip-yoke design at the rear output is the design choice that bites lifted Jeeps. Maintenance is short and cheap. The slip-yoke conversion is a decision, not a repair, and it matters once you go past 3 inches of lift.\n\nFor fluid service, the NP231 holds roughly 2.2 quarts of ATF. Mopar specifies ATF+4 and that is the right choice for warranty and longevity, but the case is genuinely not picky \u2014 Dexron, Mercon, or ATF+4 all work fine, and most TJ owners run ATF+4 because they already buy it for the transmission. Change the fluid every 30,000 miles or sooner if you wheel in water. Water in the case looks milky and is the fastest way to kill bearings.\n\nThe slip-yoke story is the part most TJ owners need to understand before they lift. The NP231 rear output is a long splined shaft that the driveshaft yoke slides onto and off of as the suspension articulates. Stock, it works. Lifted, the driveshaft angle steepens, the yoke loads off-center, and the rear output seal starts weeping. Worse, the longer effective driveshaft length means a shorter shaft, which amplifies vibration at highway speed. A slip-yoke eliminator (SYE) kit replaces the long splined output with a short fixed yoke, then pairs with a CV-style driveshaft. Once installed, the case stops leaking and the geometry tolerates 4\u20136 inches of lift without driveline vibration.\n\nSYE options range from budget to professional. The Rough Country shaft kit around $130 is the cheapest path and works if you do not need a new tail housing. The Teraflex 2204000 kit around $295 swaps the output shaft and tail housing \u2014 the most common mid-tier choice. The JB Conversions Super Short SYE around $425 is the strongest available and uses a 32-spline output that handles 35s and a built motor without flinching. Pair any SYE with a new front-bolt CV driveshaft (Tom Wood's, Adams) and call it done.","steps":["For fluid change: warm the case with a short drive, then position a drain pan under the transfer case.","Remove the 13mm fill plug first (top of case) to confirm it will come out, then remove the drain plug.","Let the case drain fully, then reinstall the drain plug torqued to 22 ft-lbs.","Pump fresh ATF+4 into the fill hole until it overflows. The case is level-full when fluid runs out.","Reinstall the fill plug torqued to 22 ft-lbs and wipe clean.","For SYE install: remove the rear driveshaft, support the case, drop the tail housing, swap to the SYE shaft and housing per kit instructions, refill with ATF+4.","Order a new CV-style rear driveshaft sized to the new yoke-to-pinion distance \u2014 measure with the suspension at ride height."],"pros":["Fluid change is 30 minutes and under $30","NP231 internals are nearly bulletproof \u2014 bearings and chain last 200k+","SYE eliminates the rear output leak and tames driveline vibration","JB and Teraflex kits both bolt on with standard hand tools"],"cons":["Slip-yoke design weeps under lift and angle","Original 27-spline output limits power-handling on heavily built rigs","SYE plus driveshaft is $400\u2013$700 all in","Water intrusion kills the chain bearings quickly"]},{"id":"tj-regear","title":"Re-Gearing the TJ \u2014 4.56, 4.88, and 5.13 Ratios","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":12},"cost_usd":{"low":700,"high":2200},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","pinion depth gauge","dial indicator","bearing press","INCH-lb torque wrench","socket set","RTV sealant","gear marking compound"],"skills_required":["differential work","bearing preload setting","ring and pinion installation","torque specification knowledge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["gears","regear","drivetrain","ring-and-pinion","axle"],"parts":[{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion Set for Dana 30 (TJ front) YG D30-488","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Yukon Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion Set for Chrysler 8.25\" (TJ rear) YG C8.25-488","vendor":"Yukon Gear & Axle","price_estimate_usd":209},{"name":"Rugged Ridge 4.56 Master Ring & Pinion Overhaul Kit (Dana 30 or 8.25\", includes bearings)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":329},{"name":"Nitro Gear 4.88 Ring & Pinion for Dana 30 G D30-488","vendor":"Nitro Gear","price_estimate_usd":175}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Re-Gear Guide: Choosing the Right Ratio","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/regear-guide-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Yukon Gear \u2014 Ring and Pinion Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.yukongear.com/installation-instructions"},{"title":"4WheelDrive Hardware \u2014 TJ Gear Ratio Calculator","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/gear-ratio-calculator"}],"alternatives":["tj-locker-rear","tj-tire-fitment-35s","tj-long-arm-lift"],"summary":"Stock TJ gear ratios were designed for 29\"\u201331\" tires \u2014 moving to 35s without re-gearing trades engine RPM at highway speed and kills the low-end torque that makes the 4.0L worth having off-road.","description":"The TJ's 4.0L inline-six makes its best torque between 2,000\u20133,500 RPM. Stock gear ratios (3.07 on 1997\u20132001 Dana 35 base models, 3.73 on most others) produce acceptable highway performance with 30\" tires because they keep the engine in that band at 65\u201370 mph. Put 35\" tires on a 3.73-geared TJ and you've effectively reduced the mechanical advantage by roughly 14%, which means the engine now has to rev higher to do the same work, low-range crawl ratio suffers, and the transmission is hunting for a lower gear on every hill. The 4.0L will tolerate it, but it will never feel right.\n\nGear ratio selection follows the tire diameter. For 35\" tires, 4.56 is the widely-used street/trail ratio \u2014 highway manners are restored and the crawl ratio improves significantly. For 35\" tires on a dedicated trail build, or for 37\" tires on any build, 4.88 is the better answer: more low-end authority for rock work and acceptable highway performance with the overdrive working harder. The 5.13 ratio is for highly modified rigs running 37\"\u201340\" tires with aggressive gearing strategies \u2014 it's not a street ratio and carries a real fuel economy penalty.\n\nFront and rear gears must match exactly. The TJ uses independent axle housings front and rear, and if you install different gear ratios in each axle, the transfer case will transfer torque between them every time 4WD is engaged, creating binding in the drivetrain. The common mistake is gearing one axle at a time for budget reasons \u2014 the result is a rig you can't use 4WD on safely until both axles match. Do both axles at the same time or be prepared to drive the first axle in 2WD-only until the second axle is geared.\n\nBudget for professional installation if you don't have differential experience. Ring and pinion installation requires setting bearing preload with an inch-pound torque wrench, measuring backlash with a dial indicator, and reading gear tooth contact patterns with marking compound. A misset pinion depth or incorrect preload will destroy the gears within 5,000\u201310,000 miles.","steps":["Drain both front and rear differentials completely.","Remove axle shafts from the axle to be geared (front requires removing the front driveshaft and disconnecting the front axle shafts; rear requires removing rear axle shafts).","Remove the differential carrier from the housing.","Remove the ring gear from the carrier; install new ring gear with new ring gear bolts. Torque ring gear bolts to 70\u201380 ft-lbs in a crossing pattern.","Press old pinion bearing races out of the axle housing; press new races in.","Install new pinion with correct shim stack for target depth (requires pinion depth gauge \u2014 measure, shim, check, repeat).","Set pinion bearing preload using crush sleeve or shim pack per manufacturer spec (rotating torque: 15\u201325 in-lbs on new bearings).","Install carrier with new side bearing shims; set backlash to 0.006\"\u20130.009\" (Dana 30) or 0.005\"\u20130.008\" (Chrysler 8.25\").","Check tooth contact pattern with gear marking compound \u2014 pattern should be centered on the drive face of each tooth.","Install new diff cover gasket or RTV; torque cover bolts to 25 ft-lbs.","Refill with correct gear oil: 80W-90 synthetic (Dana 30), 80W-140 synthetic (Chrysler 8.25\").","Repeat for second axle.","Drive 500 miles before re-checking fluid for metal particles indicating break-in wear."],"pros":["Restores highway drivability and proper low-end torque after moving to 35\" tires","4.88 gears with 35s produce a crawl ratio (with the NP231 4:1 low range) of approximately 87:1 \u2014 genuinely capable for rock work","Matched front/rear gear sets are required for safe 4WD operation \u2014 this upgrade is non-negotiable for 35\" tire builds","Yukon and Nitro gear sets are widely available with documented fitment for TJ axles","Improves fuel economy compared to stock gears + oversized tires by returning the engine to its efficient RPM range"],"cons":["Installation requires differential expertise and specialized tools \u2014 incorrect installation damages gears within miles","Both axles must be geared simultaneously or 4WD cannot be used safely","Full cost including labor at a shop runs $1,500\u2013$2,200 for both axles","Gear break-in requires 500 miles of varied driving before hard off-road use","Chrysler 8.25\" limited-slip owners must verify the locker/LSD choice is compatible with the new gear set's side bearing spec"]},{"id":"tj-sye-kit","title":"SYE Kit for the NP231 Transfer Case \u2014 Eliminating Driveshaft Vibration","vehicle":"tj","category":"drivetrain","type":"part","difficulty":4,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":599},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","snap ring pliers","bearing press","drain pan","transfer case oil"],"skills_required":["drivetrain work","transfer case disassembly","driveshaft installation"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["sye","transfer-case","driveshaft","np231","vibration"],"parts":[{"name":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts SYE Kit for NP231 (TW SYE-231)","vendor":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Advance Adapters SYE Kit for NP231 (AA-241630)","vendor":"Advance Adapters","price_estimate_usd":279},{"name":"Rubicon Express SYE Kit for NP231 (RE3530)","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":299},{"name":"Tom Wood's Custom CV Rear Driveshaft for TJ (custom-built to spec after SYE install)","vendor":"Tom Wood's Custom Driveshafts","price_estimate_usd":250}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 NP231 SYE Install Guide with Photos","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/np231-sye-install-guide/"},{"title":"Tom Wood's Driveshafts TJ SYE Overview","url":"https://www.tomwoodsdriveshaft.com/sye"},{"title":"Advance Adapters SYE for NP231 Instructions","url":"https://www.advanceadapters.com/sye-np231-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-regear"],"summary":"Above 2.5\" of lift, the NP231's slip-yoke rear output causes driveshaft vibration at highway speed that makes the TJ unpleasant to drive \u2014 an SYE kit and CV rear driveshaft eliminates it.","description":"The NP231 transfer case uses a slip-yoke output at the rear \u2014 a design where the yoke slides in and out of the output shaft as the driveshaft plunges and extends with suspension travel. At stock ride height, this works fine. Lift the vehicle 2.5\"\u20134\" and the rear driveshaft's operating angle steepens beyond what the slip yoke and standard U-joint can absorb without vibration. At highway speed, the resulting vibration feels like a tire imbalance or a failing u-joint \u2014 a buzz or thump that comes on around 45\u201355 mph and worsens as speed increases.\n\nThe SYE (Slip Yoke Eliminator) kit replaces the slip yoke output with a fixed-yoke flange. This requires fitting a new rear output shaft with a fixed-yoke companion flange, which then accepts a custom-length CV (constant velocity) rear driveshaft. The CV driveshaft uses a double-cardan joint at the transfer case end that allows steeper operating angles without the velocity variation that causes vibration with a single U-joint. The result is a smooth, vibration-free rear driveshaft at any legal highway speed regardless of lift height.\n\nTom Wood's SYE kit is the benchmark. Tom Wood's manufactures both the SYE hardware and the companion CV driveshaft, so the entire system is matched and tested. They build custom driveshafts to measured length after the SYE is installed \u2014 send them your measurements and they build the shaft to spec. Advance Adapters' SYE ($279) is a reliable, lower-cost alternative that uses the same design principle and works with any quality rear CV shaft.\n\nThe Rubicon's NP242 (Select-Trac) transfer case is a different unit with a different output configuration. This SYE kit does not fit the NP242. Rubicon owners with the NP241OR have their own SYE solutions, but the NP241OR is less commonly used outside Rubicon trim and the aftermarket solutions are more limited.","steps":["Drain the transfer case fluid completely.","Disconnect the rear driveshaft from the transfer case output yoke (4 bolts at the output flange or U-joint strap).","Remove the transfer case rear output housing bolts (typically 6 bolts around the rear face of the transfer case).","Slide the OEM slip yoke output assembly out of the transfer case rear housing.","Install the SYE's new output shaft into the rear housing per kit instructions. Verify the output shaft engages the chain drive and rotates freely.","Install new output housing (included in SYE kit) with fresh gasket or RTV. Torque housing bolts to 35\u201340 ft-lbs.","Install the new fixed-yoke companion flange onto the SYE output shaft; torque the retaining nut to 150\u2013175 ft-lbs.","Measure the rear driveshaft distance from the SYE flange to the pinion flange with suspension at ride height. Order a custom CV rear driveshaft to this length (Tom Wood's, Adams Driveshafts, or similar).","Reinstall rear driveshaft to the SYE flange and pinion. Torque U-bolt straps to 15\u201320 ft-lbs.","Refill transfer case with ATF+4 automatic transmission fluid (NP231 capacity: approximately 1.4 pints).","Test drive on a highway \u2014 vibration should be eliminated. If any vibration remains, verify pinion angle (target: 1\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 downward from horizontal at ride height)."],"pros":["Completely eliminates highway-speed driveshaft vibration that is otherwise unavoidable above 2.5\" of lift","Fixed-yoke output is stronger and more reliable than the OEM slip yoke under trail torque loads","Tom Wood's matched SYE + CV driveshaft is a proven system with years of TJ trail history","Bolt-in install on the NP231 requires no special machining or permanent modifications to the transfer case housing","CV driveshaft can accommodate additional lift in the future without needing replacement (within the joint's angle limits)"],"cons":["Does not fit the NP242 (Sahara Select-Trac) \u2014 verify your transfer case before purchasing","Custom rear driveshaft must be ordered after SYE install and measured correctly \u2014 plan for lead time on the driveshaft","Transfer case must be fully drained and refilled \u2014 use ATF+4, not gear oil","Total system cost (SYE + CV driveshaft) runs $550\u2013$650 when both are purchased together \u2014 budget accordingly when planning a mid-lift or long-arm install","Incorrect pinion angle after installation will cause a different type of vibration \u2014 verify angle with a digital angle finder before closing out the install"]},{"id":"tj-alternator-replacement","title":"TJ Alternator Replacement \u2014 Symptoms, Testing, and Install","vehicle":"tj","category":"electrical","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":85,"high":220},"tools_required":["multimeter","10mm socket","13mm socket","serpentine belt tool or breaker bar","pry bar or tensioner tool"],"skills_required":["basic electrical testing","belt removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["electrical","alternator","charging","battery"],"parts":[{"name":"Remy 94554 remanufactured alternator \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Remy","price_estimate_usd":95},{"name":"Bosch AL7520X remanufactured alternator","vendor":"Bosch","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"DB Electrical new alternator ADR0399","vendor":"DB Electrical","price_estimate_usd":88},{"name":"Duralast Gold remanufactured alternator","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":105}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Test an Alternator","url":"https://www.autozone.com/diy/charging-system/how-to-test-an-alternator"},{"title":"TJ Wrangler electrical troubleshooting","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A TJ alternator going bad shows up first as a battery warning light or dim headlights at idle. Confirm with a multimeter before replacing \u2014 it's a 90-minute swap, but don't replace the alternator if the battery is actually the problem.","description":"The 4.0L TJ runs a 90-amp or 117-amp alternator depending on year and trim \u2014 accessible on the passenger side of the engine, driven by the serpentine belt. Failure mode is typically gradual: the internal voltage regulator begins undercharging before it quits entirely. You'll see the battery light flicker on the dash, or notice the headlights dimming slightly at idle while the stereo is running. Hard failure is less common but happens \u2014 usually a brushes or diode pack failure.\n\nTest before replacing: a healthy charging system measures 13.8 to 14.7V at the battery with the engine at idle. Below 13.5V means the alternator isn't keeping up with demand. Above 15.5V means the regulator is overcharging, which kills batteries fast. AutoZone and O'Reilly will test your alternator for free with a load tester if you'd rather have a second opinion.\n\nRemanufactured alternators are the common replacement path \u2014 typically $90 to $120 with a core exchange. New units from DB Electrical run similar money and don't require a core. Avoid the cheapest no-name units on Amazon \u2014 TJ alternators have a reputation for failing prematurely on undersized knockoffs.\n\n- Replacement alternator (confirm amp rating matches your build \u2014 winches and aux lighting may warrant upgrading to the higher-output unit)\n- 10mm and 13mm sockets\n- Serpentine belt tensioner tool or 1/2\" breaker bar (the tensioner is a square drive)\n- Multimeter for pre- and post-install verification\n\n1. **Disconnect the negative battery terminal first.** Not optional \u2014 you're working next to live wiring.\n\n2. **Relieve serpentine belt tension.** Insert a breaker bar or tensioner tool into the square hole on the belt tensioner pulley. Rotate to release tension and slip the belt off the alternator pulley.\n\n3. **Disconnect wiring.** One large main power wire (10mm nut) and one multi-pin plug. Take a photo first.\n\n4. **Remove mounting bolts.** Two or three bolts depending on year \u2014 the TJ uses a pivot bolt at the bottom and an adjustment bolt at the top (or an automatic tensioner on later models). Remove all bolts and lift the alternator out. It weighs about 10 lb.\n\n5. **Compare old to new.** Mounting tabs, pulley diameter, and wire connector location should match. Most TJ alternators are direct-fit.\n\n6. **Install new alternator.** Thread mounting bolts by hand before tightening any of them. Torque mounting bolts to 30 ft-lb.\n\n7. **Reconnect wiring.** Power wire gets torqued snug (don't over-torque \u2014 these studs strip). Multi-pin plug clicks in.\n\n8. **Reinstall belt.** Route belt per the sticker on the radiator support. Release tensioner onto belt. Verify belt is seated in all pulley grooves.\n\n9. **Reconnect negative battery. Start engine.** Verify 13.8\u201314.7V at battery with engine at idle and accessories on.\n\n- If your TJ is running a winch, extra lighting, or other high-draw accessories, this is a reasonable time to upgrade to the 117-amp unit even if you have the 90-amp original. The difference in cost is minimal at time of replacement.\n- The serpentine belt may need replacement if it's original or showing cracks \u2014 you're already in there.\n- A new alternator that reads below 13.8V at install likely has a bad voltage regulator or is the wrong unit. Return it \u2014 don't let it sit and drain the battery.\n\nRemanufactured alternator: $90\u2013$125 with core exchange. New unit: $88\u2013$140. Belt if replacing at same time: $25\u2013$35. Total parts for a full charging service: $115\u2013$160. Plan 90 minutes including testing."},{"id":"tj-battery-upgrade","title":"TJ Battery Replacement and Upgrade Guide","vehicle":"tj","category":"electrical","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":250},"tools_required":["10mm socket or wrench","5/16\" nut driver or socket","wire brush","battery terminal cleaner"],"skills_required":["basic electrical","basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["battery","electrical","winch","starting"],"parts":[{"name":"Optima RedTop 34R (OEM replacement)","part_number":"8002-002","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"ACDelco 34 Professional (budget OEM fit)","part_number":"34P","vendor":"ACDelco","price_estimate_usd":120},{"name":"Odyssey PC1500 (dual-purpose starting/deep cycle)","part_number":"PC1500","vendor":"Odyssey","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Optima YellowTop D34 (if running accessories or winch)","part_number":"8040-218","vendor":"Optima","price_estimate_usd":265}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Battery Group Size and Location \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/battery-replacement-guide-tj.html"},{"title":"Optima RedTop vs YellowTop \u2014 What's the Difference","url":"https://www.optimabatteries.com/learn/redtop-vs-yellowtop"}],"alternatives":["tj-alternator-replacement","tj-wiring-upgrades","tj-winch-install"],"summary":"The TJ takes a Group 34 battery. Stock starting is fine with a standard AGM replacement; if you run a winch or significant accessory load, move up to a YellowTop or Odyssey PC1500 for deep-cycle capability.","description":"The TJ Wrangler battery tray sits in the engine bay on the driver's side. The factory battery is a Group 34 \u2014 that's the spec you need for a drop-in fit with no bracket modification. Group 34 is also available in AGM (absorbed glass mat) versions from Optima, Odyssey, and ACDelco, all of which handle vibration better than conventional flooded batteries \u2014 relevant for any Jeep that sees real trail use.\n\nThe standard RedTop is a starting battery: high cold-cranking amps, not optimized for repeated deep discharge. If you run a winch, auxiliary lights, a fridge, or a radio, the YellowTop or Odyssey PC1500 handles both starting and moderate accessory load. The Odyssey PC1500 is the most capable option in a stock tray but costs more than the Optima line.\n\nBattery life on TJs is typically 4\u20136 years. If the engine turns over slowly on cold mornings or the battery tests below 12.4V after sitting overnight, it's time.\n\n- Reversed polarity will fry the PCM. Double-check which terminal is positive before connecting.\n- The TJ's PCM may lose radio presets, clock settings, and idle trim after a battery disconnect. The idle can feel rough for the first 10\u201320 minutes while the PCM relearns. Don't be alarmed \u2014 it normalizes.\n- If the engine still cranks slowly after a new battery, suspect the alternator or battery cables. A voltage drop test across each cable (meter on each end, engine cranking) will identify high-resistance connections.\n- Vent caps on flooded batteries need to be accessible. AGM and gel batteries don't vent, so bracket orientation matters less with those.\n\n- ACDelco 34 conventional or AGM: $90\u2013$140 at AutoZone, O'Reilly, or Amazon\n- Optima RedTop 34R: $200\u2013$230 at most auto parts stores or Amazon\n- Optima YellowTop D34: $250\u2013$275\n- Odyssey PC1500: $240\u2013$270\n\nThe Optima line is widely available same-day at major chains; the Odyssey typically ships 2\u20133 days from Batteries Plus or online.","steps":["Disconnect the negative terminal first (10mm). Tuck the cable away so it can't touch the terminal during the swap.","Disconnect the positive terminal (10mm).","Remove the battery hold-down bracket. On the TJ it's a J-hook style clamp secured with one 10mm bolt at the base.","Lift the battery straight out. Stock batteries weigh 38\u201345 lbs; AGM batteries can run heavier (the Odyssey PC1500 is 55 lbs).","Inspect the battery tray for corrosion. Clean with a wire brush and a baking soda + water solution if needed. Let dry.","Clean the terminal clamps with a terminal cleaner or fine wire brush. Corrosion here is a real voltage drop \u2014 don't skip it.","Set the new battery in the tray, terminals oriented the same as the original.","Reinstall the hold-down bracket. It should be snug \u2014 a loose battery will damage the case on rough terrain.","Connect positive first, then negative. Don't overtighten \u2014 terminal clamps crack if you crank on them.","Apply terminal grease or anti-corrosion pads to both terminals.","Start the engine and verify charging voltage with a multimeter: 13.5\u201314.5V at idle is correct."]},{"id":"tj-oil-change","title":"TJ 4.0L Oil Change \u2014 Specs, Interval, and What to Use","vehicle":"tj","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":25,"high":55},"tools_required":["14mm box wrench or socket","oil filter wrench (optional)","drain pan","funnel","rags","torque wrench (optional but recommended)"],"skills_required":["basic maintenance"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["engine","oil","maintenance","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM oil filter","part_number":"04884899AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Wix 51515 oil filter","part_number":"51515","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"Mobil 1 5W-30 full synthetic (5 qt)","vendor":"Mobil 1","price_estimate_usd":32},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife 10W-30 high-mileage (5 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":24}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ 4.0L Engine Oil Specification \u2014 Chrysler Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/oil-specs-for-4-0.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler TJ Oil Drain Plug Torque","url":"https://www.fcpeuro.com/torque-specs/jeep-wrangler-oil-drain-plug"}],"alternatives":["tj-coolant-flush","tj-air-filter-replacement","tj-transmission-fluid-change"],"summary":"The TJ 4.0L takes 6 quarts of 10W-30 (factory spec) or 5W-30 for colder climates. Change it every 3,000\u20135,000 miles with conventional oil or every 5,000\u20137,500 miles with full synthetic.","description":"The AMC-derived 4.0L inline-six has been running since 1987 and it rewards consistent oil changes. The factory spec is 10W-30 conventional, but Jeep engineers used 10W-30 when most of their production went south of the Mason-Dixon line. For cold weather or extended intervals, 5W-30 full synthetic is a legitimate upgrade \u2014 it flows faster on cold starts, which is where most wear happens.\n\nCapacity is 6 quarts with a filter change. The drain plug is on the passenger side of the oil pan, recessed toward the rear. The filter is on the driver's side of the block near the front \u2014 easy to reach. A filter wrench makes the initial break loose easier but hand-tight is the correct install torque on most spin-on filters.\n\nHigh-mileage oil (100,000+ miles) makes sense if the engine is running dry between changes or if you're seeing minor seepage at the valve cover. The seal conditioners in high-mileage blends reduce seepage noticeably. Don't switch to high-mileage expecting it to fix a real leak \u2014 it won't.\n\n- 25 ft-lb on the drain plug is firm but not aggressive. The TJ oil pan threads strip if you gorilla it. If you feel it getting tight faster than expected, stop and check alignment.\n- A dry filter gasket can cause a slow oil leak that doesn't show up until the first heat cycle. Always oil the gasket.\n- Brown sludge on the dipstick or inside the fill cap means the oil is emulsified \u2014 usually a head gasket leak introducing coolant, or oil going too long between changes. Address the source before just changing the oil.\n- New filter spin-on gaskets should seat against clean metal. Old gasket residue left on the block causes a double-gasket situation and guaranteed leak.\n\n- Wix 51515 filter: $7\u2013$9 at NAPA or Amazon\n- Conventional 10W-30 (6 quarts): $18\u2013$24\n- Full synthetic 5W-30 (6 quarts): $30\u2013$40\n- Drain plug (replace if threads look rough): $5\u2013$8 at any parts store","steps":["Warm the engine for 3\u20135 minutes, then shut off. Warm oil drains faster and carries more suspended contaminants.","Set the Jeep on level ground. Raise and support on jack stands if needed to access the drain plug.","Place drain pan under the passenger-side drain plug.","Remove the drain plug (14mm). Let fully drain \u2014 3\u20135 minutes.","While draining: locate the oil filter on the driver's side of the block. Position drain pan under it.","Remove the oil filter with a wrench or by hand. Some oil will spill \u2014 the pan should catch it.","Wipe the drain plug threads and inspect. The magnetic drain plug (if equipped) will have gray metallic paste \u2014 that's normal wear debris. Wipe clean.","Reinstall drain plug and torque to 25 ft-lb. Don't over-torque \u2014 that's a frequent cause of stripped oil pans on TJs.","Lightly coat the new filter's gasket with fresh oil. Thread on hand-tight, then 3/4 turn by hand. No wrench at install.","Add 6 quarts of oil through the valve cover fill cap. Check dipstick after adding 5.5 quarts \u2014 different filter brands hold slightly different amounts.","Start engine, let run 30\u201360 seconds. Check for leaks at the drain plug and filter.","Shut off, wait 2\u20133 minutes, check oil level on dipstick. Correct to full.","Reset oil change interval if using an oil life monitor."]},{"id":"tj-spark-plugs","title":"TJ 4.0L Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 Interval, Gap, and Which Plugs to Buy","vehicle":"tj","category":"engine","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":55},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket (with rubber insert)","3/8\" ratchet","6\" and 3\" extensions","torque wrench","plug gap tool","anti-seize compound"],"skills_required":["basic engine work"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["engine","ignition","tune-up","maintenance","fuel-economy"],"parts":[{"name":"NGK V-Power copper plugs \u2014 TJ 4.0 (set of 6)","part_number":"ZFR6F-11","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"Champion Double Platinum plugs (extended interval)","part_number":"7436","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":28},{"name":"Bosch Platinum+4 plugs (4.0L spec)","vendor":"Bosch","price_estimate_usd":35},{"name":"Denso Iridium plugs \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Denso","price_estimate_usd":48}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep TJ 4.0L tune-up specs","url":"https://www.jeepspecs.com/tj-wrangler-specs/"},{"title":"NGK spark plug gap guide","url":"https://www.ngksparkplugs.com/en/support/spark-plugs/gap"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 4.0L likes copper plugs changed every 30,000 miles or platinum every 50,000 \u2014 gapped to 0.035\". Most TJs that run rough at idle have fouled or worn plugs as the first thing to check.","description":"The AMC/Chrysler 4.0L inline-six is a robust engine with a straightforward ignition system: six plug wires, six conventional plugs, one distributor. No coil-on-plug complexity. Plug replacement is a genuine 45-minute job even on the first attempt.\n\nThe 4.0L specifies a gap of 0.035 inches. Stock NGK copper plugs are the benchmark \u2014 they run well, cost under $20 for a set of six, and gapping them fresh out of the box is recommended even if the package says \"pre-gapped.\" Heat cycles cause plugs to drift. The Chrysler service interval for copper plugs is 30,000 miles; platinum or iridium plugs extend that to 50,000 to 60,000 miles. For a daily driver, copper at 30k is a perfectly reasonable maintenance plan. For a rig that gets less use, platinum or iridium makes sense \u2014 less chance of them sitting for years and seizing into the head.\n\nOne consistent TJ problem: the fourth and fifth plug wires deteriorate from heat exposure and crack internally. Rough idle or a misfire code on cylinder 4 or 5 is often the plug wire, not the plug. Replace wires at the same time if they're more than 8 years old.\n\n- 5/8\" spark plug socket with rubber insert (the insert holds the plug for extraction \u2014 a regular socket lets it fall)\n- 3/8\" ratchet with 6\" and 3\" extensions\n- Torque wrench\n- Spark plug gap gauge\n- Anti-seize compound (thin coat on threads \u2014 prevents seizing into the aluminum head)\n- Dielectric grease for the plug boot ends\n\n1. **Work on a cold engine.** Plug threads are much harder to start in aluminum when the head is hot.\n\n2. **Remove plug wires one at a time.** Twist the boot 90 degrees before pulling \u2014 don't yank straight off. Lay the wire aside in order so you don't mix up cylinder positions.\n\n3. **Blow compressed air into the plug well before removal.** Debris in the well goes straight into the cylinder once the plug is out.\n\n4. **Remove plug with socket and ratchet.** If a plug feels tight, add penetrant and wait 15 minutes rather than forcing it. Stripped threads in an aluminum head are an expensive repair.\n\n5. **Check the old plugs.** Tan or gray electrode = healthy. Black and sooty = rich mixture or oil burning. White or blistered = running lean or overheating. Worn electrode (rounded, large gap) = past interval.\n\n6. **Gap new plugs to 0.035\".** Use the gap tool to check \u2014 adjust by bending the ground electrode (outer tip) only. Never adjust the center electrode.\n\n7. **Apply thin coat of anti-seize to plug threads.** Not needed with copper plugs in steel heads, but the 4.0 has an aluminum head \u2014 anti-seize prevents galling.\n\n8. **Thread plugs in by hand first.** Any binding means cross-threaded \u2014 back out and re-start. Torque to 18 ft-lb. No torque wrench? Snug + 1/4 turn after seating on a new gasket.\n\n9. **Apply dielectric grease inside the plug boot.** Reinstall wire. Press until it clicks.\n\n10. **Start engine and verify no misfires.** Check for codes with an OBD-II scanner if any misfires are present.\n\n- Plug 6 (rear-most) is the tightest access point on the 4.0. Use a universal joint on the extension if needed. A wobble extension makes it manageable.\n- If any plug comes out with oil on the threads or the boot end, you likely have a valve cover gasket leak getting into the plug wells. That's a separate repair, but worth knowing.\n- Don't over-torque into the aluminum head. 18 ft-lb is the spec \u2014 plugs feel \"not that tight\" at 18 ft-lb, which is correct.\n\nNGK copper plug set (6): $15\u2013$20. Denso iridium set: $45\u2013$55. Plug wire set if replacing: $30\u2013$50 (NGK or Accel). Full tune-up parts (plugs + wires): $50\u2013$80. One of the better dollar-per-result maintenance jobs on the 4.0."},{"id":"tj-fuel-filter-replacement","title":"TJ Fuel Filter Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1.5},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["fuel line disconnect tool set (spring-lock)","flathead screwdriver","drain pan or rags","safety glasses","gloves"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","comfort working with fuel lines"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["fuel","maintenance","filter"],"parts":[{"name":"Wix 33481 fuel filter \u2014 TJ 4.0L","part_number":"33481","vendor":"Wix","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Purolator F54631 fuel filter","part_number":"F54631","vendor":"Purolator","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"Motorcraft FG891 fuel filter (Ford equivalent, same Bosch filter)","part_number":"FG891","vendor":"Motorcraft","price_estimate_usd":15}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Fuel Filter Location and Replacement \u2014 WranglerTJForum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/fuel-filter-location-replacement.html"},{"title":"Jeep Wrangler Fuel Filter Replacement Guide \u2014 RockAuto Tech","url":"https://www.rockauto.com/en/catalog/jeep,1997,wrangler,4.0l+l6,1228898,fuel+&+air,fuel+filter"}],"alternatives":["tj-fuel-pump-replacement","tj-engine-oil-change","tj-air-filter-replacement"],"summary":"The TJ fuel filter is under the Jeep on the driver's side frame rail, about midway between the front and rear axles. It uses spring-lock quick-connect fittings \u2014 you need the right disconnect tool or the job becomes a wrestling match with flammable fuel spraying everywhere.","description":"Chrysler recommends replacing the TJ fuel filter every 30,000 miles but that interval assumes the Jeep has been fueled at reputable stations without water intrusion. If you've run off-road with the fuel cap seal compromised or fueled in areas with questionable fuel quality, every 20,000 miles is more conservative and appropriate. A clogged fuel filter shows up as hesitation under load, hard starting when hot, or a consistent lean code (P0171) that the O2 sensor keeps calling out.\n\nThe job is rated difficulty 2 because of the fuel pressure. You're not doing hard mechanical work \u2014 you're working with fuel-pressurized lines that will spray when disconnected. Eye protection is non-negotiable. Work with the engine cold (at least 30 minutes after shutdown) and have rags under the filter before you disconnect anything.\n\nSpring-lock disconnect tools are sold as a set for around $8\u2013$12 at any auto parts store. The TJ uses two sizes. Without the right tool, you'll squeeze the retaining tabs unevenly and they'll break before releasing.\n\n- Fuel line fittings that don't click fully are a fire hazard. Tug test every connection before starting.\n- If a quick-connect fitting looks cracked or the locking tabs are deformed, replace the fuel line section \u2014 don't risk it.\n- Residual fuel in the old filter will drain when you remove it. The rag or pan is not optional.\n- The replacement filter should have rubber end caps on the fittings during shipping. Don't remove them until you're ready to connect \u2014 dirt in a fuel filter defeats the purpose.\n\n- Wix 33481 or equivalent: $10\u2013$15 at NAPA, O'Reilly, or RockAuto\n- Spring-lock disconnect tool set: $8\u2013$12 at any auto parts store (keep them \u2014 you'll use them again)\n- Total job cost: $20\u2013$30","steps":["Relieve fuel pressure: remove the fuel pump fuse (check the under-hood fuse box legend), crank the engine until it stalls, then crank again 2\u20133 times to bleed residual pressure. Or: remove the fuel cap to equalize tank pressure, then proceed carefully \u2014 there will still be line pressure.","Put on safety glasses and gloves.","Raise and support the Jeep on jack stands \u2014 the filter is on the frame rail about midway down the driver's side.","Place a rag or small drain pan under the filter.","Note the flow direction arrow on the filter. The replacement must be installed in the same orientation.","Use the spring-lock disconnect tool on the inlet line (fuel coming from the tank, rear fitting). Insert the tool into the fitting, push in slightly, and pull the line off.","Repeat for the outlet line (toward the engine).","Loosen the bracket clamp bolt (typically 10mm) holding the filter to the frame rail.","Slide the old filter out of the bracket.","Compare old and new filters \u2014 confirm the new one is the same diameter and fitting size.","Slide the new filter into the bracket with the flow arrow pointing toward the engine.","Tighten the bracket clamp snugly \u2014 not gorilla-tight; you don't want to crush the filter body.","Reconnect the fuel lines. Push each line onto the fitting until you feel and hear the retaining clip snap. Tug firmly on the line to confirm it's locked.","Reinstall the fuel pump fuse.","Turn the key to ON (don't crank) for 5 seconds, then back to OFF. Repeat 3 times to prime the system and build pressure before starting.","Start the engine and check for leaks at both fittings. Let the engine idle 2\u20133 minutes with the Jeep on stands and inspect."]},{"id":"tj-throttle-body-cleaning","title":"TJ 4.0L Throttle Body Cleaning \u2014 Idle Issues and Carbon Buildup","vehicle":"tj","category":"fuel","type":"procedure","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":8,"high":15},"tools_required":["Phillips screwdriver","10mm socket","shop towels or lint-free rags","soft brush (old toothbrush works)"],"skills_required":["basic engine access"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["fuel","throttle-body","idle","tune-up","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"CRC Throttle Body and Air Intake Cleaner","vendor":"CRC","price_estimate_usd":10},{"name":"WD-40 Specialist Contact Cleaner (alternative)","vendor":"WD-40","price_estimate_usd":9}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Clean a Throttle Body","url":"https://www.popularmechanics.com/cars/how-to/a26303/how-to-clean-your-throttle-body/"},{"title":"TJ idle surge troubleshooting","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/engine-tech-12/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Rough cold idle, idle surge when warm, or a stumble off-throttle on a TJ 4.0 that isn't throwing codes \u2014 clean the throttle body first. It takes 30 minutes and costs $10, and it fixes the problem more often than not.","description":"The TJ 4.0 draws air through a single-bore throttle body mounted between the air intake tube and the intake manifold. Over years of use, oil vapor from the PCV system coats the bore with a sticky carbon and oil residue. This layer builds up on the throttle plate (the butterfly valve) and the bore walls behind it, progressively reducing the air passage at closed throttle \u2014 which is exactly where idle quality lives.\n\nSymptoms: hunting idle that cycles between 500 and 900 RPM, a stumble when you first crack the throttle from a stop, rough cold start that smooths out after 30 seconds, or the occasional stall when you drop back to idle after highway driving. None of these are serious, and none of them require a new throttle body \u2014 cleaning resolves it in the vast majority of cases.\n\nThe 4.0 throttle body has no IAC (idle air control) solenoid to clean separately \u2014 idle control is managed through the throttle plate position and the PCM. What you're cleaning is just the bore and plate.\n\n- Throttle body cleaner spray ($8\u2013$10 \u2014 CRC or equivalent)\n- Shop rags or microfiber cloths\n- Soft brush\n- Flathead screwdriver or trim tool to prop the throttle plate open during cleaning\n\n1. **Engine cold or warm is fine for this job \u2014 not hot.** Let it sit 15 minutes after driving.\n\n2. **Locate the throttle body.** It's the oval housing where the large air intake tube meets the intake manifold. Two hose clamps hold the intake tube to it.\n\n3. **Loosen the intake tube clamp** at the throttle body and pull the tube aside enough to access the bore opening. You don't need to remove the throttle body from the engine.\n\n4. **Prop the throttle plate open.** You can use your finger, a flat-blade screwdriver held at the throttle linkage, or have a helper hold it. The plate is the round disc inside the bore.\n\n5. **Spray the bore walls and plate with throttle body cleaner.** Let it soak for 30 seconds. The cleaner dissolves the carbon deposits.\n\n6. **Wipe with a lint-free cloth, scrub stubborn buildup with a soft brush.** Repeat until the bore walls are clean metal and the plate surface is residue-free.\n\n7. **Wipe dry.** No cleaner residue should remain.\n\n8. **Reconnect the intake tube.** Tighten the clamp.\n\n9. **Start the engine.** Expect rough idle for the first 30\u201360 seconds while the PCM relearns idle. Let it stabilize.\n\n10. **Drive a short cycle.** The idle should settle back to smooth within one warmup cycle.\n\n- Don't use carburetor cleaner on a throttle body with an IAC port or oxygen sensor \u2014 the stronger solvent can damage sensor components. Throttle body cleaner is specifically formulated to be safe for these surfaces.\n- If the idle issues persist after cleaning, the IAT sensor (located in the intake tube) or a vacuum leak is the next place to look.\n- This is not the fix for codes P0505 or P0506 (IAC circuit codes). Those require IAC testing or replacement. Throttle body cleaning addresses mechanical carbon buildup, not electronic sensor failures.\n\nCRC throttle body cleaner: $8\u2013$12. Total job cost: under $15 and 30 minutes. Probably the best diagnostic step before spending money on anything fuel or idle related on a high-mileage TJ."},{"id":"tj-seat-upgrade","title":"TJ Seat Upgrade \u2014 Options, Fitment, and What's Worth It","vehicle":"tj","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":800},"tools_required":["13mm socket","15mm socket","ratchet","T45 Torx (some brackets)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["interior","seats","comfort","daily-driver"],"parts":[{"name":"Corbeau Baja RS bucket seat (single)","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":330},{"name":"Smittybilt Neoprene XRC seat (pair)","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":350},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Replacement seat \u2014 driver","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Corbeau TJ/LJ direct-fit seat bracket kit","vendor":"Corbeau","price_estimate_usd":120}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Seat Replacement and Upgrade Options \u2014 JeepForum","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/threads/seat-replacement-options-tj.html"},{"title":"Corbeau Seat Fitment for TJ Wrangler","url":"https://www.corbeau.com/fitment/jeep/wrangler"}],"alternatives":["tj-carpet-replacement","tj-center-console-tj","tj-door-trim"],"summary":"TJ factory seats are marginal \u2014 low bolstering and aging foam are the main complaints. Corbeau and Smittybilt make direct-fit replacements. Budget $300\u2013$700 for a pair depending on whether you need new brackets.","description":"The Wrangler TJ's factory seats were designed for a $20,000 truck in 1997. They do the job on a grocery run. After a day of trail driving \u2014 constant bracing, lateral loads, and climbing in and out over rocks \u2014 the lack of bolstering and worn foam become obvious. If you drive the Jeep regularly, a seat upgrade is one of the higher-value interior investments.\n\nAftermarket TJ seats fall into three categories:\n\n**OEM-style replacements** (Rugged Ridge, Bestop, Smittybilt CAMO): Drop in on the factory tracks. Vinyl or neoprene fabric, shaped like stock seats but with new foam. Best for: replacing worn originals without changing the look. $150\u2013$200 per seat.\n\n**Bucket seats with direct-fit brackets** (Corbeau, Mastercraft, PRP): These are reclining bucket seats designed for off-road use. Higher bolstering, adjustable lumbar, and real harness slots if you're running a 4-point. Corbeau makes a specific TJ bracket kit ($100\u2013$120) that bolts to the factory floor holes. Best for: daily drivers who want meaningful comfort improvement and may add a harness later. $300\u2013$450 per seat plus brackets.\n\n**Racing-style fixed seats** (Corbeau FX1, Mastercraft Sprint): Non-reclining, lightweight, maximum lateral support. More relevant for rock crawlers where you're strapped in hard. Requires a proper harness and possibly a harness bar. Best for: dedicated trail rigs. $400\u2013$600 per seat.\n\nThe driver's and passenger's tracks are different \u2014 driver gets a slider, passenger is typically fixed or has a shorter slider. Order specifically by side.\n\n- Seat belt anchor bolt torque matters. It's typically a higher-grade bolt than the seat track bolts \u2014 replace it with new hardware if it looks corroded.\n- Some aftermarket bucket seats sit 1\u20132 inches higher than the factory seat, which can create headroom issues with a soft top. Check before buying if you're taller than 6'.\n- Heated seat kits can be retrofitted into many aftermarket seats \u2014 worth considering before you close the interior back up.\n- Seat covers for the Corbeau and Smittybilt line fit differently than OEM seat cover patterns. Order specifically for your seat model.\n\n- OEM-style replacement pair: $300\u2013$400 (Rugged Ridge, Smittybilt)\n- Corbeau Baja RS pair + brackets: $760\u2013$860\n- PRP seats: comparable to Corbeau at $350\u2013$450 per seat\n- Quadratec, 4WheelParts, and Amazon all carry the major brands; Corbeau's direct website often has the best bracket fitment information","steps":["Tilt the existing seat forward to access the rear mounting bolts (13mm, sometimes T45 Torx depending on year).","Remove front mounting bolts (13mm). The seat and track come out together \u2014 it's heavy and awkward, get a helper.","If installing new brackets: bolt the bracket to the new seat per the manufacturer's instructions before putting the assembly in the Jeep.","Drop the new seat/track/bracket assembly into position. Line up the floor holes.","Start all four bolts by hand before tightening any.","Torque to 30\u201335 ft-lb. Factory spec is 35 ft-lb \u2014 these are safety fasteners.","Test the seat slider (if applicable) through full range of motion.","Reinstall the seatbelt anchor bolt if it was relocated for access."]},{"id":"tj-sound-deadening","title":"TJ Sound Deadening \u2014 Reducing Road and Trail Noise","vehicle":"tj","category":"interior","type":"upgrade","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":4,"high":8},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":250},"tools_required":["trim removal tool","roller or credit card (for pressing deadener)","scissors or utility knife","heat gun (optional)","isopropyl alcohol"],"skills_required":["basic trim removal"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["interior","noise-reduction","NVH","comfort","overland"],"parts":[{"name":"Kilmat 80 mil butyl sound deadener (50 sq ft)","vendor":"Kilmat","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Noico 80 mil sound deadener mat (36 sq ft)","vendor":"Noico","price_estimate_usd":60},{"name":"Dynamat Xtreme door kit","vendor":"Dynamat","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"Second Skin Audio Damplifier (36 sq ft)","vendor":"Second Skin Audio","price_estimate_usd":125}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Jeep interior noise reduction guide","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/interior-accessories-59/"},{"title":"Kilmat vs Dynamat vs Noico \u2014 Sound Deadener Comparison","url":"https://www.crutchfield.com/S-abcdefgh/learn/car-audio-noise-reduction.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A TJ is loud by design \u2014 thin floors, no insulation from the factory, and a soft top that transmits every trail vibration. Sound deadening the floors and firewall cuts road fatigue on long drives without changing the character of the rig.","description":"The TJ's interior noise is structural: thin uncoated sheetmetal floors, a firewall with minimal acoustic treatment, and door skins with no deadener. On highway miles you're competing with tire roar, wind noise through the soft top seams, and exhaust resonance through the floorpan. Fixing this is not about making the TJ quiet \u2014 that's not achievable \u2014 it's about making 3 hours of highway driving feel like 3 hours instead of 6.\n\nButyl sound deadener (the adhesive constrained-layer type) is the right material for floors and firewall. It converts sheetmetal vibration to heat and takes the ringing out of flat metal panels. Foam closed-cell insulation (like MLV or HushMat Ultra) goes on top of the deadener to block sound transmission. You don't need both everywhere \u2014 deadener on the floors and firewall alone makes a noticeable difference.\n\nDynamat is the brand name most people know. Kilmat and Noico are functionally equivalent at a third of the price \u2014 multiple independent tests show similar performance at 80 mil thickness. The premium of Dynamat is largely brand positioning at this point. Second Skin Audio is a legitimate mid-tier alternative with better customer support than the budget brands.\n\nPriority areas in order of impact: (1) floorpan (largest flat panel, biggest vibration source), (2) firewall (engine and exhaust noise), (3) doors (rattles from half-doors especially), (4) rear tub walls.\n\n- 36 to 50 sq ft of 80 mil butyl deadener (measure your floor area first \u2014 TJ floors run about 20 sq ft, firewall is about 8 sq ft)\n- Roller, wooden dowel, or credit card for pressing\n- Isopropyl alcohol and rags for surface prep\n- Heat gun or hair dryer (warm butyl is easier to conform to compound curves)\n- Trim removal tools for carpet and seats\n\n1. **Remove carpet, seat mounts, and any trim panels over the target area.** TJ factory carpet is minimal \u2014 most of it lifts out after removing the front seats (4 bolts each) and pulling up the floor mats.\n\n2. **Clean the sheetmetal.** Wipe with isopropyl alcohol and let dry fully. Deadener won't bond to oily or dusty metal.\n\n3. **Cut deadener sheets to shape.** Don't try to cover every square inch \u2014 60\u201370% coverage is sufficient to achieve most of the damping effect. Focus on the larger flat panels rather than complex curved areas.\n\n4. **Peel and stick.** Start at one edge, press down as you unroll to avoid trapping air. Use the roller to burnish firmly across the entire piece.\n\n5. **Warm complex areas with a heat gun.** The butyl gets more pliable at 80\u2013100\u00b0F and will conform to compound curves without bubbling.\n\n6. **Reinstall carpet and seats.** Torque seat bolts to spec.\n\n7. **Drive before judging results.** You'll notice the difference most at highway speed and on rough washboard roads.\n\n- Don't apply deadener over any drain holes or drain plugs in the floor. TJ drains are at the corners of the tub \u2014 water has to get out.\n- If your TJ has rust on the floor, treat or repair it before sealing it under deadener. Trapping moisture accelerates rust significantly.\n- Half doors contribute to noise more than most owners expect. Deadening the inner door skin of half doors (even just one layer) is worth doing if you daily drive with them on.\n\nKilmat or Noico 80 mil (36 sq ft): $60\u2013$75. Enough for floors and firewall with a little left over. Dynamat Xtreme comparable coverage: $180\u2013$220. The performance difference does not justify the cost difference for a TJ. Budget $80 and a Saturday for a full floor-and-firewall treatment."},{"id":"tj-serpentine-belt","title":"Serpentine Belt Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":60},"tools_required":["1/2 breaker bar or serpentine belt tool","15mm socket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["belt","accessory-drive","4.0l","2.5l"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar Serpentine Belt 4.0L w/ A/C","part_number":"5281668AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Gates K060910 Micro-V Belt (97-02 4.0L)","vendor":"Gates/RockAuto","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Goodyear/Continental serpentine belt equivalent","vendor":"AutoZone","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"Mopar Serpentine Belt 5281668AA listing","url":"https://www.justforjeeps.com/sebefor4tjwj.html"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 belt part number discussion","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/serpentine-belt-part-number.2613/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"If your TJ's belt is older than five years or you can see cracks across the ribs when you flex it, replace it before it strands you \u2014 total job is under an hour with one wrench.","description":"The TJ runs a single serpentine belt driving the alternator, power steering pump, water pump, A/C compressor (if equipped), and on some years the idler. A snapped belt kills charging and steering at the same time, and on the 4.0L it also kills the water pump, so an overheat is minutes away. Belts crack with age more than mileage \u2014 a garage-queen TJ with 30k on the clock and 10 years on the belt is just as likely to grenade one as a daily driver.\n\nPart numbers depend on year and accessories. For 2000\u20132006 4.0L with A/C, the Mopar belt is 5281668AA. The Gates equivalent is K060910 (six-rib, ~91\" effective length). For 4.0L without A/C, use a 75\" six-rib (Gates K060749 or similar). For the 2.5L four-cylinder, length differs again \u2014 confirm by VIN at Gates.com or measure your old belt before buying.\n\nThe tensioner is a spring-loaded pulley on the driver's-side front of the engine. Rotate it counterclockwise with a 1/2\" breaker bar or a dedicated serpentine belt tool to release tension. Slide the old belt off, route the new one according to the diagram on the radiator support (or photograph the old routing before removal \u2014 this is the single most common mistake). Let the tensioner spring back against the new belt. Done.\n\nWhile the belt is off, spin every accessory pulley by hand. Any roughness, wobble, or grinding means a bearing is on the way out \u2014 tensioner, idler, or water pump are the usual suspects. Replace anything that doesn't spin clean. A fresh belt on a dying idler will tear itself up in months.","steps":["Park the TJ, key off, hood up, engine cool.","Photograph the existing belt routing or note the diagram on the radiator support.","Position a 1/2\" breaker bar on the tensioner pulley center bolt.","Rotate the tensioner counterclockwise to relieve tension.","Slide the belt off the alternator pulley first, then off the tensioner.","Spin each pulley by hand \u2014 alternator, idler, tensioner, water pump, P/S pump, A/C.","Route the new belt per diagram, slipping it onto every pulley except the alternator last.","Pull tensioner counterclockwise again, slip the belt onto the alternator pulley.","Release the tensioner slowly against the new belt.","Verify routing, start engine, watch for proper tracking with no squeals."],"pros":["Cheapest preventive job in the engine bay","30\u201360 minutes start to finish","No special tools beyond a breaker bar","Lets you inspect every accessory pulley while you're in there"],"cons":["Wrong belt length is a real risk \u2014 confirm by VIN or measure the old one","Tensioner can be tight to break loose if it has never been serviced","Mis-routed belt will shred immediately on startup"]},{"id":"tj-brake-fluid-tj","title":"Brake Fluid Flush","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":15,"high":35},"tools_required":["10mm box wrench","clear vinyl tubing (1/4\")","turkey baster","catch bottle","shop rags"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","bleed sequence"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["brakes","preventive","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline DOT 3/4 Brake Fluid (32oz)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":8},{"name":"Mopar DOT 3 Brake Fluid (12oz)","part_number":"4318080AD","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"ATE Type 200 (amber, German spec) \u2014 for DOT 4 upgrade","vendor":"ATE","price_estimate_usd":18}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 Brake Fluid Capacity Discussion","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid.6954/"},{"title":"TJ Brake Fluid Change Procedure","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/brake-fluid-change.21283/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The TJ specs DOT 3, but DOT 4 is a smart upgrade for any rig that sees mountain descents or heavy towing. Plan on 32oz of fluid and a one-person gravity bleed or a two-person pedal bleed \u2014 both work.","description":"Brake fluid is hygroscopic, which is a fancy way of saying it sucks water out of the air. Wet fluid boils at a lower temperature than dry fluid, and the moment your fluid boils mid-pedal, you have no brakes. The factory recommends flushing every two years \u2014 almost nobody does, and almost nobody should be surprised when their pedal goes soft on a long descent down Schnebly Hill. If your fluid in the reservoir looks dark amber or coffee-colored, you're overdue.\n\nStart by sucking the old fluid out of the master cylinder reservoir with a turkey baster. Get every drop you can without dragging the screen at the bottom. Refill with fresh fluid to the MAX line. This step alone replaces about 30% of the system's volume and is worth doing on its own if you're short on time.\n\nFor the actual flush, the TJ bleed sequence is rear passenger \u2192 rear driver \u2192 front passenger \u2192 front driver. Furthest from the master cylinder first, closest last. Pop a 10mm box wrench on the bleeder, slide a piece of clear 1/4\" vinyl tubing over the nipple, and run the tubing into a bottle with about an inch of fresh fluid in the bottom (the fluid in the bottle keeps air from sucking back in).\n\nTwo-person method: helper presses the pedal slowly to the floor, you crack the bleeder, fluid pushes out, you close the bleeder, helper releases. Repeat five or six times per wheel until clear fluid runs through the tubing. Critical rule \u2014 never let the master cylinder run dry. Top it off after every wheel. A dry master cylinder pulls air into the ABS module on later TJs and turns a 90-minute job into a several-hour ordeal.\n\nPlan on about a quart of fluid total for a proper flush. Buy two quarts so you're never tempted to skimp. If you're switching from DOT 3 to DOT 4, just flush thoroughly \u2014 they're chemically compatible. Do not mix DOT 5 (silicone) with anything; that's a full rebuild conversation.","steps":["Park on level ground, wheels chocked. Open the hood.","Use a turkey baster to remove old fluid from the master cylinder reservoir.","Refill the reservoir to MAX with fresh fluid.","Crack each bleeder loose with a 10mm wrench before starting, then re-snug. (Confirms they aren't seized.)","Start at rear passenger wheel. Slide clear tubing over the bleeder nipple into a catch bottle with fresh fluid in the bottom.","Helper depresses the pedal slowly. Open the bleeder, let fluid flow, close the bleeder before pedal hits floor. Helper releases pedal.","Repeat 5\u20136 times per wheel until fluid running through tubing is clear and bubble-free.","Check the master cylinder after every wheel and top off \u2014 never let it run dry.","Move to rear driver, then front passenger, then front driver in that order.","Final master cylinder top-off to MAX. Press the pedal \u2014 it should be firm.","Test in driveway at low speed before hitting the road."],"pros":["$15 of fluid prevents brake fade on long descents","Restores firm pedal feel that owners often blame on calipers or master cylinders","One of the highest safety-to-effort ratios in DIY maintenance"],"cons":["Letting the master cylinder run dry pulls air into the ABS module on later TJs","Brake fluid eats paint \u2014 wipe spills immediately","Two-person method is faster but you need a helper"]},{"id":"tj-coolant-flush-tj","title":"Coolant System Flush and Inspection","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1.5,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":80},"tools_required":["drain pan (3+ gal)","garden hose","flathead screwdriver","channel lock pliers","funnel"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","fluid handling"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["cooling","preventative","4.0L"],"parts":[{"name":"Zerex G-05 HOAT concentrate (1 gal)","part_number":"ZXG051","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":20},{"name":"Distilled water (2 gallons)","vendor":"any grocery","price_estimate_usd":4},{"name":"Mopar 195\u00b0F thermostat (optional refresh)","part_number":"5083285AA","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Radiator cap, 16 psi (optional)","part_number":"RC52","vendor":"Stant","price_estimate_usd":8}],"citations":[{"title":"WranglerTJForum \u2014 cooling system flush writeup","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/how-to-flush-the-cooling-system-on-your-jeep-wrangler-tj.1737/"},{"title":"WranglerForum \u2014 TJ 4.0 coolant capacity thread","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/tj-4-0-coolant-capacity.1534681/"},{"title":"CarCareKiosk \u2014 2004 Wrangler 4.0L coolant flush video","url":"https://www.carcarekiosk.com/video/2004_Jeep_Wrangler_Rubicon_4.0L_6_Cyl./coolant_antifreeze/flush_coolant"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Drain the radiator and block, flush with distilled water until the runoff is clear, then refill with HOAT (G-05) coolant at 50/50. The TJ 4.0L holds about 2.6 gallons dry; plan for 1.5 gallons of new coolant on a drain-and-fill.","description":"The TJ 4.0L cooling system is robust, but coolant breaks down chemically every 5 years regardless of mileage. Once the corrosion inhibitors are spent, the inside of your radiator, heater core, and block start eating themselves. A fresh flush is cheap insurance and the most common cause of TJ overheating is not a bad water pump \u2014 it is a radiator core silted shut from a decade of skipped flushes.\n\nFull dry capacity is 10.5 quarts (about 2.6 gallons). A standard drain through the radiator petcock recovers around 1.5 gallons, which is what you replace on a routine service. If the existing coolant is brown, rusty, or smells off, you need a full flush \u2014 drain the radiator, drain the block (8mm hex plug on the passenger side of the engine, just above the starter), and then run distilled water through the system until clear.\n\nCoolant choice matters. The factory spec for 1997\u20132001 TJs is the green ethylene glycol coolant; 2002+ shipped with HOAT (Mopar's pink). Either is fine for older Jeeps as long as you fully flush before switching. The safe, modern answer for any TJ is Zerex G-05 \u2014 it is the OEM HOAT formula sold under the Valvoline label and is compatible with iron blocks and brass/copper radiators. Stay away from universal \"works with everything\" coolants; they tend to gel when mixed with leftover residue.\n\nA few things to inspect while the system is empty: pressure-test the radiator cap (16 psi is spec), squeeze the upper and lower hoses for soft spots and cracks at the bend, and look at the back of the water pump weep hole for crusty buildup. If the thermostat is original, this is the moment to swap it for a $14 Mopar 195\u00b0F replacement and stop guessing about why the temp gauge wanders.\n\nRefilling needs a bleed. The TJ has a high spot in the heater core circuit that traps air. With the cap off, start the engine, set the heater on full hot, and let the system burp for 10\u201315 minutes. Top off as the level drops. Drive a heat cycle, let it cool overnight, and check the level again before you call it done.","steps":["Park on level ground, engine cold. Remove the radiator cap.","Place a drain pan under the radiator. Open the petcock (lower passenger side) \u2014 turn the wing tab counterclockwise.","Drain fully. For a complete flush, also remove the block drain plug (8mm hex, passenger side above starter).","Close the petcock and block plug. Reinstall both.","Optional full flush: refill with plain distilled water, run engine to temp with heater on, drain again. Repeat until water runs clear.","Refill the radiator with 50/50 G-05 (or premix) to the top. Fill the overflow bottle to the FULL HOT line.","Leave the cap off. Start engine, heater on full hot, idle 10\u201315 minutes.","Top off the radiator as the level drops while air bleeds out.","Install cap. Drive a heat cycle. Recheck level the next morning."],"pros":["One of the cheapest preventative jobs you can do \u2014 $35 in fluid","Catches a failing thermostat, weak cap, or soft hose before it strands you","Restores cooling capacity if the system was full of old, depleted coolant","No special tools beyond a drain pan and a garden hose"],"cons":["Petcock on older TJs gets brittle and can break off \u2014 be gentle","Skipping the heater-on bleed traps air and causes false overheating","Mixing coolant types without a flush causes gel and corrosion","Block drain plug is in an awkward spot above the starter"]},{"id":"tj-differential-fluid","title":"Differential Fluid Change \u2014 Dana 30/35/44","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"drivetrain","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":35,"high":80},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","3/8 hex driver (for fill plug)","1/2 breaker bar","fluid pump","drain pan","RTV silicone or cover gasket"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["maintenance","drivetrain","axles","fluids"],"parts":[{"name":"Valvoline SynPower 75W-90 (1 qt)","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":12},{"name":"Royal Purple Max-Gear 75W-90 (1 qt)","vendor":"Royal Purple","price_estimate_usd":16},{"name":"Mopar 4 oz friction modifier (Trac-Lok only)","part_number":"4318060AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":14},{"name":"Lubelocker differential cover gasket","vendor":"Lubelocker","price_estimate_usd":22}],"citations":[{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 Fluid Capacities and Recommendations","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/wrangler-tj-fluid-capacities.195/"},{"title":"Motobilt \u2014 Jeep Wrangler Differential Fluid Capacity Chart","url":"https://motobilt.com/blogs/tech/jeep-wrangler-differential-fluid-capacity-chart"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Change TJ differential fluid every 30,000 miles or every wet trail run \u2014 Dana 30 front takes about 1.25 quarts, Dana 35 rear takes about 1.7 quarts, Dana 44 rear takes about 2 quarts of 75W-90 synthetic gear oil.","description":"The TJ Wrangler shipped with three axle combinations: Dana 30 front on every model, Dana 35 rear on most non-Rubicon trims, and Dana 44 rear on the Rubicon (2003\u20132006) and some early Sahara/65th Anniversary builds. All three use the same basic service procedure \u2014 pull the cover, drain, scrape, reseal, refill through the fill plug \u2014 and all three benefit from a 75W-90 or 75W-140 synthetic gear oil. The most common mistake is changing the rear without checking what's actually back there. A Dana 35 has a smaller, more rounded cover; the Dana 44 cover is larger with a flatter bottom edge and 10 bolts.\n\nCapacities differ. The Dana 30 front takes roughly 1.25 quarts (1.65 quarts on 1997 models, which used a slightly different housing). The Dana 35 rear takes about 1.7 quarts. The Dana 44 Rubicon rear takes 2 quarts. Buy 3 quarts if you've got the Dana 30 + Dana 35 combination, 4 quarts if you've got the Dana 30 + Dana 44 combination, and you'll have enough to refill both with a little left over for spillage.\n\nFluid choice matters more than the brand. 75W-90 GL-5 synthetic is the daily-driver standard. If you tow regularly or run 33s and above with regular trail use, step up to 75W-140 synthetic \u2014 the extra viscosity protects the ring and pinion under load. Skip the cheapest conventional 80W-90 unless you're flushing fluid every 15k miles. Rubicon Tru-Lok lockers do not require friction modifier. Trac-Lok limited-slip differentials (often found in the Dana 44 rear, some Dana 35s) require 4 oz of Mopar friction modifier (P/N 4318060AB) per fill, or the clutches will chatter on turns.\n\nMost TJ axles do not have a drain plug. You drain by removing the rear differential cover, which means you'll also be replacing or reusing the cover gasket. RTV silicone (Permatex Ultra Black) is the OEM-style seal \u2014 clean both surfaces fully, lay a 3/16\" bead around the bolt circle, install the cover, and let RTV cure 30 minutes before refilling. A Lubelocker reusable gasket is the upgrade \u2014 install dry, no cure time, and reusable for the next service.","steps":["Drive the vehicle for 10 minutes to warm the fluid for easier draining. Park level.","Place a drain pan under the differential. Loosen the fill plug FIRST with a 3/8 hex driver \u2014 if it's stuck or stripped, you need to know before you've already drained the axle.","Remove the differential cover bolts in a star pattern. Leave the top two bolts loose to control fluid release.","Crack the cover loose with a flathead screwdriver. Fluid will pour out \u2014 let it fully drain (5\u201310 minutes).","Remove the cover. Inspect the inside of the housing for metal chunks, glitter, or chipped gear teeth.","Wipe the housing interior, ring gear, and magnet (if equipped) clean of old fluid and debris. Reinstall the magnet.","Scrape old RTV from both mating surfaces with a plastic scraper. Wipe with brake cleaner.","Apply a 3/16\" bead of RTV around the cover bolt circle, or install a Lubelocker gasket dry.","Reinstall the cover and torque bolts in a star pattern to 30 ft-lbs.","Wait 30 minutes for RTV to set (skip if using Lubelocker).","Pump 75W-90 synthetic gear oil through the fill hole until fluid trickles out the bottom of the fill hole. For Trac-Lok diffs, add 4 oz friction modifier before topping off.","Reinstall and torque the fill plug to 25 ft-lbs. Check for leaks after the first short drive."],"pros":["Catches metal debris before it grinds the ring and pinion","Cheap insurance \u2014 $40 in fluid vs. $1,500+ for a ring-and-pinion replacement","Reveals worn gears, chipped teeth, or excessive backlash early","Synthetic gear oil runs cooler under load"],"cons":["Messy job \u2014 fluid drips everywhere if you rush","Requires RTV cure time (or a reusable gasket)","Friction modifier is non-optional with Trac-Lok or it will chatter","The Dana 30 front fill plug is often seized \u2014 soak in penetrant the night before"]},{"id":"tj-oil-change-tj","title":"Oil Change \u2014 4.0L and 2.5L","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"engine_oil","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":30,"high":60},"tools_required":["13mm socket","oil filter wrench (76mm cap or strap)","drain pan","torque wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["oil-change","4.0l","2.5l","maintenance"],"parts":[{"name":"10W-30 or 5W-30 conventional or synthetic blend (6 quarts 4.0L / 4 quarts 2.5L)","part_number":"MS-6395","vendor":"any API SN+ rated","price_estimate_usd":24},{"name":"Mopar oil filter (spin-on)","part_number":"5281090","vendor":"Mopar / equivalent","price_estimate_usd":6},{"name":"Drain plug gasket","part_number":"33002770","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":1}],"citations":[{"title":"AMC/Chrysler 4.0L service manual oil capacity (6 qt with filter)","url":"https://www.fsmrepairmanual.com"},{"title":"TJ owner's manual \u2014 10W-30 above 40\u00b0F, 5W-30 below","url":"https://www.jeep.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 4.0L holds 6 quarts of 10W-30 (5W-30 in cold climates) with a spin-on filter. The 2.5L 4-cylinder holds 4 quarts. Drain plug torques to 25 ft-lb, filter hand-tight plus 3/4 turn. Job takes 30 minutes.","description":"The TJ Wrangler runs either the 4.0L straight-six or the 2.5L AMC four-cylinder. Both use a conventional spin-on filter mounted on the lower passenger side of the engine block and a 13mm hex drain plug on the oil pan. The procedures are identical \u2014 only the capacity changes.\n\n**Oil viscosity.** The factory called for 10W-30 above 40\u00b0F and 5W-30 below 40\u00b0F. Either is fine year-round in most climates. Some owners run 10W-40 in hot Southwest summers \u2014 the 4.0L wears in for the long haul on anything in this range. Phoenix and Las Vegas owners can safely run 15W-40 conventional. Don't run 0W-20 \u2014 the 4.0L has wider bearing clearances than modern engines and will burn it.\n\n**Synthetic or conventional?** Conventional changed every 3,000 miles or full synthetic every 5,000 miles both work. The 4.0L is an old design with loose tolerances; it doesn't reward expensive synthetic the way a modern direct-injected engine does. The exception: if your TJ has 200K+ miles and burns a quart between changes, switching to a high-mileage synthetic blend with seal conditioner can stop leaks at the rear main and valve cover.\n\n**Filter.** Any name-brand spin-on filter that fits the M22x1.5 thread works \u2014 WIX 51515, Mopar 5281090, FRAM PH3614, Mobil 1 M1-110A. The filter sits at an angle on the block; expect a quart of mess if you don't position the drain pan right.","steps":["Run engine 2\u20133 minutes to thin oil. Shut off.","Position drain pan under the oil pan plug (rear of pan, 13mm hex).","Remove drain plug. Inspect the gasket \u2014 replace if crushed or torn.","While oil drains, reposition the pan under the filter (slightly forward and to the passenger side).","Spin off the old filter (counterclockwise from below). It will dump 4\u20138 oz of oil straight down \u2014 be ready.","Wipe the filter mount clean. Verify the old filter gasket came off with the filter (a stuck gasket is the #1 cause of catastrophic oil leaks after a DIY change).","Wet the new filter's gasket with fresh oil. Spin on by hand until snug, then 3/4 turn more. Do not use a filter wrench to tighten \u2014 that's how you crush the can.","Reinstall drain plug with new gasket. Torque to 25 ft-lb.","Add oil through the valve cover fill cap. 4.0L: 6 qt. 2.5L: 4 qt.","Start engine. Verify oil pressure light goes out within 3 seconds. Idle 30 seconds, shut off.","Wait 5 minutes, check dipstick. Top off as needed.","Drive 5 minutes, check for leaks around the filter and drain plug when you stop."],"pros":["Spin-on filter is the easiest design ever made","30-minute job with hand tools only","Cheap parts at any store","4.0L doesn't care about brand of oil"],"cons":["Filter location dumps oil on the front axle and skid plate every time","Stuck old gasket = oil all over the driveway in 10 miles","Owners who overtighten filters strip the threaded boss in the block (expensive fix)","Drain plug crush gasket is often skipped \u2014 leaks slowly until it's noticed"]},{"id":"tj-spark-plugs-tj","title":"Spark Plug Replacement \u2014 4.0L","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","subcategory":"ignition","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":18,"high":45},"tools_required":["5/8\" spark plug socket","3/8\" ratchet and 6\" extension","torque wrench","spark plug gap gauge","dielectric grease (optional)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["spark-plugs","ignition","4.0l","tune-up"],"parts":[{"name":"Champion RC12LYC OEM copper (qty 6)","part_number":"RC12LYC","vendor":"Champion","price_estimate_usd":18},{"name":"NGK ZFR5N copper (qty 6)","part_number":"5165","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"NGK ZFR5F-11 platinum (qty 6, longer life)","part_number":"3459","vendor":"NGK","price_estimate_usd":35}],"citations":[{"title":"Chrysler TJ 4.0L service info \u2014 0.035\" gap, 27 ft-lb plug torque","url":"https://www.jeep.com"},{"title":"Champion RC12LYC OEM application data for 4.0L Jeep","url":"https://www.championautoparts.com"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The 4.0L takes six plugs, gapped at 0.035\", torqued to 27 ft-lb. Copper plugs (Champion RC12LYC or NGK ZFR5N) are the OEM choice and run cleaner than premium platinums on this old engine. Job takes 30\u201360 minutes with a 5/8\" plug socket.","description":"The 4.0L straight-six uses a simple distributor-and-coil ignition through 1997, then coil-on-distributor with cap-and-rotor through 2006. Either way, the plugs sit in a row down the passenger side of the engine, fully exposed, no intake to remove. This is the easiest spark plug job on any modern engine.\n\n**Plug choice.** The factory plug is the Champion RC12LYC copper. NGK ZFR5N is the direct equivalent. Both gap to 0.035\" out of the box. Service interval is 30,000 miles on copper, 60,000 on platinum (NGK ZFR5F-11), 100,000 on iridium (NGK IZFR5F-11). On an engine this simple, copper is the better answer: cheaper, runs cooler, easier to read for diagnostics.\n\n**Why not premium plugs?** The 4.0L was designed for copper. Iridium plugs sometimes cause cold-start hesitation on this engine because the precious metal tip has a slightly higher firing voltage requirement and the older ignition coil isn't always up to it. If your TJ runs perfectly on copper and you switch to iridium and get rough idle, that's why.\n\n**Gap matters.** Pre-gapped doesn't mean correctly gapped. Always verify with a wire gauge \u2014 0.035\" should slide through with light drag. If you can shove a 0.040\" gauge through too, the plug is no good (don't try to close the gap on iridium; the tip chips).\n\n**Anti-seize warning.** Most modern plugs ship with a nickel-coated thread that does not need anti-seize. Adding anti-seize will cause you to overtorque (the lubricant reduces friction, so your torque wrench reads correct but the actual clamp load is too high). If you must use it, a tiny dab on the threads only.","steps":["Engine cold (or at least cool to the touch). Hot heads can pull threads out when you remove plugs.","Pop the plug wire boots off one at a time. Twist before pulling to break the seal \u2014 don't yank straight up.","Blow compressed air around the plug well to remove any dirt before unscrewing.","Use the 5/8\" plug socket with extension. Back the plug out a half turn, then turn by hand the rest of the way to feel for cross-threading.","Inspect the old plug: tan/brown = correct mixture. White/glazed = lean. Black/sooty = rich. Oily = valve seal or piston ring issue.","Check the new plug's gap with a wire gauge. Should be 0.035\". Bend the side electrode only, never the center.","Apply a small dab of dielectric grease inside the plug wire boot (helps prevent moisture-related misfires).","Thread the new plug in by hand until snug. If it doesn't go in by hand, stop \u2014 you're cross-threading.","Torque to 27 ft-lb with a torque wrench. On an aluminum head this is non-negotiable; the 4.0L iron head is more forgiving but a wrench is still smart.","Reattach the plug wire. Repeat for all six.","Start, listen for misfire. If you have a code reader, scan for P0301\u2013P0306."],"pros":["Easiest plug job on any modern engine","All six plugs exposed, no intake removal","Copper plugs cost $18 for the set","Old plug condition tells you a lot about engine health"],"cons":["Yanking plug wires straight up tears the terminal off","Hot-engine plug removal pulls aluminum threads out of cylinder heads (rare on 4.0L iron head but possible on 2.5L)","Anti-seize causes overtorque if you don't reduce torque spec by ~25%","Aftermarket plug wires from auction sites cause more misfires than any worn plug"]},{"id":"tj-transfer-case-fluid-tj","title":"Transfer Case Fluid \u2014 NP231 and NV231","vehicle":"tj","category":"maintenance","type":"task","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":40},"tools_required":["3/8 ratchet","fluid pump","drain pan","1/2 drive ratchet for fill/drain plugs"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["drivetrain","transfer-case","service-interval","atf"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar ATF+4 (1 quart)","part_number":"68218057AC","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":9},{"name":"Valvoline MaxLife ATF (1 quart)","part_number":"773775","vendor":"Valvoline","price_estimate_usd":7},{"name":"Mopar transfer case fill/drain plug crush washer","part_number":"06034203","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":2}],"citations":[{"title":"Novak \u2014 Guide to the New Process / New Venture Gear 231 Transfer Case","url":"https://www.novak-adapt.com/knowledge/transfer-cases/np231.html"},{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 Hit 1.5 quarts on 231 transfer case, should I keep going?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/hit-1-5-quarts-on-231-transfer-case-should-i-keep-going.52014/"},{"title":"TJ Forum \u2014 ATF or ATF+4 for transfer case fluid?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/atf-or-atf-4-for-transfer-case-fluid.4818/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The TJ's NP231 transfer case takes about 2 quarts of ATF+4 and the fluid change takes 30 minutes with hand tools \u2014 the transfer case is unfussy about ATF brand, but it does need clean fluid every 30,000 miles to keep the planetary gears and chain happy.","description":"The NP231 (later relabeled NV231 after New Venture Gear took over) is the standard TJ transfer case for non-Rubicon builds. It's a chain-driven, part-time 4WD unit with a planetary low range. The lubrication system is simple: ATF splashes from a pump inside the case onto the chain, the planetary, and the bearings. Clean fluid keeps the chain from wearing the case and keeps the bearings from pitting.\n\nMopar's spec is ATF+4. The forums spend a lot of energy debating whether Dexron/Mercon or other ATFs work \u2014 the short answer is yes, they do, the transfer case isn't picky like a modern automatic transmission. ATF+4 is the safe call because it's the manufacturer spec and Walmart sells it for $9 a quart. If you have leftover Valvoline MaxLife from a transmission service, that's fine in the transfer case too.\n\nCapacity is the more useful question. The shop manual says 2.2 pints (about 1.1 quarts), but the real fill range from owners who pump until fluid comes out the fill hole is 1.5 to 2 quarts. The \"correct\" answer is: pump fluid in until it runs out the fill hole, then stop. Don't trust a fixed number \u2014 fluid level varies with how level the rig is parked and where the fill hole is positioned on the case. Park as level as you can manage.\n\nThe plugs are both 1/2\" drive square plugs \u2014 no socket, just stick a 1/2\" ratchet directly into the plug. Drain plug is at the bottom of the case; fill plug is on the rear of the case, roughly halfway up. Crack the FILL plug first. If it won't budge, do not drain. A stuck fill plug means you can drain a transfer case you can't refill, and you'll be towing the Jeep instead of driving it.\n\nA common upgrade is a magnetic drain plug \u2014 replaces the factory plug, catches metal fuzz, and gives you a diagnostic check at the next service. About $15 from B&M, AFE, or any magnetic-plug vendor.","steps":["Park as level as possible. Warm the transfer case by driving a few miles.","Slide a wide drain pan under the t-case.","Crack the FILL plug first (1/2\" ratchet drive, rear of case). Confirm it breaks loose. If not, stop.","Remove the drain plug (bottom of case). Let it drain fully \u2014 15\u201320 minutes.","Inspect the drained fluid. Brown is normal. Milky means water intrusion (check the vent line and any submerged fords). Metal flakes mean internal wear \u2014 investigate before refilling.","Reinstall drain plug with crush washer. Torque to 24 ft-lb (snug \u2014 these plugs are soft).","Use a fluid pump in the new ATF+4 jug. Pump fluid through the fill hole until it runs out steady. Usually 1.5\u20132 quarts.","Reinstall fill plug, torque to 24 ft-lb. Wipe down and inspect for leaks after a short drive."],"pros":["Cheap and fast \u2014 30 minutes, $20 in fluid","No special tools beyond a ratchet","Diagnostic value: fluid condition tells you what's happening inside","Upgrading to a magnetic drain plug catches problems early"],"cons":["Easy to start without confirming the fill plug breaks loose \u2014 stuck plug = stranded rig","Manual capacity (1.1 qt) and actual fill (1.5\u20132 qt) disagree, which causes confusion for first-timers","Plug heads strip if you over-torque \u2014 they're soft cast plugs"]},{"id":"tj-recovery-gear-kit","title":"Recovery Gear Starter Kit for the TJ","vehicle":"tj","category":"recovery","type":"kit","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":200,"high":550},"tools_required":[],"skills_required":["recovery rigging basics","kinetic rope technique"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["recovery","traction","kinetic-rope","hi-lift","maxtrax"],"parts":[{"name":"Bubba Rope 20' Sidewinder Kinetic Rope 3/4\" (17,600 lb MBS)","vendor":"Bubba Rope","price_estimate_usd":99},{"name":"Rhino USA 3/4\" D-Ring Shackles (2-pack, 41,850 lb WLL)","vendor":"Rhino USA","price_estimate_usd":29},{"name":"Maxtrax MKII Recovery Boards (pair, LDPE plastic)","vendor":"Maxtrax","price_estimate_usd":349},{"name":"Hi-Lift Jack HL-485 (48-inch, 4,660 lb capacity)","vendor":"Hi-Lift Jack Company","price_estimate_usd":89},{"name":"ARB Recovery Bag (medium, waterproof storage)","vendor":"ARB 4x4 Accessories","price_estimate_usd":49}],"citations":[{"title":"Overland Journal \u2014 Building a Recovery Kit for Trail Use","url":"https://overlandjournal.com/gear/recovery-kit-guide/"},{"title":"Bubba Rope Kinetic Recovery Rope Selection Guide","url":"https://www.bubbarope.com/how-to-choose"},{"title":"Hi-Lift Jack Usage and Safety Instructions","url":"https://hi-lift.com/instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-winch","tj-front-bumper","tj-rear-bumper"],"summary":"No recovery kit means depending entirely on trail partners to unstick you \u2014 and trail partners aren't always available.","description":"A recovery gear kit is the minimum equipment every TJ owner should carry before leaving pavement. The components don't require a winch, don't require a spotter, and don't require any mechanical skill to use effectively \u2014 but they do require knowing what each piece does before you're stuck in a ditch. Buy the gear, read the instructions, and practice the techniques at home before you need them on a trail.\n\nThe core of the kit is the kinetic rope. Unlike a static tow strap that transmits force rigidly (and can damage recovery points, snap, or jerk the stuck vehicle hard), a kinetic rope stretches under tension \u2014 typically 20\u201330% elongation \u2014 and stores energy like a spring. A vehicle stuck in mud or sand gets a momentum-based tug rather than a rigid yank. Bubba Rope's Sidewinder is a broadly-trusted kinetic rope at a reasonable price; the 3/4\" / 20' version is correctly sized for the TJ's weight class. Two D-ring shackles connect the rope to the recovery points (front bumper D-rings or tow hooks on both vehicles).\n\nMaxtrax recovery boards solve a different problem: low-traction situations where the tires are spinning without forward progress. Sand, mud, loose dirt \u2014 MKII boards go under the drive tires, the LDPE material grips both the tire and the ground, and the rig drives itself out. No second vehicle needed, no rope rigging. At $349 the Maxtrax are the expensive item in this kit, but they're also the only piece of gear that's 100% self-sufficient for single-vehicle recovery.\n\nThe Hi-Lift Jack is worth carrying on any TJ build, but using it safely requires knowing its limitations. It's not a replacement for a proper floor jack \u2014 it's for field recovery: lifting a wheel off a rock, changing a tire in the backcountry, or using the HL-485's winch adapter as a manual winch in emergency situations. The HL-485 requires solid mounting points to jack against; on a TJ with a steel front bumper or rock sliders, this is straightforward.","steps":["Purchase all items and assemble in an ARB or similar waterproof recovery bag.","Mount recovery bag in the TJ's cargo area \u2014 accessible but secured so it doesn't move in rough terrain.","Mount Hi-Lift Jack on the exterior (hood, tailgate bar, or purpose-built mount) \u2014 it's too long to store inside most TJ cargo spaces effectively.","Verify the TJ has functional front and rear recovery points (D-rings, shackle mounts, or tow hooks) before heading out \u2014 recovery gear is useless without anchor points.","Practice kinetic rope rigging on flat ground before a trail: attach rope to recovery points on both vehicles, drive the recovery vehicle forward until rope goes taut, then accelerate through the stretch."],"pros":["Kinetic rope and shackles solve the most common recovery scenario (mud/sand stuck, second vehicle available) for under $130","Maxtrax boards enable self-recovery with no other vehicle or winch required","Hi-Lift Jack is a multi-purpose tool \u2014 recovery lift, tire change, manual winch adapter","Kit covers the three most common backcountry recovery scenarios: traction loss, soft-surface burial, and wheel lift","No tools required \u2014 recovery techniques are learnable in one practice session"],"cons":["Kinetic rope requires a second vehicle to use \u2014 solo recovery still requires a winch or traction boards","Hi-Lift Jack is dangerous if used incorrectly \u2014 the handle recoil under load can injure a hand or arm; training required","Maxtrax boards do not work in situations where the vehicle is high-centered or axle-deep in mud","Full kit weight is 30\u201340 lbs \u2014 plan for storage in the cargo area","D-ring shackles must be fully tightened and backed off one-quarter turn before use; a loose shackle can unthread under load"]},{"id":"tj-winch","title":"Winch Selection for the TJ Wrangler","vehicle":"tj","category":"recovery","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":299,"high":799},"tools_required":["socket set","torque wrench","wire strippers","crimping tool","heat gun (for heat-shrink connectors)"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","12V wiring basics"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["recovery","winch","self-recovery","synthetic-rope"],"parts":[{"name":"Warn M8000 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity, steel cable)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":499},{"name":"Warn VR EVO 8 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity, steel cable)","vendor":"Warn Industries","price_estimate_usd":399},{"name":"Smittybilt X20 Gen2 9,500 lb Winch with Synthetic Rope 98495","vendor":"Smittybilt","price_estimate_usd":329},{"name":"Mile Marker 8000 Electric Winch (8,000 lb capacity)","vendor":"Mile Marker Industries","price_estimate_usd":299}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Winch Selection and Install Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/winch-selection-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Warn VR EVO 8 Product Page and Specs","url":"https://warn.com/vr-evo-8"},{"title":"Overland Journal \u2014 Synthetic vs. Steel Winch Line Comparison","url":"https://overlandjournal.com/gear/synthetic-vs-steel-winch-line/"}],"alternatives":["tj-recovery-gear-kit","tj-front-bumper"],"summary":"An 8,000\u20139,500 lb winch is the right size for a TJ \u2014 the rig's GVWR sits between 3,500 and 4,000 lbs, and the 2x rule for winch capacity puts you squarely in this range.","description":"The TJ's curb weight is approximately 3,200\u20133,500 lbs depending on spec, and GVWR tops out around 4,000 lbs with a loaded rig. The standard recovery calculation is twice the vehicle weight for winch capacity, which means 7,000\u20138,000 lbs is the floor and 9,500 is a sensible ceiling. A 12,000 lb winch will technically work on a TJ, but it adds weight and wiring requirements without providing any practical benefit \u2014 the pull capacity you'd use in a real recovery scenario tops out well below what an 8,000 lb unit handles.\n\nWarn remains the benchmark for built-in-the-USA winch quality. The M8000 ($499) has been a trail standard for over a decade \u2014 known for consistent performance, a robust motor, and Warn's parts/warranty support. The VR EVO 8 ($399) is the current-generation value tier from Warn: it gives you Warn reliability and a 3-stage planetary gear system at a lower price point by using a different motor spec and a simpler remote. For most TJ owners, the VR EVO 8 is the right choice.\n\nSmittybilt's X20 Gen2 ($329) comes with a synthetic rope from the factory, which is a meaningful safety advantage over steel cable. Synthetic rope doesn't store kinetic energy the way steel cable does \u2014 when steel cable snaps under load, it recoils with force proportional to the stored tension. A synthetic rope failure is dramatic but far less dangerous. If the budget dictates a Smittybilt over a Warn, the X20 Gen2 is the pick. Expect the motor and solenoid to work harder than a Warn under heavy repeated use \u2014 it's a trail winch, not an arborist winch.\n\nA winch is useless without a winch-capable bumper. Verify your front bumper has a proper winch mount plate tied into the frame rails before purchasing. A fairlead (hawse for synthetic rope, roller fairlead for steel cable) is required and typically not included with the winch.","steps":["Verify front bumper has a winch-compatible mounting plate with frame tie-in.","Slide winch into bumper mounting channel; start all four mounting bolts hand-tight.","Torque winch mounting bolts to 55\u201365 ft-lbs.","Route power cables to the battery: positive to battery positive terminal, negative to battery negative or chassis ground. Route cables away from heat sources and sharp edges; use loom or split conduit.","Install solenoid box per manufacturer instructions (typically mounts to the winch or bumper with included hardware).","Verify power connections are tight and sealed with dielectric grease.","Install hawse fairlead (for synthetic rope) or roller fairlead (for steel cable) to bumper fairlead opening. Torque to 15\u201320 ft-lbs.","Spool the winch line through the fairlead and confirm correct drum direction \u2014 winch should pull from the bottom of the drum.","Test-fire the winch under no load in both directions with the remote. Verify solenoid actuation.","Apply winch line to a stationary point and test under light load before trail use."],"pros":["8,000\u20139,500 lb capacity is correctly sized for the TJ's weight \u2014 neither under- nor over-spec","Warn VR EVO 8 delivers brand-name reliability at a meaningful discount vs. the M8000","Synthetic rope (Smittybilt X20) is safer than steel cable in a snap scenario","Self-recovery capability removes dependence on trail partners for getting unstuck","Doubles as an anchor for vehicle-assisted tree pulls and recovery assistance for others"],"cons":["Requires a winch-compatible steel front bumper \u2014 adds to total cost if not already installed","Wiring installation must be done correctly or the winch will underperform or damage the electrical system","Winch adds 60\u201385 lbs to the front axle weight \u2014 affects handling and front suspension loading","Steel cable kinetic snap hazard \u2014 if using a steel-line winch, always drape a recovery blanket over the line during a pull","Winches are not waterproof \u2014 avoid submerging past the motor housing without a waterproofing kit"]},{"id":"tj-drag-link-tj","title":"Drag Link Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":90,"high":350},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","tie rod separator","metric sockets","torque wrench","tape measure","pliers"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["steering","death-wobble","wandering"],"parts":[{"name":"Moog DS1448 Drag Link Assembly","part_number":"DS1448","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"Synergy TJ Heavy Duty Drag Link","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":325},{"name":"RuffStuff Y-Link/Drag Link kit","vendor":"RuffStuff Specialties","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ Wrangler Torque Specs","url":"https://www.extremeterrain.com/tj-wrangler-torque-specs.html"},{"title":"Wrangler TJ Forum \u2014 Tie Rod End Torque","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/torque-specs-for-tie-rod-ends.72235/"}],"alternatives":["tj-track-bar-tj","tj-steering-stabilizer-tj"],"summary":"A worn drag link is the most common cause of TJ steering wander and a leading contributor to death wobble \u2014 replacement is a one-evening job that restores precise steering feel.","description":"The TJ drag link runs from the pitman arm down to the passenger-side knuckle on the Y-style steering setup that came on every 1997\u20132006 Wrangler. The tie rod ends are sealed and not greasable from the factory, so they wear out on a predictable schedule \u2014 usually around 80,000\u2013120,000 miles, sooner if the truck has been off-road or sees salt. Once the joints develop play, you'll feel the steering wheel wander on the highway, the front end will track over road camber, and bump-steer over uneven pavement will get sharper.\n\nThree replacement paths work depending on your goals. For a stock-tire daily driver, a Moog DS1448 unit is $90\u2013$120 and uses Moog's \"problem solver\" greasable design that lasts longer than the original. For a lifted truck on 33\"\u201335\" tires, the Synergy heavy-duty drag link ($325) gives you a chromoly bar and rebuildable greasable metal-on-metal ends \u2014 the same upgrade philosophy as their JK and XJ kits. RuffStuff offers a Y-link conversion that includes a heavier drag link if you're already replacing both the tie rod and drag link together.\n\nIf you're going to crossover steering (T-style), the drag link layout changes completely and you'll need a kit designed for that configuration. Don't try to convert with mismatched pieces.\n\nThe install is straightforward, but the TJ uses a left-hand thread on the pitman-arm end of some years \u2014 check before you start turning anything. Always measure the existing drag link length so you can set the replacement to match and avoid a major alignment swing.","steps":["Park level, chock the rear wheels, jack the front axle and put it on stands.","Measure the existing drag link center-to-center between the two TRE centers and write it down.","Remove the cotter pins and castle nuts from both ends. Spec is 55 ft-lb at the TREs and 65 ft-lb at the tie rod / drag link connection.","Use a tie rod separator to pop the ball studs free of the pitman arm and the knuckle.","Note the orientation and thread direction at each end before unthreading the old drag link from any adjusting sleeve.","Thread the new drag link to match the recorded length. Snug clamp bolts lightly until installed.","Install the new drag link in place, install new castle nuts, torque to spec, and continue tightening just enough to align the cotter pin slots.","Install fresh cotter pins. Tighten the clamp bolts to 36 ft-lb (drag link clamp) and 20 ft-lb (tie rod clamp).","Grease the new TREs if they have zerk fittings.","Drop the truck, road test, and get an alignment."],"pros":["Resolves wander, bump steer, and a major death-wobble contributor","Greasable upgrades last far longer than OEM","One-evening DIY with hand tools"],"cons":["Sealed OEM-style replacements wear again on the same schedule","Some years use a left-hand thread that catches people off guard","Alignment required after install"]},{"id":"tj-steering-stabilizer-tj","title":"Steering Stabilizer \u2014 When It Helps and When It Masks","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":0.5,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":40,"high":110},"tools_required":["pitman arm puller","metric sockets","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["death-wobble","diagnostic","safety-critical"],"parts":[{"name":"Rancho RS5000 Steering Stabilizer","part_number":"RS5407","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":45},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 Steering Stabilizer","part_number":"24-065238","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"OME Steering Stabilizer","vendor":"Old Man Emu","price_estimate_usd":95}],"citations":[{"title":"TJ steering stabilizer overview","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-wrangler/jeep-wrangler-tj/steering-stabilizer/"},{"title":"Why a stabilizer doesn't fix death wobble","url":"https://www.torquenews.com/4316/fixing-death-wobble-in-jeep-steering-wheel-not-fix"},{"title":"Diagnosing death wobble and non-DW shimmies (TJ)","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/diagnosing-death-wobble-and-fixing-non-dw-shimmies-and-wobbles-tj-version.78090/"}],"alternatives":["tj-tie-rods-tj","tj-track-bar-tj"],"summary":"A steering stabilizer is a damper, not a fix \u2014 replace yours when it's worn for normal steering smoothness, but if it's the only thing keeping the wheel calm, you have a real problem hiding in your front end.","description":"The TJ steering stabilizer is a horizontal shock that bolts between the axle and the drag link. Its job is to dampen small high-frequency oscillations \u2014 tire imbalance, road texture, bump steer over washboard. It is not designed to control death wobble, and a fresh stabilizer that \"fixes\" a wobble is masking worn track bar bushings, loose tie rod ends, or bad ball joints.\n\nStock TJ stabilizers wear out around 60,000\u2013100,000 miles. Symptoms of a dead stabilizer: steering wheel kicks sharply over speed bumps, slight shimmy after hitting a pothole, oil leaking down the body of the damper. Replacement is a 30\u201360 minute job with the right puller.\n\nThe Rancho RS5000 RS5407 is the budget pick at around $45 and is the same form factor as the OEM unit. The Bilstein 5100 ($75) gives tighter damping and longer life. Old Man Emu and AEV options run $90\u2013110 and tend to last the longest on a daily-driven TJ. Avoid the cheapest no-name dampers \u2014 Rancho has documented issues with internal binding combined with track bar play causing harsh steering feel, and even cheaper units fail faster.\n\nHere's the honest part: if your TJ has shaky steering, you should not replace the stabilizer first. Inspect the track bar bushings (both ends), the tie rod ends, the drag link, and the steering box mount before throwing a new damper at the problem. Jack the front end up and grab the tie rod \u2014 any play means a worn end. Grab the track bar at the axle bracket \u2014 any side-to-side movement means worn bushings or a cracked bracket. Fix those first. Then replace the stabilizer.","steps":["Spray penetrating oil on the stabilizer-to-drag-link stud and the bracket bolt; let it sit 10 minutes.","Loosen the nut on the tapered stud but leave it on by a few threads.","Use a pitman arm puller (rentable free at most parts stores) to break the tapered stud loose from the drag link.","Remove the nut, then unbolt the stabilizer body from the axle bracket.","Install the new stabilizer body to the axle bracket first.","Set the new stabilizer at mid-stroke before tightening the drag link end.","Torque the drag link nut to 35 ft-lbs and install a fresh cotter pin if the nut is castellated.","Bounce the front of the vehicle, turn wheel lock-to-lock, and verify no binding."],"pros":["Smooths small high-frequency steering inputs","Inexpensive and quick to replace","A fresh unit reduces driver fatigue on washboard"],"cons":["Does not fix death wobble or worn front-end parts","Can mask a serious problem until it fails","Replacing a healthy stabilizer changes nothing about steering accuracy"]},{"id":"tj-tie-rods-tj","title":"Tie Rod Replacement \u2014 Stock and HD Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","subcategory":"tie_rod","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":80,"high":700},"tools_required":["pickle fork or tie rod separator","torque wrench","metric sockets","vise grips","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","alignment understanding","thread chasing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tie-rod","steering","death-wobble","Y-link","currectlync"],"parts":[{"name":"Moog stock-replacement inner tie rod end (driver)","part_number":"ES3490T","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"Moog stock-replacement outer tie rod end (passenger)","part_number":"ES3491T","vendor":"Moog","price_estimate_usd":38},{"name":"RockJock Currectlync HD tie rod kit (TJ/LJ/XJ/MJ/ZJ)","part_number":"CE-9701","vendor":"RockJock 4x4","price_estimate_usd":600},{"name":"Steer Smarts Yeti XD tie rod","part_number":"75070103","vendor":"Steer Smarts","price_estimate_usd":650}],"citations":[{"title":"Quadratec \u2014 RockJock CE-9701 Currectlync HD tie rod kit (84\u201306 TJ/XJ)","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/56116_200_07.htm"},{"title":"RockJock 4x4 \u2014 CE-9701TR Currectlync Tie Rod (Complete)","url":"https://www.rockjock4x4.com/CE-9701TR"},{"title":"JeepSpecs \u2014 Currie Heavy Duty Steering on the TJ","url":"https://jeepspecs.com/models/jeep-wrangler/jeep-wrangler-tj/currie-heavy-duty-steering/"}],"alternatives":["tj-drag-link-tj","tj-steering-stabilizer-tj"],"summary":"Stock TJ tie rod ends wear out around 80\u2013120k miles and are a common death-wobble trigger. Replacing them with Moog problem-solver ends ($75\u2013$100 for the pair) restores stock behavior. Heavy-duty kits like the RockJock Currectlync ($600) swap the bent factory rod for a straight 1\u00bc\" solid bar \u2014 worth it if you run 33\"+ tires on rocks, overkill on a daily driver.","description":"The TJ's factory tie rod is a bent steel tube running behind the front axle. Two threaded ends (driver inner, passenger outer) thread into the rod with jam nuts and connect to the steering knuckles via tapered studs. The bent shape was a packaging compromise \u2014 it clears the differential but bends easily under impact, and the ends wear at predictable mileage.\n\n**When to replace.** Slop in the tie rod ends shows up as on-center wander, lazy returning-to-center, and at worst, death wobble. Pull the front wheels off the ground, grip each tire at 9 and 3 o'clock, and rock it. Any clunk that doesn't show in the wheel bearing or ball joints is a tie rod end. Visible boot tears mean the joint is contaminated regardless of feel \u2014 replace it.\n\n**Stock-replacement vs. heavy-duty.** Moog problem-solver ends (ES3490T driver, ES3491T passenger) install in the existing rod, take about an hour, and bring the truck back to factory feel. About $80 for the pair. For a daily-driver TJ on 31\"\u201333\" tires this is the right call.\n\n**The Currectlync upgrade.** The RockJock Currectlync (part CE-9701, fits TJ/LJ/XJ/MJ/ZJ) replaces the rod itself with a 1\u00bc\" solid alloy bar \u2014 straight, not bent \u2014 rated about 200% stronger than stock. It uses 7/8\" tie rod ends with included boots. The kit is a direct bolt-in on stock-height to 4\"-lifted trucks; over 4\" lift you need a 2\" dropped pitman arm to keep the drag link geometry correct. Steer Smarts Yeti XD is the other top-tier option, similar concept, slightly different end design.\n\n**Why HD is worth it on a wheeler.** A bent stock tie rod becomes a bent stock tie rod the first time you rock-strike it. A straight 1\u00bc\" solid bar takes the same hit and shrugs. If your TJ sees real rocks, the upgrade pays for itself the first time you avoid trailering home.\n\n**Set toe correctly.** Whatever rod you install, set toe before the alignment shop sees it. Target 1/16\" to 1/8\" toe-in with the wheels straight ahead. Measure between tire tread centers at the front and rear of the tire \u2014 the front measurement should be 1/16\" to 1/8\" smaller. Loosen jam nuts, twist the rod to adjust, retighten to 55 ft-lb. Then take it for an alignment to confirm camber and caster.","steps":["Park on level concrete. Loosen front lug nuts. Lift the front and set on jack stands. Pull both front wheels.","Diagnose \u2014 rock each tire 9/3, look for clunk independent of wheel bearing.","With ignition on and helper turning the wheel, watch each tie rod end joint for independent movement.","Measure existing toe between tire centers, front and rear of tire, to know where to set the new rod.","Loosen jam nuts at each end of the tie rod. Note approximate thread engagement.","Remove cotter pins and castle nuts at the knuckle. Use a tie rod separator (not a pickle fork \u2014 pickle fork damages the boot of any part you intend to reuse).","Unthread the rod from both ends or pull the entire assembly and transfer to new HD kit.","Install new ends or new HD rod. Snug to length matching the original measurement.","Set toe to 1/16\"\u20131/8\" toe-in.","Torque castle nuts (40 ft-lb, then tighten to align cotter pin slot \u2014 never back off). Install new cotter pins.","Torque jam nuts to 55 ft-lb.","Road test. Then alignment shop within a week."],"pros":["Restores precise steering feel","Eliminates a common death-wobble trigger","HD kit takes rock impacts that bend stock","Bolt-in with hand tools"],"cons":["HD kit is $600+","Over 4\" lift requires a dropped pitman arm to keep drag-link geometry","Pickle fork removal damages boots \u2014 use a proper separator","Always follow with an alignment to nail camber and caster"]},{"id":"tj-track-bar-tj","title":"Track Bar Replacement \u2014 TJ Specifics","vehicle":"tj","category":"steering","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":150,"high":400},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","21mm socket","18mm socket","breaker bar","torque wrench","tape measure"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","measurement"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["death-wobble","axle-centering","steering-geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Synergy TJ/LJ adjustable front track bar","part_number":"8520-01","vendor":"Synergy Manufacturing","price_estimate_usd":360},{"name":"Currie RockJock Johnny Joint adjustable track bar","part_number":"CE-9120TJJ","vendor":"Currie Enterprises","price_estimate_usd":330},{"name":"Rough Country adjustable front track bar","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":160},{"name":"Mopar OEM replacement","part_number":"52088433AB","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":145}],"citations":[{"title":"Synergy TJ Adjustable Front Track Bar","url":"https://www.synergymfg.com/synergy-jeep-tjlj-xj-zj-mj-adjustable-front-track-bar.html"},{"title":"RockJock TJ Trac Bars","url":"https://www.rockjock4x4.com/products/shop-by-vehicle/jeep-tj/jeep-tj-trac-bars"},{"title":"TJ Front Track Bar Torque","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/front-track-bar-torque-specs.12653/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"The TJ front track bar locates your axle side-to-side; if you've added a lift over 2\" or you feel highway wander, replace it with an adjustable HD bar to re-center the axle and stop death wobble.","description":"The TJ front track bar uses a tapered-stud mount on the frame side and a bolt-through bracket on the axle side. The factory bar is fine at stock height, but when you lift the front the axle shifts toward the driver side because the bar pulls it that way through the lift arc. After a 2\"\u20134\" lift, an adjustable bar is required if you want the steering to feel right.\n\nThe TJ has one quirk worth knowing: the factory frame-side mount uses a tapered hole. Some aftermarket bars (Synergy, RockJock) keep the tapered fitment; others (cheaper bars) convert it to a straight through-bolt and require drilling the axle bracket to 9/16\". The drilled-out conversion is permanent. If you want to keep the option of reverting to a stock bar, choose a bar that preserves the taper.\n\nBrand picks: Synergy 8520-01 ($360) \u2014 dual-durometer bushings, Teflon bearing, maintenance-free, quiet ride. RockJock CE-9120TJJ ($330) \u2014 4130 chromoly tube with rebuildable Johnny Joints, the choice for hard wheeling. Rough Country adjustable ($160) \u2014 budget option, urethane bushings, fine for daily-driver lifts under 3\". For stock-height TJs replacing a worn factory bar, OEM Mopar 52088433AB ($145) is a direct swap.\n\nSymptoms that you need a new track bar: steering wheel off-center on a straight road, axle visibly off-center between the wheel wells, clunking over bumps from worn bushings, or active death wobble after hitting a bump at 45+ mph. Death wobble has multiple causes (worn ball joints, loose tie rod, bent steering link), but a worn track bar is the single most common trigger.\n\nFinal torque happens at ride height. The factory spec is 74 ft-lbs at the frame side and 55 ft-lbs at the axle bracket \u2014 these are lower than JK numbers because the TJ uses smaller hardware. Aftermarket bars with larger bolts will specify their own torque, often 100\u2013125 ft-lbs. Always follow the bar manufacturer's torque spec for the supplied hardware.","steps":["Park on level ground, set parking brake, chock rear wheels.","Loosen front lug nuts, lift front, set on jack stands under frame, remove wheels.","Measure frame rail to inner tire edge on both sides \u2014 record.","Support front axle on floor jack to take tension off the track bar.","Remove the axle-side bolt first (typically 18mm head).","Remove the frame-side bolt (21mm) \u2014 note tapered stud fitment if applicable.","Compare new bar to old at equal length as starting point.","Install frame side first, then axle side, finger-tight.","Reinstall wheels, lower to ride height.","Torque to manufacturer spec at ride height.","Re-measure axle centering \u2014 adjust bar length if off by more than 1/8\".","Drive 50 miles, re-check all torque."],"pros":["Cures TJ death wobble from worn factory bar","Adjustable bar re-centers axle after lift","HD bars resist bending on rocks","Bolt-in install if you choose a bar that keeps the tapered mount"],"cons":["Some bars require drilling the axle bracket \u2014 irreversible","Tapered-stud mount can seize and be hard to remove","Torque-at-ride-height step is critical or bushings wear early","Bar replacement alone won't fix death wobble if ball joints or tie rod are also worn"]},{"id":"tj-budget-lift","title":"Budget Lift Kit \u2014 Coil Spacers and Entry Springs (\u2264$600)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":129,"high":599},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","spring compressor"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","budget","coil-spacer","entry-level"],"parts":[{"name":"Daystar 2\" Coil Spacers KJ09123BK (front and rear, polyurethane)","vendor":"Daystar Products","price_estimate_usd":129},{"name":"Rough Country 2\" Budget Lift Kit TJ 674","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":189},{"name":"Skyjacker 3.5\" Suspension Lift Kit with Hydro Shocks TJ35K","vendor":"Skyjacker","price_estimate_usd":599}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Budget Lift Discussion","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/budget-lift-tj-wrangler/"},{"title":"Rough Country TJ 2-Inch Lift Installation Guide","url":"https://roughcountry.com/installation-instructions"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-shocks-upgrade"],"summary":"A budget lift gets you off the ground without raiding the build fund \u2014 but know exactly what you're not getting before you buy.","description":"The TJ's coil-spring front suspension makes spacer lifts genuinely easy to install compared to leaf-sprung rigs. A 2\" coil spacer drops on top of the existing spring perch in roughly a Saturday afternoon and costs less than a decent set of floor mats. For drivers who want mild clearance gains, a more aggressive stance, or room for 31\" or 32\" tires, a budget lift is a legitimate starting point \u2014 not a compromise you'll regret for long.\n\nWhat the budget tier doesn't include matters as much as what it does. Most kits under $300 ship with no geometry correction hardware. The TJ's front track bar runs at the stock angle for a stock ride height; raise the body 2\" without relocating the drag link and track bar and you've introduced bump steer and steering wander. It won't be catastrophic at 2\", but it's real and noticeable at highway speed. Skyjacker's 3.5\" kit at the top of this range does include a track bar drop bracket \u2014 it's worth the extra cost if you're planning to stay at this level for more than one season.\n\nThe Rough Country 2\" kit ($189) is a reliable, widely-used entry point that gets 31\" tires under the stock flares without rubbing at full flex. Expect softer-than-stock ride quality from the replacement coil springs; Rough Country specs for value, not articulation or on-road compliance. Daystar's polyurethane spacers ($129) are the true floor-entry option \u2014 they sit on top of your existing springs and do nothing for shock travel. If your stock shocks are in good shape and you're not planning trails with significant flex, spacers are perfectly adequate. If your shocks are tired, replace them at the same time or you'll be back under the rig in six months.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose before lifting the vehicle.","Lift and support the vehicle on jack stands at the frame rails \u2014 never under the axles when removing suspension components.","Support the front axle with a floor jack before disconnecting sway bar end links.","For coil spacers: disconnect the track bar from the axle bracket and carefully lower the axle to allow spring removal. Seat the spacer on the spring perch; reinstall spring above it.","For full replacement springs (Rough Country, Skyjacker): use a spring compressor if required. Install new coil springs per manufacturer instructions.","Repeat for rear coils.","Install included track bar drop bracket if kit provides one.","Torque all fasteners to spec: track bar bolt 74 ft-lbs, sway bar end link nuts 11 ft-lbs, lower control arm bolts 130 ft-lbs.","Lower the vehicle and torque wheel lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs.","Drive 100 miles and re-torque all suspension fasteners.","Get a front-end alignment \u2014 camber and toe will have shifted."],"pros":["Lowest barrier to entry; most installs require no specialty tools beyond a spring compressor","Gets 31\"\u201332\" tires under the fender without significant trimming","Bolt-on install is fully reversible","Most budget kits maintain a recognizable ride quality on pavement"],"cons":["No geometry correction at the 2\" level \u2014 track bar and drag link run at factory angles, creating mild bump steer","Budget spring rates are typically softer than factory, not stiffer \u2014 not ideal for loaded overland builds","Spacers reduce effective shock travel even with stock-length shocks","At 2.5\" and above, the NP231 slip yoke becomes an issue at highway speed \u2014 plan for an SYE kit down the road","Skyjacker 3.5\" kits with hydro shocks are an entry-grade shock \u2014 upgrade shocks before serious trail use"]},{"id":"tj-front-coil-spring","title":"TJ Front Coil Spring Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":120,"high":500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","13mm socket","15mm socket","18mm socket","T55 Torx","torque wrench","pry bar"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension disassembly"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["coils","lift","ride-quality"],"parts":[{"name":"Mopar OEM front coil TJ","part_number":"52087789","vendor":"Mopar","price_estimate_usd":105},{"name":"Old Man Emu front coil 2\" lift (heavy)","part_number":"2937","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":150},{"name":"Currie 3\" front coil pair","vendor":"Currie","price_estimate_usd":220},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" front coil set","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Steps for replacing front coil springs \u2014 TJ Forum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/steps-for-replacing-front-coil-springs.13739/"},{"title":"2003 Jeep TJ Torque Specifications \u2014 Wandering Trail","url":"http://www.wanderingtrail.com/Mechanical/TJLJ/2003_Jeep_Torques.htm"},{"title":"Stock Spring Swap Walk-Thru \u2014 TJ Forum","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/stock-spring-swap-walk-thru-with-pics.12655/"}],"alternatives":["tj-rear-leaf-replacement","tj-shock-replacement","tj-lift-options"],"summary":"Front TJ coils come out the top of the spring perch once you drop the axle and disconnect the track bar \u2014 no spring compressor needed for stock or up to 3.5\" coils.","description":"The TJ uses a coil-sprung four-link front suspension that descends directly from the XJ, but with shorter coil pockets and a panhard (track) bar that has to come loose before the axle can drop far enough to free the springs. Almost every TJ coil swap turns into a half-hour fight at the track bar because the bolt is captured in a tight bracket and the bushing is corroded onto the sleeve. Spray it with penetrant the night before and the job goes faster.\n\nPricing splits cleanly: OEM Mopar coils are around $100\u2013$120 each, used to refresh a sagging stock rig. Aftermarket 2\"\u20133\" lift coils run $200\u2013$350 a pair. Old Man Emu 2937 heavy-rate fronts are the daily-driver favorite for owners running a winch or steel front bumper; Currie and Rubicon Express are the mid-tier choices; Rock Krawler and Clayton sit above $400 with progressive-wound springs. For 4\"+ lifts you'd be doing this swap as part of a full kit with longer shocks and adjustable control arms.\n\nThe TJ's geometry gets touchy past 3.5\" because the factory control arms are short and the panhard angle steepens as the axle drops. Going beyond 3\" of coil lift without adjustable arms will leave the axle pulled to the passenger side and the caster down around 1\u00b0\u20132\u00b0 (factory spec is 7\u00b0). You'll feel the wandering on the freeway before you make it home from the install. Either keep the lift at 3\" or budget for adjustable control arms and a longer or adjustable track bar in the same project.\n\nDrop the axle slowly. The coils are captive in the lower spring pocket by a retainer clip but the upper pocket is just an isolator. Once the axle drops about 5 inches the coil tension is gone and the spring will sit loose in the pocket. Don't let it fall out and bend the lower isolator. After install, the bumpstops should sit roughly 1\" off the axle pad with the rig on the ground \u2014 if they're touching, the coils are sagging or the wrong rate. If there's a 3\"+ gap, you may need bumpstop extensions to prevent the shock bottoming out before the bumpstop does.\n\nTorque specs that matter on this job: front shock at axle 21 ft-lb (this is correct, not a typo \u2014 it's a small bolt), spring retainer clip 16 ft-lb, sway bar end link 75 ft-lb, track bar at frame 40 ft-lb, track bar at axle 125 ft-lb, lower control arm at axle 130 ft-lb, lower control arm at frame 115 ft-lb.","steps":["Loosen front lug nuts. Spray track bar bolt and sway bar end link bolt with penetrant.","Lift the front of the Jeep, support on stands under the frame just behind the front axle.","Remove front wheels. Place a floor jack under the differential.","Disconnect one sway bar end link at the axle (18mm, T55). One side is enough \u2014 keeps the axle from twisting.","Disconnect the track bar at the axle bracket (21mm).","Unbolt both front shocks at the axle (13mm long bolt, 10mm box end on shaft).","Slowly lower the floor jack to drop the axle.","Once coils lose tension (~5\" of drop), remove the spring retainer clip at the lower seat (13mm).","Tilt the coil and pull it out the top.","Install the new coil \u2014 seat it in the upper isolator and lower spring pad.","Reinstall the retainer clip (16 ft-lb).","Jack the axle back up, reconnect shocks, track bar, sway bar end link. Snug only.","Lower the Jeep to the ground.","Final-torque: shocks 21 ft-lb, track bar 125 ft-lb at axle and 40 ft-lb at frame, sway bar end link 75 ft-lb.","Get an alignment. Caster target: 5\u00b0\u20137\u00b0."],"pros":["Stock or 3\"-or-less coils swap with no spring compressor","Bolt-in with hand tools","Wide aftermarket \u2014 heavy-rate fronts available for built-up rigs","Doesn't require pulling brake lines"],"cons":["Track bar bolt is the bottleneck \u2014 penetrant the night before","3.5\"+ lifts pull caster out of spec; need adjustable arms","Bumpstop height after install needs to be checked","Always get an alignment after \u2014 caster shift is unavoidable"]},{"id":"tj-lift-options","title":"TJ Lift Kit Options \u2014 Coil vs. Long Arm","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":20},"cost_usd":{"low":400,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","spring compressor (or coil pull method)","metric sockets","torque wrench","welder (long arm only)"],"skills_required":["suspension R&R","alignment basics","welding (long arm only)"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","geometry","build-planning"],"parts":[{"name":"Rough Country 2.5\" budget boost coil spacer kit","part_number":"65530","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":250},{"name":"Old Man Emu 2.5\" coil and shock kit","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":1200},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" Super-Flex short arm kit","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":1400},{"name":"Clayton Off Road long arm upgrade kit (4.5\")","vendor":"Clayton Off Road","price_estimate_usd":2800},{"name":"Metalcloak 3.5\" Game-Changer short arm kit","vendor":"Metalcloak","price_estimate_usd":1800}],"citations":[{"title":"Choosing the Right Jeep Lift Kit: Long vs. Short Arm Lift Kits","url":"https://dixie4wheeldrive.com/choosing_right_jeep_lift_kit_long_vs_short_arm_lift_kits/"},{"title":"Long arm vs adjustable short arm worth the $$$?","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/long-arm-vs-adjustable-short-arm-worth-the.334/"},{"title":"Metalcloak TJ Long Travel Short Arm Lift Kit","url":"https://metalcloak.com/tj-long-travel-short-arm-suspension-system-lift-kit.html"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Up to 3.5 inches of lift on a TJ, stay with a short arm kit and adjustable arms \u2014 it's cheaper, bolts in, and rides fine. At 4 inches and beyond, the stock control arm angles get steep enough that a long arm upgrade is worth the welding and the money.","description":"The TJ runs a coil-sprung suspension at all four corners, which makes lift kits cleaner than the leaf-sprung XJ. The catch is the short factory control arms: when you lift the body, the axle pivots forward through an arc, caster drops, and the driveshaft angles steepen. Coil spacers ignore all of that and just push the body up. New taller coils with matched shocks address ride height correctly. Adjustable control arms fix axle position and caster. Long arms fix the geometry by moving the control arm pivot rearward, which flattens the arm angle and brings the suspension closer to factory geometry at full lift height.\n\nTiers worth knowing about: a budget coil spacer kit from Rough Country runs about $250 and gives 2 to 2.5 inches \u2014 fine for a daily driver clearing 31s, terrible if you want to actually flex on a trail. A mid-range new-coil kit (Old Man Emu Heavy 2.5\", Rubicon Express Super-Flex 3.5\", Metalcloak Game-Changer 3.5\") sits in the $1,200 to $1,800 range with shocks and adjustable arms \u2014 this is the sweet spot for the vast majority of TJ builds. A long arm upgrade from Clayton, Savvy, or Currie runs $2,500 to $3,500 plus install, requires welding new frame brackets onto the unibody, and only makes sense if you're building for 35-inch tires, 4.5+ inches of lift, or daily rock crawling. Rough Country's long arm kits are cheaper but cut corners on bushings and brackets \u2014 many TJ owners report squeaks and premature wear.\n\nThe single most important add-on at any lift height is the slip yoke eliminator (SYE) plus a CV rear driveshaft. At 3 inches and up, the stock NP231 transfer case output is at an angle the slip yoke can't handle, and you get vibration that no amount of u-joint cycling will fix. Budget another $700 to $1,000 for the SYE and driveshaft on top of the lift itself. A 3.5-inch short arm kit, slip yoke eliminator, adjustable track bars, and an alignment is the most common \"complete\" mid-range TJ build.\n\nDon't lift higher than you need. Each inch of lift pushes the center of gravity up, hurts on-road manners, and forces another bracket of compromises (adjustable track bar, longer brake lines, longer sway bar end links, possibly re-gearing). A 3-inch TJ on 33s is a more capable trail rig than a 6-inch TJ on 35s for most people.","steps":["Decide tire size first. Lift exists to clear the tires you want \u2014 not the other way around.","Pick a lift height: 2 to 2.5\" clears 31s and 32s, 3 to 3.5\" clears 33s, 4\"+ for 35s.","At 3\" and up, plan for: adjustable track bars (or relocation brackets), SYE and CV rear driveshaft, longer brake lines, longer sway bar links.","Pick coil spring rates appropriate to your weight \u2014 front winch bumper and rear tire carrier require heavy springs.","Install kit per manufacturer instructions. Torque all suspension bolts at ride height, not at droop.","Get a four-wheel alignment after install. Caster should land between 4 and 6 degrees.","Re-check torque after the first 100 miles on the trail."],"pros":["Coil suspension means lifts are usually bolt-in up to 3.5\"","Mid-range short arm kits give 95% of the long arm benefit at half the cost","TJ aftermarket support is deep \u2014 every brand makes a kit","Adjustable arms recover caster without welding"],"cons":["Cheap coil spacer kits ride harshly and don't add wheel travel","Long arm kits require welding and frame fabrication","Any lift over 3\" needs SYE plus CV driveshaft, adding $700\u2013$1,000","Lift adds rollover risk if you stack lift on lift on tires"]},{"id":"tj-long-arm-lift","title":"Long-Arm Lift System \u2014 4.5\"+ for Rock Crawling ($2,000+)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":5,"time_hours":{"low":16,"high":24},"cost_usd":{"low":1800,"high":3500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","angle grinder","welder (for some kits)","drill press","alignment tool","pry bar"],"skills_required":["suspension work","fabrication basics","torque specification knowledge","welding (kit-dependent)"],"build_tiers":["moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","long-arm","articulation","rock-crawler","geometry"],"parts":[{"name":"Iron Rock Offroad 4.5\" Long-Arm Lift System TJ (bolt-on, LCG skid-mount)","vendor":"Iron Rock Offroad","price_estimate_usd":2199},{"name":"Rock Krawler Max Travel 3.5\" 3-Link Front Suspension TJ","vendor":"Rock Krawler","price_estimate_usd":2499},{"name":"Rubicon Express 4.5\" Super-Flex LCG Long-Arm Kit TJ RE7130","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":2899}],"citations":[{"title":"Pirate4x4 \u2014 TJ Long-Arm vs Short-Arm Suspension Build Thread","url":"https://www.pirate4x4.com/forum/jeep-wrangler-tj/tj-long-arm-suspension/"},{"title":"Iron Rock Offroad TJ Long-Arm Installation Instructions","url":"https://www.ironrockoffroad.com/instructions"},{"title":"Rock Krawler TJ Max Travel System Overview","url":"https://www.rockkrawler.com/tj"}],"alternatives":["tj-mid-lift","tj-regear","tj-locker-rear"],"summary":"Long arms fix what short arms can't \u2014 if the TJ is your dedicated rock rig, this is the upgrade that unlocks its full suspension geometry.","description":"Short-arm suspension \u2014 the factory layout \u2014 uses relatively short control arms that create a tight, responsive geometry at stock ride height. Raise the axle 3.5\"\u20134.5\" on short arms and the pinion angle, caster correction, and anti-squat geometry all degrade at full droop. The axle wants to rotate forward under compression and backward under droop, creating handling that's noticeably compromised on aggressive terrain. Long arms solve this by moving the front and rear pivot points forward (farther from the axle), which flattens the arc of axle travel and keeps the pinion angle stable through the full range of suspension travel.\n\nThe practical result is dramatic: a well-built TJ long-arm system delivers more axle droop, less pinion angle change through the suspension cycle, and far better on-trail predictability than a short-arm setup at the same lift height. On rock crawling terrain \u2014 ledges, V-ditches, side-hills \u2014 this translates directly to keeping tires on the ground when the terrain gets uneven. The trade-off is installation complexity and cost. Most long-arm systems require cutting factory crossmembers, welding in new mounts, or bolting in a new LCG (low center of gravity) skid crossmember that replaces the factory unit and provides the new long-arm pivot points.\n\nLow Center of Gravity (LCG) designs, used by Rubicon Express and Iron Rock Offroad, drop the front control arm mounting points as low as possible while running longer arms. This geometry lowers the TJ's center of gravity compared to a conventional high-mount long-arm, which meaningfully improves side-hill stability \u2014 relevant on any trail with off-camber sections. Rock Krawler's 3-link front is a different approach: a panhard bar replaces the track bar and a third upper link controls axle rotation, which allows even longer effective arm length without the geometry compromises of a standard 4-link.\n\nPlan for the full build to take an entire weekend for an experienced mechanic. Rear driveshaft replacement (SYE + rear CV shaft) is required at this lift height and should be bundled into the project. Wheel alignment is mandatory after installation.","steps":["Photograph all existing suspension geometry before disassembly \u2014 caster angle, pinion angle, and control arm orientation are reference points.","Raise the vehicle at the frame rails; support on jack stands at all four corners. Support both axles on separate floor jacks.","Drain transfer case if the skid crossmember must be removed for the LCG bracket.","Remove factory front and rear control arms, track bar, and (for LCG kits) factory crossmember.","Cut or remove factory control arm mounts per kit instructions \u2014 most kits specify which mounts to trim or remove entirely.","Install new LCG crossmember / frame brackets per kit; torque to specification.","Install long-arm control arms with new mounting hardware \u2014 do not fully torque until suspension is loaded.","Install new coil springs and shocks (included in kit or spec'd by kit documentation).","Reconnect sway bar end links and brake lines; check brake line length \u2014 extended lines are required at this lift height.","Lower the vehicle to the ground (suspension loaded) and torque all control arm bolts: front lower 130 ft-lbs, front upper 75 ft-lbs, rear lower 130 ft-lbs, rear upper 75 ft-lbs.","Set caster at 4\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 positive using adjustable upper control arms.","Verify pinion angle \u2014 should be 1\u00b0\u20133\u00b0 downward from horizontal at full droop.","Install SYE kit and rear CV driveshaft before driving.","Alignment shop visit is required \u2014 request a 4-wheel alignment with caster correction.","Drive 50 miles on pavement, re-torque all suspension fasteners."],"pros":["Dramatically improves axle articulation and droop compared to short-arm kits at the same lift height","Stable pinion angle through full suspension travel reduces U-joint wear and vibration","LCG designs lower the vehicle's center of gravity relative to conventional long-arm mounting","Rock Krawler 3-link eliminates track bar geometry limitations entirely","Best-in-class geometry for serious rock crawling builds"],"cons":["Installation requires partial frame modification on most kits \u2014 not fully reversible without significant effort","Welding skills may be required depending on kit and installer preference","Total project cost including SYE, rear driveshaft, extended brake lines, and alignment can reach $3,500\u2013$4,500","Long control arms can reduce departure angle and ground clearance at the frame if not paired with proper skid plates","Requires front-end alignment by a shop familiar with modified TJ geometry","Not appropriate for mild trail or mostly-street builds \u2014 this is a committed rock rig upgrade"]},{"id":"tj-mid-lift","title":"Mid-Tier Lift Kit \u2014 3\"\u20134\" Spring Lift ($600\u2013$1,500)","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"kit","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":6,"high":10},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1500},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","torque wrench","socket set","spring compressor","ball joint press","alignment tool"],"skills_required":["suspension work","basic wrenching","torque specification knowledge"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["lift","suspension","mid-lift","coil-spring","geometry-correction"],"parts":[{"name":"OME 3\" Lift Kit with Nitrocharger Sport Shocks (TJ front/rear coils + shocks)","vendor":"Old Man Emu / ARB","price_estimate_usd":649},{"name":"Rubicon Express 3.5\" Super-Flex Suspension Lift Kit RE7000","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":749},{"name":"Rough Country 4\" Suspension Lift Kit with Premium N3 Shocks TJ 68230","vendor":"Rough Country","price_estimate_usd":699}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 OME vs Rubicon Express TJ Lift Comparison","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/ome-vs-rubicon-express-tj/"},{"title":"Rubicon Express TJ Super-Flex Installation Guide","url":"https://www.rubconexpress.com/instructions"},{"title":"4WD Hardware \u2014 TJ Suspension Lift Overview","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/TJ-Wrangler-Suspension-Lifts"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-long-arm-lift","tj-shocks-upgrade"],"summary":"The 3\"\u20134\" lift is where the TJ starts performing like a trail rig \u2014 but it also introduces geometry problems that a quality kit must address to drive right on the road.","description":"At 3.5\" of lift the TJ genuinely transforms. You gain clearance for 33\" tires without fender trimming, meaningful flex improvement, and a ride height that holds its own on moderate trails. The problem this lift height introduces \u2014 and that distinguishes a good mid-tier kit from a budget one \u2014 is caster angle correction and track bar geometry. As the axle drops away from the body, the front axle's caster angle decreases, causing wandering and poor return-to-center steering. Every quality kit at this level includes adjustable upper control arms or cam bolts for caster correction.\n\nThe OME 3\" kit is the overland standard for a reason. Old Man Emu builds their coil rates around real-world load calculations, and the Nitrocharger Sport shock is a genuine upgrade over most stock units. The TJ OME setup delivers a controlled, comfortable ride on pavement that budget kits rarely match. It's not the most aggressive lift \u2014 3\" is the stated height \u2014 but OME spring rates are consistent and predictable over time, and they don't sag the way soft budget springs do after 30,000 miles.\n\nRubicon Express's 3.5\" Super-Flex kit leans toward articulation over on-road comfort. The Super-Flex mono-tube shocks and modified spring rates produce noticeably more droop than the OME setup, which pays off on technical trails with uneven terrain. The kit includes a track bar drop bracket and adjustable front upper control arms for caster correction. If you're building for moderate trail use over pavement comfort, this is the better choice. Rough Country's 4\" kit rounds out the tier with more lift height for less money \u2014 the N3 shocks are serviceable, but plan to upgrade them if you push the rig harder than light dirt roads.\n\nAt any lift over 2.5\", plan to add an SYE kit for the NP231 transfer case. Without it, the slip yoke output causes driveline vibration above 45 mph that gets worse with lift. It's not an emergency fix, but it's a known issue you'll want to budget for.","steps":["Break lug nuts loose before lifting the vehicle.","Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support securely on jack stands at all four corners.","Support the front axle independently on a floor jack.","Disconnect sway bar end links and front track bar from the axle bracket.","Disconnect front upper and lower control arms from axle (if kit includes replacement control arms).","Lower the front axle carefully to release coil spring tension; remove old springs.","Install new coil springs; reconnect control arms hand-tight.","Install track bar drop bracket per kit instructions; reconnect track bar.","Repeat spring swap for rear coils (rear control arms may also need adjustment depending on kit).","Install new shocks \u2014 front and rear \u2014 per manufacturer instructions.","Torque all control arm bolts and track bar bolts with the suspension loaded (vehicle on ground). Front lower control arm: 130 ft-lbs. Track bar: 74 ft-lbs. Sway bar end links: 11 ft-lbs.","Set caster angle using adjustable upper control arms or cam bolts per kit spec (typically 4\u00b0\u20137\u00b0 positive caster on the TJ).","Drive 100 miles, re-torque all fasteners.","Schedule a full 4-wheel alignment immediately after install."],"pros":["Quality kits include geometry correction hardware (track bar drop bracket, adjustable upper control arms)","3.5\" clears 33\" tires without trimming; 35s are possible with minor flare trimming","OME kit provides one of the best on-road ride qualities in this price range","Rubicon Express Super-Flex provides trail articulation that approaches long-arm performance","Kit components are widely available and well-documented for DIY builds"],"cons":["Requires a professional front-end alignment after install","Lifts above 2.5\" require SYE kit + rear CV driveshaft to eliminate driveshaft vibration","At 4\", stock brake lines may be marginal \u2014 inspect and replace extended lines if needed","Budget shocks in lower-cost kits (Rough Country N3) are adequate but not trail-capable long-term","Adjustable upper control arms require periodic re-checking of caster after significant trail use"]},{"id":"tj-rear-leaf-springs","title":"Rear Leaf Spring Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","subcategory":"springs","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":5},"cost_usd":{"low":250,"high":700},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","metric sockets","breaker bar","torque wrench","pry bar","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["suspension disassembly","torque sequencing"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["springs","lift","ride-quality","rear-suspension"],"parts":[{"name":"Old Man Emu CS049R Medium Load Rear Leaf (TJ)","part_number":"CS049R","vendor":"ARB / OME","price_estimate_usd":285},{"name":"BDS 2\" Rear Leaf Spring (TJ)","vendor":"BDS Suspension","price_estimate_usd":320},{"name":"Rubicon Express Standard Coil Rear Lift (TJ uses coils, not leaves \u2014 note: TJ is coil-rear)","vendor":"Rubicon Express","price_estimate_usd":0},{"name":"Rancho RS44113 TJ Rear Leaf (NOTE: TJ rear is coil \u2014 see body)","vendor":"Rancho","price_estimate_usd":0}],"citations":[{"title":"Old Man Emu YJ Rear Leaf CS036R reference","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/products/16090_5058.htm"},{"title":"Wrangler Forum \u2014 TJ vs YJ leaf spring discussion","url":"https://www.wranglerforum.com/threads/jeep-leaf-springs.588145/"}],"alternatives":["tj-coil-spring-tj","tj-lift-options-tj"],"summary":"The TJ is a coil-sprung Wrangler \u2014 if you're searching for a TJ \"rear leaf spring,\" you've either got an LJ-to-leaf conversion build, or you're confusing the TJ with the older YJ Wrangler. This guide covers both situations.","description":"Stock TJ Wranglers (1997-2006) and LJ Unlimiteds (2004-2006) use coil springs front and rear \u2014 not leaves. That's the headline. If you came into the garage expecting leaf-spring perches under the rear axle, you've got a YJ (1987-1995) or an aftermarket leaf-conversion kit. The coil-sprung rear is one of the TJ's biggest advantages over the YJ: better articulation, smoother ride, easier to lift.\n\nThat said, there are two real reasons to land on this page. First: you own a TJ with an aftermarket leaf-spring conversion (rare, usually shop-built for racing or specific suspension geometry). Second: you're cross-shopping a TJ with a YJ build and want to understand the differences. The TJ's coil-and-track-bar geometry is covered in detail in our [TJ Coil Spring Replacement guide](tj/suspension/coil-spring-tj.md). If your goal is rear lift, you want coil springs, not leaves.\n\nFor the rare TJ-with-leaves build (typically a Currie or shop-built setup): leaf packs are sized by load rating and arch. Old Man Emu, BDS, and Skyjacker make spec-matched leaves for YJ that physically bolt to a TJ-to-leaf conversion frame bracket \u2014 but you need to confirm bushing eye diameter and arch height against your specific conversion kit. Mopar OE leaves do not exist for the TJ because the platform was never coil-rear from the factory.\n\nReplacement procedure (for the conversion build): support the frame, remove the U-bolts holding the axle to the spring pack, swing the spring forward off the front eye bolt, remove the rear shackle, replace the spring pack with the new one, torque U-bolts to 90-100 ft-lb in a cross pattern. Re-torque after 500 miles \u2014 leaf packs settle.\n\nIf you bought a TJ and want a 2-3\" rear lift, you're shopping coil springs. Skip this guide and go to the coil spring page.","steps":["Confirm your TJ actually has leaf springs in the rear (look for a multi-leaf pack between axle and frame). Stock TJ = coils, not leaves.","If coil-rear: stop. Go to the TJ coil spring guide.","If leaf-converted: chock front wheels; lift the rear; support frame on jack stands behind the axle.","Place a floor jack under the axle for support.","Spray penetrant on U-bolt nuts; let sit 10 minutes.","Loosen and remove U-bolt nuts; remove U-bolt plate and U-bolts.","Loosen the front eye bolt and rear shackle bolts.","Lower the axle to relieve tension on the spring pack.","Swing the spring forward off the front eye; remove the shackle; pull the spring out.","Install the new spring: front eye onto front mount bolt first, then rear into the shackle.","Center the axle on the spring perch (use the center pin alignment hole).","Reinstall U-bolts and plate; torque to 90-100 ft-lb in a cross pattern.","Torque front eye bolt to 80 ft-lb; shackle bolts to 55 ft-lb.","Drive 500 miles; re-torque U-bolts."],"pros":["Cheaper than coil-and-track-bar replacement (for conversion builds)","Leaf packs are tough and tolerant of bad geometry","Easy to add load capacity by adding leaves"],"cons":["TJ was not designed for leaves \u2014 converting from coils gives up articulation","Confusing platform mismatch: most TJ owners actually want the coil guide","Aftermarket conversion brackets are not common; parts harder to source","Rides stiffer than coil-rear unless tuned with proper shocks"]},{"id":"tj-rocker-arm-bushing","title":"Rocker Arm Bushing Replacement","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"task","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":3,"high":6},"cost_usd":{"low":60,"high":280},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","bushing press or shop press","ball joint press kit","metric sockets","torque wrench","penetrating oil"],"skills_required":["intermediate wrenching","press tool use"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["suspension","bushings","control-arms","alignment"],"parts":[{"name":"Prothane front control arm bushing kit (poly)","part_number":"1-204","vendor":"Prothane","price_estimate_usd":65},{"name":"Energy Suspension front control arm bushing kit (poly)","part_number":"2.3106","vendor":"Energy Suspension","price_estimate_usd":80},{"name":"MOOG lower control arm bushing (rubber, OE-style)","part_number":"K7420","vendor":"MOOG","price_estimate_usd":42},{"name":"Currie / Clayton Johnny Joint replacement arms (full set)","vendor":"Currie/Clayton","price_estimate_usd":280}],"citations":[{"title":"Prothane 1-204 TJ Bushing Kit","url":"https://www.prothanesuspensionparts.com/1204"},{"title":"Energy Suspension 2.3106 TJ kit","url":"https://www.energysuspensionparts.com/2.3106"},{"title":"Control arm bushing replacement discussion (TJ Forum)","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/control-arm-bushing-replacement.72483/"}],"alternatives":["tj-coil-spring-tj","tj-tie-rods-tj"],"summary":"Worn control arm bushings cause vague steering, clunks over bumps, and uneven tire wear; replacement requires pressing the old sleeves out \u2014 most TJ owners replace the whole arm instead of fighting the press job.","description":"The TJ has eight control arm bushings \u2014 two per arm at each end, four arms total. They wear by 80,000-150,000 miles depending on use, and a lifted TJ that's seen mud or saltwater destroys them faster. Symptoms: clunking on direction changes, steering wander, premature inner-edge tire wear, and a \"loose\" feel that doesn't track straight on the highway. Grab each arm and try to twist it \u2014 significant rotational play at the bushing eye means it's done.\n\nThree real options:\n\n**Replace the bushings in your existing arms.** Cheapest at $60-$80 in parts, but the labor is the catch. The bushing sleeves are pressed into the arm eye and bonded with rubber. Burning the rubber out with a torch, drilling the sleeve, and cutting it with a sawzall is the proven method \u2014 a 12-ton shop press will handle the new bushing install. Plan four to six hours total. Poly bushings (Prothane 1-204, Energy 2.3106) transmit more road noise than rubber but last twice as long.\n\n**Replace the whole arms with new OE-spec ones.** Replacement arms with fresh bushings run $35-$50 each in stamped steel from MOOG or Dorman. Total: $140-200 for all four, no press work needed. This is the right call for most owners.\n\n**Upgrade to tubular arms with rebuildable joints.** Currie, Clayton, and JKS make tubular arms with Johnny Joints or similar \u2014 $280-$500 per pair. Rebuildable for life, much stronger than stock stamped arms, and necessary above a 3\" lift to correct geometry.\n\nWhatever you choose: torque to spec **with the suspension at ride height**, not with the wheels hanging. Torquing at full droop preloads the bushings and tears them apart within a year.","steps":["Park on level ground. Loosen the lug nuts before jacking.","Lift the front, set on jack stands under the frame. Support the axle with a floor jack so it stays at ride height.","Soak control arm bolts with penetrating oil. These are 18mm and notoriously rusty.","Remove the lower arm first \u2014 one bolt at the frame, one at the axle bracket. Repeat for the upper.","If pressing bushings: burn out rubber with a torch (in open air, away from fuel lines), drill the sleeve, slot it with a sawzall, collapse it inward, drive out. Press new bushings in with a shop press.","If swapping whole arms: install new arm, hand-thread both bolts.","Repeat for remaining arms. Switch to the rear and repeat \u2014 same process, different arm geometry.","Lower the vehicle so the suspension is at ride height. Torque all control arm bolts to 130 ft-lb.","Test drive at low speed checking for clunks. Get an alignment within 100 miles."],"pros":["Restores tight steering and bump feel","Stops premature tire wear","Replacing whole arms avoids the press job entirely","Poly bushings last twice as long as rubber"],"cons":["Pressing bushings is genuinely difficult \u2014 most owners pay a shop $100-150 per arm","Poly bushings transmit road noise","Must torque at ride height or you'll destroy the new bushings","Alignment required after"]},{"id":"tj-shocks-upgrade","title":"Shock Absorber Upgrade \u2014 OME, Bilstein, and Fox Options","vehicle":"tj","category":"suspension","type":"part","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":380,"high":900},"tools_required":["floor jack","jack stands","socket set","torque wrench","open-end wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","suspension work"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["shocks","suspension","dampers","ride-quality","trail-performance"],"parts":[{"name":"OME Nitrocharger Sport TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (90006 front / 90051 rear, for 3-3.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Old Man Emu / ARB","price_estimate_usd":420},{"name":"Bilstein 5100 TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (33-187084 front / 33-187079 rear, for 0-2.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Bilstein","price_estimate_usd":520},{"name":"Fox 2.0 Performance Series TJ Front and Rear Shock Set (for 2-3.5 inch lift)","vendor":"Fox Racing Shox","price_estimate_usd":720}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Shock Upgrade Guide: OME vs Bilstein vs Fox","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/shocks-upgrade-tj-ome-bilstein-fox/"},{"title":"ARB OME TJ Shock Fitment Guide","url":"https://arbusa.com/ome-tj-shocks"},{"title":"Bilstein 5100 TJ Product Page","url":"https://www.bilstein.com/us/en/products/5100-series"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-mid-lift","tj-long-arm-lift"],"summary":"Shock quality is where budget lift kits lose ground the fastest \u2014 replacing the included shocks with a set of OME, Bilstein, or Fox units is one of the highest-return investments you can make on a TJ.","description":"Shock absorbers are the component most budget lift kits shortchange. A spring moves the vehicle; a shock controls how fast it moves and damps the oscillation. An undersized or overvalved shock on a TJ produces a ride that either bobs continuously on pavement or bottoms out hard on trail impacts. The TJ's coil-spring suspension responds extremely well to quality shocks because the coil springs themselves have relatively linear rates; the damper is doing the majority of the ride-quality work.\n\nOME's Nitrocharger Sport remains the value standard for TJ overland builds. The gas-charged monotube design provides consistent damping through extended use, and OME calibrates their valving specifically for loaded TJs \u2014 they account for the extra weight of armor, tools, and camping gear that's typically on an overland rig. The OME set at $380\u2013$450 is the pick if you want the best ride quality on pavement with competent trail performance. OME shocks are sized for their own spring kits, so verify fitment against your lift height before purchasing.\n\nBilstein's 5100 series is a serious performance upgrade with a monotube design and zinc-plated body. Bilstein valves their shocks for consistent performance across the range from full bump to full droop \u2014 they feel firmer than OME on pavement but provide notably better control on rough trail surfaces where damper velocity is high. The 5100 is the choice for a TJ that's on technical trails regularly and needs a shock that can keep up with repeated high-frequency inputs.\n\nFox 2.0 Performance shocks are the upper tier in this category: larger 2.0\" diameter body, better heat dissipation on extended trail use, and adjustable rebound damping on some configurations. For builds at 3\"\u20134\" of lift with 35\" tires that see serious trail use, the Fox 2.0 is worth the premium. For anything milder, OME or Bilstein is the right answer.","steps":["Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support on jack stands. Keep the axle supported on a separate floor jack to allow adjustment of suspension height.","Spray all shock hardware with penetrating oil and allow 10\u201315 minutes to soak on any vehicle over five years old.","Remove the top shock mount nut while holding the shock shaft with a second wrench to prevent spinning.","Remove the lower shock mount bolt.","Slide out the old shock; slide in the new shock. Orient the shock so the reservoir (if present) is positioned per manufacturer instructions.","Install lower mount bolt hand-tight. Install top mount nut hand-tight.","Load the suspension to ride height (lower the axle jack until the vehicle rests normally) before torquing fasteners.","Torque lower shock mount bolts to 40\u201350 ft-lbs; torque upper mount nuts to 22\u201326 ft-lbs.","Repeat for all four corners.","Drive 50 miles and check all fasteners."],"pros":["OME Nitrocharger Sport provides the best on-pavement ride quality in the under-$500 price range for TJ applications","Bilstein 5100 provides consistent trail performance across a wide range of input speeds","Fox 2.0 Performance handles heat and repeated high-velocity inputs better than twin-tube designs at this lift height","All three brands are TJ-specific fitments \u2014 correct lengths and mounting geometry are well-documented","Shock upgrade is independent of spring changes \u2014 can be done as a standalone upgrade on any existing lift kit"],"cons":["Shock length must match lift height \u2014 purchasing shocks without verifying your actual lift height results in either binding at full bump or falling out at full droop","OME shocks are valved for their own spring kits; mixing OME shocks with non-OME springs may produce suboptimal results at the extremes","Fox 2.0 rebound adjustment requires understanding of damping theory to set correctly \u2014 incorrect adjustment produces worse results than a non-adjustable shock","All shock upgrades are per-axle \u2014 mismatching front and rear shock quality produces inconsistent handling","Shocks should be replaced in sets of four \u2014 a single replacement changes the handling balance"]},{"id":"tj-obd2-diagnostics","title":"TJ OBD-II Diagnostics \u2014 Reading Codes, Common Faults, and What They Mean","vehicle":"tj","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":0.25,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":20,"high":80},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner or code reader"],"skills_required":["basic electrical knowledge helpful but not required"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2002","2003-2006"],"tags":["diagnostic","OBD2","check-engine","PCM","tools_diagnostic"],"parts":[{"name":"Autel AL319 basic code reader","vendor":"Autel","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"BlueDriver Bluetooth OBD-II scanner (iOS/Android)","vendor":"BlueDriver","price_estimate_usd":100},{"name":"BAFX Products Bluetooth OBD-II adapter (Android)","vendor":"BAFX","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Innova 3100j code reader with live data","vendor":"Innova","price_estimate_usd":55}],"citations":[{"title":"Jeep Wrangler TJ OBD-II Code List \u2014 Jeep Service Manual","url":"https://wranglertjforum.com/threads/obd-ii-codes-and-descriptions.html"},{"title":"TJ PCM Diagnostics Overview \u2014 QuadraJeep","url":"https://www.quadratec.com/jeep-wrangler-tech/obd2-diagnostics"}],"alternatives":["tj-o2-sensor-replacement","tj-mass-airflow-sensor","tj-throttle-position-sensor"],"summary":"The TJ has been OBD-II compliant since 1997. Plug in a $20 code reader, pull the fault code, and you have the starting point for any check engine light diagnosis \u2014 don't pay a shop $80 to do that part.","description":"The TJ Wrangler uses Chrysler's OBD-II system through the PCM (powertrain control module). The diagnostic port (DLC) is under the dash on the driver's side, near the steering column. Any generic OBD-II scanner reads it.\n\nA check engine light on a TJ means the PCM has stored a Diagnostic Trouble Code (DTC). Some codes are serious; most are not. Reading the code is step one \u2014 it tells you where to look, not necessarily what to replace.\n\n**Common TJ fault codes and their realistic meaning:**\n\n- **P0300\u2013P0306** (Random/cylinder misfire): Ignition system first \u2014 cap, rotor, plug wires, plugs. The 4.0L burns through plug wires every 60\u201380K miles. Cap and rotor failure is also common.\n- **P0171** (System lean, Bank 1): Check for vacuum leaks (cracked intake boot, cracked TPS-area hoses), a dirty MAF sensor, or a weak fuel pump. On high-mileage engines it can also be worn injectors.\n- **P0122/P0123** (Throttle Position Sensor range): TPS failure is common on the TJ 4.0L. Erratic idle, hesitation on acceleration, and transmission shift points that feel off are the symptoms. New TPS is a $15\u2013$30 part.\n- **P0351\u2013P0356** (Ignition coil circuit): The TJ 4.0L uses a distributor-based ignition. A failing ASD (automatic shutdown) relay can trigger these. Also check spark plug wires for shorts to ground.\n- **P0138/P0141** (O2 sensor downstream): The downstream (post-cat) O2 sensor on TJs fails regularly at 100,000+ miles. It monitors catalyst efficiency and this code often appears with no drivability symptoms \u2014 just the light.\n- **P0340** (Camshaft position sensor circuit): The cam position sensor on the 4.0L is inside the distributor. If this code is active with hard starting or stalling, the distributor may need replacement.\n- **P1391** (Intermittent loss of CMP or CKP): This TJ-specific code is almost always the crankshaft position sensor or its wiring. Common failure mode on high-mileage 4.0Ls.\n\nA code reader (Autel AL319, $22) pulls stored and pending codes and clears the light. That's enough for most diagnostics. A scanner with live data (Innova 3100j, BlueDriver) lets you watch O2 sensor response, MAP pressure, coolant temp, and fuel trims in real time \u2014 worth the extra $30\u2013$50 if you're doing your own maintenance regularly.\n\nBluetooth adapters (BAFX, Veepeak) pair with free apps like Torque Pro on Android. They can't do everything a dedicated scanner does, but for code reading and live PIDs they're fully functional.\n\n- Clearing codes without fixing the underlying issue is pointless \u2014 the light will return within one drive cycle.\n- Multiple codes together tell a better story than one code in isolation. P0171 + P0174 together means fuel delivery or a large vacuum leak; P0171 alone could be one small vacuum leak or a lazy O2 sensor.\n- The Jeep has to complete a \"drive cycle\" for monitors to reset after clearing. Most monitors require highway and city driving, several cold starts, and some idle time. Inspection stations will fail the Jeep for an incomplete monitor even if no codes are present.\n\n- Autel AL319 (basic): $20\u2013$25 on Amazon\n- Innova 3100j (with live data): $50\u2013$60 on Amazon\n- BlueDriver (full diagnostics + repair reports): $100 on Amazon or Best Buy\n- Many auto parts stores (AutoZone, O'Reilly) will read codes for free with their loaner scanner","steps":["Locate the DLC port \u2014 driver's side, under the dash, near the steering column.","Plug in the scanner with the ignition OFF.","Turn key to ON (don't crank). The scanner will power on.","Select \"Read Codes\" on the scanner menu.","Note all stored codes (confirmed faults) and pending codes (one-trip faults that haven't reached confirmed threshold).","Research the specific code before replacing parts. The code points to a system or circuit, not always to the failed component.","After diagnosis and repair, clear codes and drive through one full drive cycle to confirm the light doesn't return."]},{"id":"tj-vacuum-leak-diagnosis","title":"TJ Vacuum Leak Diagnosis \u2014 Finding and Fixing Intake Leaks","vehicle":"tj","category":"tools_diagnostic","type":"diagnostic","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":3},"cost_usd":{"low":5,"high":35},"tools_required":["OBD-II scanner","propane torch (unlit)","carb cleaner spray","smoke machine (optional)","vacuum hand pump"],"skills_required":["basic diagnostic reading","intake system familiarity"],"build_tiers":["daily_driver","mild_trail","moderate_trail","overland"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tools_diagnostic","vacuum-leak","idle","diagnostic","drivability"],"parts":[{"name":"Dorman vacuum hose kit (universal assortment)","vendor":"Dorman","price_estimate_usd":15},{"name":"3M intake manifold gasket set \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Fel-Pro","price_estimate_usd":22},{"name":"Throttle body intake boot \u2014 TJ 4.0","vendor":"Dorman / OEM","price_estimate_usd":28}],"citations":[{"title":"How to Find a Vacuum Leak","url":"https://www.cars.com/articles/how-to-find-a-vacuum-leak/"},{"title":"TJ 4.0 idle surge and vacuum leak TSB","url":"https://www.wranglertjforum.com/forum/"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"A vacuum leak on the TJ 4.0 mimics half a dozen other problems \u2014 lean codes, idle surge, rough idle, hesitation. Before replacing O2 sensors or the IAC, find and fix the leak. It costs $5 in hose and 30 minutes.","description":"The 4.0L relies on intake vacuum for multiple systems: brake booster, the PCV system, the MAP sensor, the EGR system (where applicable), and various small ports on the intake manifold. Any unmetered air entering after the MAF sensor (or MAP sensor on the TJ's speed-density system) reads as a lean condition. The PCM compensates with long-term fuel trim adjustments, but a large enough leak defeats the compensation and sets codes.\n\nCommon codes from a vacuum leak: P0171 (system too lean, bank 1), P0300 (random misfire \u2014 large leak can cause this), P0507 (IAC RPM high \u2014 often a leak that artificially raises idle). If you see lean codes and the fuel system tests healthy, a vacuum leak is the first thing to rule out.\n\nOn a 25-year-old TJ, the most common failure points are: the rubber elbow at the PCV valve (cracks from age), the intake manifold gasket (particularly the center cylinders), the vacuum hose to the brake booster (large, often overlooked), and the small port hoses at the intake manifold and throttle body.\n\n- OBD-II scanner \u2014 check fuel trim values. LTFT above +10% at idle strongly suggests a vacuum leak\n- Carb cleaner spray (narrow nozzle) or unlit propane torch\n- Eyes and hands\n- Optional: smoke machine \u2014 the most reliable method, rents from many auto parts stores\n\n### Method 1 \u2014 Carb cleaner spray (common, no equipment required)\n\n1. Start the engine, let it reach operating temperature.\n2. Spray short bursts of carb cleaner at suspected leak points: vacuum hose connections, intake manifold gasket surface, throttle body boot, PCV hose.\n3. If idle RPM changes (usually increases) when you spray a spot, you've found the leak \u2014 the carb cleaner is being drawn in as extra fuel.\n4. Work slowly. Carb cleaner is flammable \u2014 stay away from exhaust components.\n\n### Method 2 \u2014 Smoke test (most reliable)\n\n1. A smoke machine pumps low-pressure white smoke into the intake system.\n2. Leaks show up as wisps of smoke escaping from crack or failed seam.\n3. Most independent shops will do a smoke test for $25\u2013$50 if you don't want to rent the equipment.\n\n### Method 3 \u2014 Visual and physical inspection\n\n1. Inspect all vacuum hoses at their connection points. Look for cracks, hard/brittle rubber, loose clamps.\n2. Squeeze each hose along its length \u2014 cracked hoses will flex and you'll feel the failure point.\n3. Check the large intake boot between the air filter housing and throttle body. Cracks here cause significant lean conditions.\n\nMost TJ vacuum leaks are hose failures \u2014 a $3 length of 3/8\" vacuum hose from the parts store fixes them. Cut to length, push on over the fitting.\n\nA failed intake manifold gasket requires more work: drain coolant, remove the intake, replace the gasket, reinstall. A Fel-Pro gasket set is $20 and the job takes 3\u20134 hours with basic tools.\n\n- Clear lean codes after repair and drive a full drive cycle before rechecking. Long-term fuel trim takes time to reset.\n- If lean codes return after fixing an obvious leak, there may be a second leak elsewhere, or the oxygen sensor itself is failing (O2 sensors go lazy around 100k miles on the 4.0).\n- Brake booster hose is large and carries high vacuum \u2014 a crack here causes a noticeable change in brake feel (pedal goes hard before the leak causes any idle or fuel trim symptoms).\n\nCarb cleaner: $6\u2013$9. Vacuum hose assortment: $12\u2013$18. Intake manifold gasket if needed: $18\u2013$25. Most TJ vacuum leaks are hose failures that cost under $20 to fix."},{"id":"tj-beadlock-wheels","title":"TJ Beadlock Wheels \u2014 What They Do and Whether You Need Them","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"reference","difficulty":3,"time_hours":{"low":2,"high":4},"cost_usd":{"low":600,"high":1400},"tools_required":["torque wrench","impact wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["wheel mounting","tire service"],"build_tiers":["rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["wheels","beadlock","tires","rock-crawler","airing-down"],"parts":[{"name":"Method Race Wheels 105 beadlock \u2014 17\" (set of 4)","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":1100},{"name":"Black Rhino Armory beadlock wheel (per wheel)","vendor":"Black Rhino","price_estimate_usd":200},{"name":"Raceline Monster beadlock wheel (per wheel)","vendor":"Raceline","price_estimate_usd":165},{"name":"Rugged Ridge Drakon beadlock wheel (per wheel)","vendor":"Rugged Ridge","price_estimate_usd":140}],"citations":[{"title":"Beadlock Wheels: What They Are and When You Need Them","url":"https://www.4wdanswerman.com/beadlock-wheels-explained/"},{"title":"Method 105 Beadlock Review","url":"https://www.trail4runner.com/articles/method-race-wheels-105-beadlock-review"}],"alternatives":[],"summary":"Beadlock wheels are the right answer for rock crawling at 5 PSI or below \u2014 they mechanically clamp the tire bead so it can't unseated. For trail driving at 15 PSI and above, they're overkill and add maintenance you don't need.","description":"A conventional wheel holds the tire bead in place through air pressure alone \u2014 the bead presses outward against the wheel's bead seat. Air the tire down to 10 PSI or below and that outward force decreases enough that a rock edge or hard sidewall flexion can push the bead inward and unseat it. Once unseated, the tire goes flat immediately and often comes off the wheel.\n\nBeadlock wheels add a mechanical clamp: the outer ring of the wheel bolts over the tire bead, physically clamping it between the ring and the wheel body. At 4 to 7 PSI \u2014 the pressure serious rock crawlers run for maximum sidewall contact patch \u2014 the bead cannot unseat regardless of sidewall flex or rock impacts.\n\nWho actually needs them: TJs being used for technical rock crawling where airing down to 6\u201310 PSI is regular practice. If your lowest trail pressure is 15 PSI, conventional wheels are fine. If you're running 33s or larger and regularly crawling ledges, beadlocks are worth the cost.\n\nThe hardware catch: DOT-approved beadlocks are available (Method, Raceline, and a few others), but most beadlock wheels are street-illegal because the outer ring can loosen. Consequences in most states: a fix-it ticket. Running DOT-approved beadlocks (Method 105, Raceline Monster-T) keeps you street-legal.\n\nBeadlock rings require re-torquing every 50 to 100 miles of hard use. The outer ring bolts loosen from wheel flex and vibration. Miss this maintenance and the ring loosens, the bead isn't clamped, and you've got a false sense of security.\n\n- Beadlock wheels (sized to your tire \u2014 most TJ builds run 15x8 or 16x8 with a 4.5\" bolt circle, but check your specific axles)\n- Torque wrench for ring bolts (spec varies by wheel, typically 8\u201312 ft-lb for ring bolts)\n- Floor jack, jack stands for mounting\n\nMethod Race Wheels 105: best combination of DOT approval, quality, and price. $250\u2013$280 per wheel, well-reviewed for trail durability. Raceline Monster: comparable quality at slightly lower price. Budget options (Rugged Ridge Drakon): fine for occasional use, ring hardware quality is less consistent \u2014 retorque more frequently. Black Rhino Armory: mid-tier, popular with JK/TJ rock crawlers.\n\nBeadlock wheels are mounted at a tire shop (they need a machine to seat the tire into the wheel). The outer ring is then torqued over the bead. Verify ring bolt torque after the first trail run.\n\n- Street legality: check your state. Non-DOT beadlocks on a street-registered vehicle are a citable offense in many states.\n- Weight: beadlocks add 4\u20138 lb per corner compared to standard steel wheels. Noticeable on-road, irrelevant off-road.\n- Mud packing: the recessed bolt circle of the outer ring packs with mud. Carry a pick tool to clear before inspecting ring bolt torque.\n\nBudget beadlocks (Rugged Ridge, Fuel Off-Road): $130\u2013$160/wheel. Mid-tier (Raceline, Black Rhino): $165\u2013$210/wheel. Quality tier (Method 105, Raceline Monster-T): $240\u2013$280/wheel. A set of 4: $520\u2013$1,120 depending on tier, not including mounting fees. If beadlocks are in the budget, don't compromise on ring hardware quality \u2014 that's what holds the tire on."},{"id":"tj-tire-fitment-35s","title":"Running 35\" Tires on the TJ \u2014 Lift and Fitment Requirements","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"upgrade_path","difficulty":2,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":2},"cost_usd":{"low":1000,"high":1600},"tools_required":["torque wrench","floor jack","jack stands","lug wrench"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching","wheel and tire mounting"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["tires","fitment","35-inch","lift-requirement","clearance"],"parts":[{"name":"BFGoodrich Mud-Terrain T/A KM3 35x12.50R15 LT (E-rated)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":280},{"name":"BFGoodrich All-Terrain T/A KO2 35x12.50R15 LT (C-rated)","vendor":"BFGoodrich","price_estimate_usd":270},{"name":"Nitto Trail Grappler M/T 35x12.50R15 (E-rated)","vendor":"Nitto Tire","price_estimate_usd":265},{"name":"Cooper STT Pro 35x12.50R15 LT (E-rated)","vendor":"Cooper Tires","price_estimate_usd":245}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 35s on a TJ: Lift Requirements and Trimming Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/35-tires-tj-wrangler-fitment/"},{"title":"BFGoodrich KM3 Product Page","url":"https://www.bfgoodrich.com/tires/mud-terrain-ta-km3"},{"title":"4WD Hardware \u2014 TJ Tire Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.4wdh.com/TJ-Tire-Fitment"}],"alternatives":["tj-budget-lift","tj-mid-lift","tj-wheels","tj-regear"],"summary":"35\" tires fit under a TJ at 2.5\" of lift with minor trimming, and clear at 4\" without touching the fender \u2014 but re-gearing is non-negotiable if you want them to drive right.","description":"The TJ was designed for tires in the 29\"\u201331\" range. Moving to 35s changes every aspect of how the rig drives: it raises the effective gear ratio (making the engine work harder), stresses the front Dana 30 and rear axles harder on rocks, and adds rotating mass that slows throttle response and hurts fuel economy. None of that is a reason not to run 35s \u2014 it's just the honest picture before you spend money on tires.\n\nFitment at various lift heights: at 2.5\" of lift, 35x12.50R15 tires will clear the fender at full droop but will contact the inner fender liner and sometimes the LCA skid at full flex. Light trimming of the inner fender liner is required for daily driving clearance. At 3.5\"\u20134\" of lift, 35s clear cleanly without trimming under most conditions \u2014 some builds still trim the factory corner flares to eliminate any potential contact at full stuff. At 2\" of lift (budget lift), 35s will rub under hard compression turns; the setup is manageable on-road with careful driving but not appropriate for trail use at full flex.\n\nThe stock 15\" wheel bolt pattern (5x114.3 / 5x4.5\") makes tire selection genuinely convenient \u2014 15\" LT tires in 35x12.50 are among the most commonly produced trail sizes, and most major brands offer multiple compounds in this fitment. Stick with a 3.75\"\u20134\" backspacing wheel to avoid contact with the control arms. A wheel with less backspacing (more offset outward) will improve clearance but stick out past the fender flares, which is a tire coverage issue in some states.\n\nOn re-gearing: the TJ stock 3.73 ratio with 35\" tires is noticeably sluggish at highway speed and has poor low-end torque for rock crawling. The accepted answer is 4.56 gears for 35s or 4.88 for 35s on a dedicated trail build. Budget for re-gearing when you buy the tires, or you'll be doing it anyway six months later.","steps":["Verify current lift height \u2014 35s require at least 2.5\" of lift.","Inspect existing wheels for backspacing measurement. 3.75\"\u20134\" backspacing required for 35s without modification.","Mount and balance new tires on wheels at a tire shop.","Install wheels; torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern.","Test-drive at slow speeds, turning lock-to-lock, and check all four corners for contact.","Identify any trim points on the inner fender liner; mark with chalk and trim with a utility knife or rotary tool as needed.","Re-torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs after 50 miles.","Schedule a speedometer recalibration or install an aftermarket speedometer corrector (SpeedoHealer or similar) \u2014 stock speedometer will read 4\"\u20136% slow with 35s vs. stock 30\" tires."],"pros":["35\" is the most common trail tire size for TJs \u2014 fitment guidance and tire selection are well-documented","15\" wheel fitment is stock \u2014 no adapter or unusual wheel sizing required","BFG KM3 and KO2 both have strong trail track records and broad availability","Meaningful ground clearance improvement over 31\" or 33\" tires, particularly for approach angle"],"cons":["Re-gearing required for acceptable road manners and trail performance \u2014 add $800\u2013$1,800 to total cost","35s stress the Dana 35 rear axle significantly; consider the 8.25\" upgrade or at minimum add a diff skid before heavy trail use","Fuel economy drops 2\u20134 mpg compared to stock tires","Rotating mass increase affects acceleration noticeably on the 2.5L and 4.0L engines","Without trimming at 2.5\" lift, full-flex contact is possible \u2014 always test before committing to a trail"]},{"id":"tj-wheels","title":"Wheel Selection for the TJ \u2014 Steel, Alloy, and Beadlock","vehicle":"tj","category":"wheels_tires","type":"part","difficulty":1,"time_hours":{"low":1,"high":1},"cost_usd":{"low":300,"high":1000},"tools_required":["torque wrench","lug wrench","floor jack","jack stands"],"skills_required":["basic wrenching"],"build_tiers":["mild_trail","moderate_trail","rock_crawler"],"year_compatibility":["1997-2006"],"tags":["wheels","rims","backspacing","beadlock","bolt-pattern"],"parts":[{"name":"Pro Comp Series 51 Steel Wheel 15x8\" 3.75\" BS (5x4.5\" bolt pattern)","vendor":"Pro Comp","price_estimate_usd":75},{"name":"Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock 15x8\" 3.5\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"Method Race Wheels","price_estimate_usd":145},{"name":"Raceline Monster 8 15x8\" 3.75\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"Raceline Wheels","price_estimate_usd":110},{"name":"KMC XD Rockstar 2 15x8\" 3.625\" BS (5x4.5\")","vendor":"KMC Wheels","price_estimate_usd":135}],"citations":[{"title":"JeepForum \u2014 TJ Wheel Backspacing and Fitment Guide","url":"https://www.jeepforum.com/forum/f9/tj-wheel-backspacing-fitment-guide/"},{"title":"Pro Comp 51 Series Steel Wheel Specs","url":"https://procompusa.com/wheels/series-51"},{"title":"Method Race Wheels 105 Beadlock Overview","url":"https://www.methodracewheels.com/105-beadlock"}],"alternatives":["tj-tire-fitment-35s","tj-budget-lift"],"summary":"The TJ's 5x4.5\" bolt pattern and 15\" stock sizing are among the most wheel-friendly specs on any 4x4 \u2014 a huge selection of both cheap steel and quality alloy fitments are available in this pattern.","description":"The TJ uses a 5x114.3mm (commonly called 5x4.5\") bolt pattern and a 2.36\" center bore. Stock wheel diameter is 15\" across all trims. This combination is fortuitous for trail builders: 15\" wheels in 5x4.5\" are the most common light-truck wheel size produced, and every major off-road brand offers multiple options in this fitment. If you're planning to run 33\" or 35\" tires, 15\" diameter is exactly what you want \u2014 LT-rated 15\" tires in trail sizes are abundant and priced competitively.\n\nBackspacing is the critical dimension for fitment. The TJ's control arms and front axle housings create a specific clearance envelope. The acceptable range for most builds is 3.75\"\u20134.25\" backspacing. At 3.75\" backspacing, wheels sit outboard and may protrude past the stock fenders \u2014 check local vehicle codes for tire coverage requirements. At 4.5\" backspacing, wheels sit inboard enough that they're more likely to contact control arms or the inner fender at full flex after a lift. The 3.75\"\u20134\" zone is the working sweet spot for lifted TJs running 33\" or 35\" tires.\n\nPro Comp's Series 51 steel wheel ($75 each) is the trail standard budget choice \u2014 heavy, no-frills, consistently available, and durable enough to take rock strikes that would crack alloy. For a rig that's on the rocks regularly, steel makes practical sense. Method's 105 beadlock ($145 each) is a step up in every dimension: lighter than steel, true dual-ring beadlock design that keeps the tire bead seated at aggressive airing-down pressures, and built to a standard that racers trust. If you're running 10\u201312 PSI in the rocks, a beadlock is the right answer. Raceline Monster 8 and KMC XD Rockstar 2 are solid alloy options in the middle of the price range for builds that need a clean alloy look without the beadlock premium.","steps":["Verify wheel backspacing and center bore match TJ spec (5x4.5\", 2.36\" center bore, 3.75\"\u20134.25\" backspacing).","Mount and balance tires on new wheels at a tire shop.","Raise the vehicle at the frame rails and support on jack stands.","Install new wheel/tire assemblies; thread lug nuts by hand.","Torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs in a star pattern.","Drive 50 miles and re-torque lug nuts to 95 ft-lbs.","Verify tire clearance to inner fender, sway bar, and brake lines at full lock \u2014 turn the steering wheel lock to lock and inspect all four corners."],"pros":["5x4.5\" bolt pattern has the widest wheel selection of any common 4x4 \u2014 abundant options at every price point","15\" wheels provide more tire sidewall than 16\" or 17\" options, improving trail cushioning and bead seating at low pressures","Pro Comp steel wheels are the budget-bulletproof choice for builds that prioritize durability over weight","Method 105 beadlocks allow aggressive airing down (8\u201312 PSI) for maximum traction on rock terrain","Alloy wheels in the 15x8\" format are readily balanced and widely available at tire shops"],"cons":["Wheels with less than 3.75\" backspacing will protrude past fender flares \u2014 check state laws for tire coverage requirements","Method beadlocks require periodic re-torquing of the beadlock ring bolts (approximately every 3,000 miles) \u2014 skip this and the ring loosens","Steel wheels add 8\u201312 lbs per corner vs. quality alloy \u2014 meaningful on a rig where unsprung weight matters for articulation","Non-beadlock wheels at very low air pressures (below 15 PSI) risk tire debeading \u2014 stay above 15 PSI with standard wheels","Wheel spacers may be needed for additional clearance on some builds \u2014 prefer correct backspacing wheel over spacers when possible"]}];